N2  - Objectives: 1.To study the effect of celecoxib add-on therapy compared to escitalopram dose escalation in patients of depression partially responding to escitalopram. 2. To study the effect of celecoxib add-on therapy on serum level of IL-1 b compared to escitalopram alone in patients of depression partially responding to escitalopram. Materials and Methods: A total of 143 adult outpatients who met the WHO ICD-10 criteria of depression enrolled in the study initially after satisfying inclusion, exclusion criteria. Patients having Montogmery-Asberg depression rating scale (MADRS) scores above 20 were prescribed escitalopram 10 mg o.d. for 4 weeks. After four weeks all patients were evaluated for clinical improvement by MADRS. Forty patients responding partially (25-50% reduction in MADRS from day 0) to escitalopram 10 mg o.d. were allocated in a random fashion in equal number (n = 20) to group A: escitalopram 20 mg/day and group B: escitalopram 20 mg/day plus celecoxib 400 mg/day (200 mg bid) for another four weeks. Serum IL-1 b levels were done at baseline (at the time of randomization) and at completion of therapy. Results: Although both treatment modalities significantly decreased the MADRS scores over the study period, the combination of escitalopram and celecoxib showed a significant superiority over escitalopram dose escalation in the treatment of symptoms of depression. Additionally celecoxib plus escitalopram therapy caused significantly more lowering of serum IL-1 b levels as compared to escitalopram dose escalation alone. Conclusion: The results of this study suggest that celecoxib may be an effective augmentation agent in the management of patients of depression partially responding to escitalopram 10 mg and anti-inflammatory pharmacotherapy should be further explored in these patients.
