N2  - Background: Lipid-lowering using HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors or statin therapy forms the cornerstone of medical therapy in the primary and secondary prevention of cardiovascular disease. The present study was designed to investigate the cardioprotective role of Atrovastain in an in vivo model of myocardial ischemic-reperfusion injury in terms of induction of endogenous anti-oxidants and heat shock protein 72 associated BAG-1 Mediated Cardioprotection, Haemodynamic effects, Histological studies and the Up regulation of ecNOS Mediated Cardioprotection Methods: Hsp72 associated BAG-1, ecNOS expression and basal levels of endogenous antioxidants [EA] (superoxide dismutase, catalase, reduced glutathione) and thiobarbituric acid reactive substances (TBARS) were measured in the myocardium of three groups of rabbits [Control: C, Atrovastain 10mg/kg:(A1) and Atrovastain 20mg/kg:(A2)]. Hsp72, BAG-1, ecNOS were determined by Western Blot analysis in the hearts from normal rabbits (negative control), heat-stressed rabbits (positive control) and Atrovastain treated rabbits. In open-chest ketamine/pentobarbitone anaesthetized rabbits, the left anterior descending coronary arterywas occluded for 15 min of ischemia followed by 60 min of reperfusion. Vehicle (saline) or Atrovastain was administered 10 min before reperfusion (post-treatment) Results: In the vehicle-treated group, ischemic-reperfusion injury (IRI) was evidenced by depression of global haemodynamic function (mean arterial pressure, left ventricular-end-diastolic-pressure, peak positive and negative dP/dt) along with depletion of myocardial high-energy phosphate (HEP) compounds and raised levels of myocardial TBARS and depletion of endogenous myocardial antioxidants (reduced glutathione, Superoxide dismutase and catalase). In the post-treatment group, in both the doses the recovery of myocardial haemodynamic function, with significant reduction in TBARS, was observed. In the A1 and A2 treatment groups has shown elevated levels of HSP72 associated with expression of cellular stress sequester BAG-1 and ecNOS. Conclusion: The results of the present study suggest that posttreatment of the 20mg/kg Atrovastain treatment group offered better cardio protection when subjected to ischemic reperfusion injury.
