N2  - Rates of postoperative sternal wound infections (SWI) have been steadily increasing despite advances in operative techniques, increased awareness and treatment of known resistant organisms, and the identification of high risk surgical candidates (1,2,4). SWIs following cardiac surgery have been associated with increased length of hospital stay, increased risk of further complications and increased mortality (1-3). Screening for the presence of resistant organisms prior to cardiac surgery in order to dose prophylactic antibiotics has become common practice at our center, however, the screen result is at times unavailable prior to surgery. This can result in patients receiving ineffective prophylactic antibiotics as per current guidelines (5). This article discusses the sternal wound care of a patient colonized with methicillin resistant staphylococcus aureas (MRSA), who received a non-specific cephalosporin at the time of his operation and subsequently developed a SWI colonized by MRSA. The lack of advanced wound care education within acute care will be discussed, as well as advocacy for best practice in regards to patient care. This patient suffered with his delay in discharge through requirement of painful complex dressings, but he also suffered through anxiety related to his isolation status, depression throughout his course of treatment, and a reported feeling of mistrust in the healthcare team. The need for proper screening practices as well as the need for advanced wound care education within acute care will be stressed, in order to raise awareness of this issue, and advocate for best practice within cardiovascular nursing.
