N2  - Along with the financial and economic crisis affecting the world since 2008, another silent crisis is affecting the world of therapeutic innovation in psychiatry. However, mental, neurological and substance-use (MNS) disorders still constitute 13% of the global disease burden, surpassing both cardiovascular disease and cancer. It is then urgent to re-examine new potential therapeutic pathways for drug development or repositioning. One of the most promising research fields is the inflammatory component of major psychiatric diseases (unipolar depressive disorder UDD, bipolar disorders and schizophrenia) [1]. We decided then to carry out a systematic comprehensive review of psychiatric effects of add-on anti-inflammatory drugs in these disorders. We followed the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analyses (PRISMA) statement. Medline (1966- 2013) and Web of Science (1975-2013) databases were explored, without any year or language restrictions. The baseline search paradigm was limited to open-labeled clinical and randomized controlled trials with the following terms: (depression, bipolar disorders, schizophrenia) and respectively anti-inflammatory drugs, NSAID, ibuprophen, duclofenac, naproxen sodium, acetylsalicylic acid, anti-Cox-2, celecoxib, anti TNF-alpha, etanercept, infliximab, adalimumab, minocycline). Based on all the data exposed in this review, we suggest to discriminate at baseline patients with high hs-CRP (>5 mg) in cases of early episodes of mood or psychotic disorders (<2 years): these patients may be specially responsive to anti-inflammatory drugs. The choice of this initial discrimination criteria seems relevant as a 'personalized medicine' and remains to be determined in controlled studies, we chose it as it is easy to carry out in routine practice and we found no other routine inflammatory marker in the literature. Next, these patients may receive low doses salicylate (<200 mg/d), celecoxib (200 mg ×2/d) or minocycline (300 mg/d), with no strict criteria for the choice of the molecule. Salicylate may be specially useful in cardio-vascular or cancer risk comorbidities, and celecoxib in depression and early schizophrenia. Minocycline seems particularly interesting given the numerous additional potential activities of this molecule (anti-microbial, anti-oxydative, neuro-protective), however, as a hypersensitivity syndrome has been described, it could be only a second-line treatment. Infliximab seems inadapted for routine first-line treatment as it should be intra-veinously administered. A number of repurposed anti-inflammatory drugs are available as adjunct treatment of treatment-resistant patients with UDD, schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. If used with attention to their possible side effects, they may be reasonable therapeutic alternatives when clinicians are out of ideas, it remains to be confirmed by randomized controlled trials. This was started recently by the team of Savitz et al. who compare in a prospective study minocycline and salicylate add-on therapies in a 2×2 trial in bipolar depression we eagerly await the results of this study [2]. We hope that this article will boost gain interest for the use of anti-inflammatory drugs in the treatment of mental illnesses and useful considerations for a near-future guidelines' update.
