N2  - Major depressive disorder (MDD) has a prevalence of 15% in the population, but its pathogenesis is not completely understood. Several studies have focused on monoaminergic systems, but this hypothesis does not appear to be sufficient to fully explain the mechanisms of MDD. Beyond the current hypothesis, several new biological substrates have been proposed for this disorder. One possible explanation that was recently proposed for these alterations is based on the inflammatory and neurodegenerative hypothesis of depression. The present study investigated whether the antiinflammatory drugs celecoxib and piroxicam have antidepressant activity in animal models of depression. Male Wistar rats, weighing 280-320 g, were used. In the acute experiment: the rats were randomly distributed into four groups: 10 mg/kg celecoxib, 10 mg/kg piroxicam, 0.9% saline, and 20 mg/kg imipramine. Three administrations of the drugs were performed, 24, 8, and 1 h before testing the animals in the open field, followed by a modified forced swim test. In the prolonged experiment the rats were randomly distributed into eight groups: stressed + 0.9% saline, stressed + 20 mg/kg imipramine, stressed + 10 mg/kg celecoxib, stressed + 10 mg/kg piroxicam, non-stressed + 0.9% saline, non-stressed + 20 mg/kg imipramine, non-stressed + 10 mg/kg celecoxib, and non-stressed + 10 mg/kg piroxicam. The stressed groups were subjected to chronic mild stress (CMS) throughout the experiment, and the non-stressed groups remained undisturbed. After 3 week (21 day) exposure to CMS, drug treatment began and continued for 21 days. The sucrose preference test was performed on day 0, 7, 14, 21, 22, 28, 35, and 42 of the experiment. The oxidative stress was evaluated in these animals. After acute administration, we observed antidepressant-like effects of celecoxib and piroxicam in the modified forced swim test in rats. Piroxicam increased serotonin and norepinephrine levels in the hippocampus. Prolonged (21 day) treatment with celecoxib and piroxicam rescued sucrose preference in a chronic mild stress model of depression. Additionally, the chronic mild stress-induced reduction of hippocampal glutathione was prevented by treatment with celecoxib and piroxicam. Superoxide dismutase in the hippocampus was increased after CMS compared with the non-stressed saline group. The non-stressed celecoxib and piroxicam groups and stressed piroxicam group exhibited an increase in hippocampal superoxide dismutase activity compared with the stressed saline group. Lipid hydroperoxide was increased in the stressed group treated with vehicle and non-stressed group treated with imipramine but not in the stressed groups treated with celecoxib and piroxicam. Altogether, our results showed that the antiinflammatory drugs piroxicam and celecoxib have antidepressant-like effects in both the FST and CMS model. The effects of piroxicam may be attributable to an increase in hippocampal monoamine levels. The present findings also suggest that the antidepressant-like effects of celecoxib and piroxicam might be caused by enhanced antioxidant defenses and attenuated oxidative stress in the hippocampus. More studies are needed to further elucidate the mechanisms of the antidepressant-like effects of celecoxib and piroxicam.
