N2  - Purpose: Depression is a chronic and recurring mental disorder associated with functional disability and increased risk of suicide. Bacterial endotoxin (lipopolysaccharide; LPS) evokes in rodents an adaptive short-term response named sickness behavior. Currently the time-point of 24h after LPS administration has been used to model depression in rodents [1]. This is based on the changes induced by this endotoxin, in stress hormones secretion and activity of brain serotonergic and noradrenergic systems that have been implicated in stress responses, fear and anxiety. Doxycycline (DOXY) is a member of the tetracycline antibiotics group. Doxycycline (6-Deoxy-5-hydroxytetracycline), another tetracycline antibiotic compound, is a broad-spectrum antibiotic widely used for infections caused by both gram-negative and gram-positive microorganisms. Doxycycline is the most frequently used tetracycline in medicine and is included in the List of Essential Medicines of the World Health Organization [3]. DOXI showed neuroprotective effects both in vitro and in vivo and this appeared to derive from its anti-apoptotic and anti-inflammatory mechanisms. Based on the recent evidences of deficits in the levels of prepulse inhibition (PPI) seem in depressed patients, the present study aimed to investigate the potential effects of DOXY against PPI deficits induced by the LPS model of depression in mice. Methods: Male Swiss mice (20-30g) were randomly divided into nine experimental groups of 8 animals each. For the prevention treatment paradigm, DOXY (25 or 50 mg/kg), imipramine (IMI 10 mg/kg) or saline (endotoxin-free) were administered i.p. to the animals 30 min prior to LPS (0.5 mg/kg, i.p.) administration. For the reversal treatment protocol, separate animals received LPS (0.5 mg/kg, i.p.) followed by treatment with DOXY (25 or 50 mg/kg, i.p.), IMI (10 mg/kg, i.p) or saline either 1.5 or 23.5 h after LPS administration. Each animal was placed in the startle chamber and submitted to 3 prepulse intensities followed by a pulse of 120 dB startle after 80 ms. The percentage PPI was calculated as previously reported [2]. For statistical analysis two-way ANOVA was used with Bonferroni test as post hoc, considering as significant p<0.05. Results: 24 h after LPS administration the animals presented a deficit in PPI levels of around 81% as compared to control animals in all prepulse intensites evaluated. The pre-administration of 25 mg/kg DOXY completely prevented the deficit in PPI levels induced by LPS in the prepulse intensities of 75 and 80 dB. This effect was also observed in IMI pre-treated animals, but only in the prepulse intensity of 80 dB. In the reversal protocol the post-administration of both DOXY 25 and 50 mg/kg normalized the decrease in PPI induced by LPS, although IMI presented no effect. Conclusion: PPI is an important parameter to be evaluated in the model of depression induced by LPS and DOXY is able to prevent and revert this parameter.
