N2  - Aim: Depression is a serious and recurrent disease that affects a large number of people around the world. It's known that inhibition of nitric oxide (NO) exerts antidepressant-like effect in animal models and some antidepressants used in the clinic also have this effect. Myricitrin, a flavonoid extracted from various plants (eg. Eugenia uniflora), gives two possible activities particularly useful for the treatment of bipolar disorder and is therefore subject of this study, namely, antimanic activity by inhibiting PKCa and PKCe and antidepressant activity based on inhibition of means of nitric acid (NO), and showed antinociceptive, anxiolytic-like and antipsychotic-like action [1,2]. The aim of this study was to evaluate the antidepressant-like activity of myricitrin. Methods: Mice were submitted to chronic unpredictable mild stress. The mice (Swiss, male, 25-35g) were divided into two groups (stress and control), the stress group was submitted weekly to a battery of pseudorandom stress procedures (food or water deprivation, exposure to light or dark constant, inclined cage and wet bedding) for 5 weeks. Sucrose preference test (1%) (SPT) was carried out after each week of stress. After the induction of anhedonia (reduction of sucrose preference) at 5th week, mice were submitted to SPT, open field test (OFT) and immobility in forced swimming test (FST). These data were considered the baseline data. The mice are treated with imipramine (IMIP) (20 mg/kg, ip), myricitrin (MYR) (10 mg/kg, ip) or saline (SAL) and submitted to SPT, OF and FST after 7 and 14 days of treatment. Results: As expected, imipramine reversed the depressive-like behaviors (anhedonia and high immobility in FST) induced by unpredictable stress. Myricitrin also reversed these behaviors. In the SPT, both drugs were effective, reversing stress-induced anhedonia. In FST, both drugs were effective in decreasing the high immobility time. No treatment was significantly difference from control group in OFT, which shows that drugs does not affect locomotor in mice. In all tests (except OFT) p <0.002. (Table Presented) Conclusion: Both drugs were effective in reversing the depressive-like behavior on the parameters analyzed. The results obtained with imipramine validated the procedures. These results support the hypothesis that myricitrin presents a potential antidepressant-like effect at doses that did not change locomotor activity. This makes it a potential target for the search for new antidepressant drugs.
