N2  - The stimulus for the development of antidepressants has been mainly based on the monoamine hypothesis of depression. Despite the plethora of antidepressants of different classes and potencies for monoamine transporters and receptors, there is little evidence that any are as effective as ECT, or that the time of onset of therapeutic action is quicker than first-generation TCAs. Recently, a major change has taken place in antidepressant research with the discovery that ketamine (an NMDA receptor antagonist/AMPA glutamate receptor agonist) has rapid and sustained activity in some antidepressant-resistant patients, although psychotomimetic and dissociative side effects may limit widespread clinical use [1]. This has stimulated interest in the role of the glutamate system in depression and provides a potential link between chronic low-grade inflammation, hypercortisolaemia and neurodegenerative changes associated with major depression [2]. The glutamate-inflammation hypothesis is supported by the following evidence: 1. An increase in plasma pro-inflammatory cytokines in depression augments NMDA-glutamate function by several mechanisms, including decreased neuronal re-uptake of glutamate and enhancing synthesis of the glutamate agonist quinolinic acid, the end product of the cytokine-activated tryptophankynurenine pathway [3]. 2. The observation that NMDA receptor antagonists have antidepressant-like effects in rodent models of depression. 3. Research showing that AMPA receptor agonists and mTOR receptor antagonists have antidepressant-like activity. Ketamine is thought to act in this way. 4. Anti-inflammatory drugs enhance antidepressant activity in depressed patients and in animal models of depression. This presentation will assess the importance of the glutamateinflammation system as a target for novel antidepressants.
