N2  - Background and objective: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a chronic autoimmune disease which can not be cured and that is responsible for loss of function, pain, stress, fatigue, depression. Disease modifying anti-rheumatic drugs (DMARDs) limit the progression of the disease but patients have to learn how to live with it. To help them to cope with RA in daily life, a multidisciplinary patient education program has been established in our University Hospital and deals with many themes. Setting and method: As pharmacists, we were asked for presenting treatments and giving tools about medicines. Using a terminology that patients can understand, we designed a pre-filled paper about drugs. Results: Patients want to gain new knowledge, share their experience, learn how to use drugs prescribed and accept the illness; they are better satisfied when their emotional well-being is considered, as described in literature. A 1-day education program in group is suggested to willing adult patients in our hospital, including drugs management. On the paper we elaborated, treatments are classified into two categories: DMARDs and symptomatic treatments. The position of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and corticosteroids in the therapeutic strategy is broached. Some additional recommendations are also simply written about, for example, administration procedures, the use of proton-pump inhibitors with anti-inflammatory drugs and laxatives with morphine. We insist on which drugs patients are allowed to take in self-medication, which associations are workable (especially to struggle against pain) and which drugs are only under prescriptions and are not in option. Issues about DMARDs are also explained (folic acid supplementation after methotrexate, anti-TNF alpha injections delayed in case of infection). Patients individually fill the pre-filled paper with the name of the drugs they used to take. Conclusions: Patients' treatments are summarized on this paper they bring back home. Thanks to that, we tempt to increase patients' empowerment and their quality of life as they should be able to selfmanage pain treatments. An evaluation of the impact of this pre-filled paper will have to be done retrospectively.
