N2  - The 'Depression Executive Dysfunction Syndrome' afflicts a considerable number of depressed elderly patients and may be resistant to conventional pharmacotherapy. We studied the Choline Alfoscerate or the ethyl ester of eicosapentaenoic acid (EEPA) as an adjunct to antidepressant treatment for episodes of depressive disorder occurring in older depressed patients (over 60 years old) with or without executive dysfunction. The design was a 12-week, parallel-group, double-blind addition of Choline Alfoscerate or EEPA to ongoing antidepressant therapy. Patients continued their current antidepressant treatment (only one SSRI) at the same dose they were receiving when they entered the study. The EEPA was given in 1-g doses twice a day for a total of 2 g/day. The Choline Alfoscerate was given in 400mg doses twice a day for a total of 800mg/day. Executive functions were assessed with Controlled Oral Word Association Test and the Korean Stroop Color-Word Test and Trail Making Test part B. Depressive symptoms were measured by Korean Geriatric depression scale (K-GDS), Quick Inventory of Depressive Symptomology-Self Report (QIDSSR). 345 patients participated. A total of 285 patients completed the study. At endpoint (12 weeks), the mean change from baseline in QIDS-SR total score was -22.2 for patients treated with Choline Alfoscerate (n = 162) and -21.5 for patients with E-EPA (n = 123), resulting in no difference. But, both abnormal controlled oral word association test and abnormal Stroop Color-Word scores were associated with a favorable response of geriatric depression to additional treatment. Conclusions: Improvement of frontal lobe dysfunction was found after supplementation of Choline Alfoscerate in geriatric depressive patients with executive dysfunction.
