N2  - Introduction: Pain is being recognized as an increasingly common and important symptom in individuals with CF, though little is known about the association between pain and clinical outcomes in the pediatric CF population. Objective: To describe the frequency and location of pain in a pediatric CF population and to assess the impact of pain on quality of life (QOL) and pulmonary exacerbations. Methods: A prospective cohort study at a single pediatric CF clinic. We enrolled consecutive patients between age 12 and 20 between October 2011 and August 2012. Patients completed a baseline questionnaire and a followup questionnaire 6 months later. The surveys included the Revised Children's Anxiety and Depression Survey (RCADS) and the Cystic Fibrosis Questionnaire Revised (CFQ-R). The electronic medical record was reviewed for the 6 months following the baseline survey to determine episodes of treatment for acute pulmonary exacerbations. Associations between pain and other variables were assessed using chi-squared tests, linear and logistic regression. Results: A total of 73 patients completed baseline questionnaires (67% of eligible population). The mean age was 15.6 years, 56% were female and 86% were white. The mean FEV1 was 2.38±0.95 L or 79.3±26.0% predicted. The questionnaires 89% of patients reported some pain in the 3 months prior to the study and it lasted a few hours or less in 80.6% of patients. Head, abdominal and chest pain were most common; 43% described pain as mild, 50% as moderate, and 7% as severe. Presence of pain in the last 7 days was significantly associated with concurrent symptoms of major depression and separation anxiety. Baseline pain was significantly associated with lower future QOL on the CFQ-R. Specifically, a one quartile increase in pain resulted in a 5.56 point lower score on the Physical domain (p=0.01) and a 5.31 point lower score on the Emotion domain (0.016). Treatment with IV antibiotics for a CF exacerbation over the 6 months following the baseline survey was more likely in patients with higher ratings of pain, OR=1.3 (1.06-1.67; p=0.01) for each 1-point increase in pain on a 10-point scale. Conclusion: Mild to moderate pain is common in adolescents and young adults with CF. It is associated with symptoms of depression and results in lower QOL and increased risk for pulmonary exacerbations. More attention is needed to understand the relationships between depression, pain, and outcomes with the goal of optimizing treatment of this challenging symptom.
