N2  - Case Description: A 66-year-old woman with past medical history of DM, peripheral vascular disease (PVD), chronic kidney disease, presented to rehabilitation after a R transmetatarsal amputation. Her outside hospital course was complicated Methicillin- resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) bacteremia related to her R gangrenous foot. Patient was prescribed 6 week treatment of antibiotics. Patient also had persistent nausea and vomiting which was attributed to gastroparesis and antibiotic intolerance. Course also notable for hallucinations and depression requiring treatment. Setting: Acute Inpatient Rehabilitation Hospital Results or Clinical Course: On admission to acute rehabilitation the patient's TMA had healed well. Rehabilitation course was then complicated by worsening nausea, vomiting and right upper quadrant abdominal pain radiating from R flank. C-reactive protein (CRP) had decreased from 37 to 7. Palpation and percussion of the cervical and thoracic spine produced no tenderness. A renal ultrasound was performed to evaluate the pain showed medical renal disease and no other acute pathology. Liver function tests were in normal range. The pain, nausea, and vomiting worsened with a leukocytosis and low grade fever. Imaging demonstrated T11-12 discitis and osteomyelitis which required abscess drainage and evacuation. A bone biopsy was consistent with MRSA. Discussion: This is a case of MRSA thoracic discitis and osteomyelitis after a R TMA presenting as abdominal pain that was complicated by depression, nausea and vomiting. Due to the medical complexity of the patient it initially felt that these symptoms were related to gastroparesis and renal disease. Infectious spinal pathology was further down the differential diagnosis especially given the decrease in CRP with antibiotic treatment. The development of leukocytosis and low grade fever led to imaging and diagnosis of discitis with eventual progression to abscess formation. The patient's strength and abdominal pain improved after surgical management. Conclusions: Infection of the thoracic spine should be considered in the differential diagnosis of abdominal pain in this patient population.
