N2  - Case Description: Limbic encephalitis (LE) is a rare disorder characterized by personality changes, irritability, depression, seizures, dyskinesias, catatonia, memory loss and sometimes dementia. It results from an autoimmune response against neuronal antigens, leading to inflammatory damage of brain cortex. We present a 29-year-old with history of ovarian teratoma and polysubstance abuse who developed a sub-acute progressive left sided weakness, facial droop with cognitive decline and psychiatric symptoms diagnosed with LE. Setting: Inpatient Rehabilitation. Results or Clinical Course: MRI demonstrated diffuse flair abnormalities involving the limbic system, the right parietal cortex and cerebellum. Laboratory studies were positive for alpha-amino- 3-hydroxy-5-methyl-4-isoxazolepropionic acid (AMPA) receptor antibody encephalitis. She completed immunomodulatory treatment including intravenous solumedrol, plasma exchange and Rituximab infusion. She also underwent ovarian tumor removal. Her hospital course was complicated by fever, tachycardia, hypertension, agitation, encephalopathy, respiratory failure, dysphagia and deep venous thrombosis. Discussion: Patients with LE develop neurological and psychiatric symptoms due to autoimmune damage to limbic and extralimbic brain areas. While some patients develop LE as a paraneoplastic manifestation of systemic cancer, recent studies suggests that LE is also associated with antibodies that are directed against cell surface antigens with a much weaker association to the neoplasm. Conclusions: We describe a case of LE in a patient with positive AMPA receptor and ovarian teratoma who underwent intensive inpatient rehabilitation including management with pharmacologic agents used for mood stabilization, insomnia and dysautonomia. AMPA receptor is functionally related to N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor and both play critical roles in synaptic plasticity. To our knowledge, this is the first case of AMPA receptor positive limbic encephalitis with features typical of anti-NMDA receptor LE including ovarian teratoma, encephalopathy and autonomic instability.
