N2  - Case Description: A 53-year-old woman with past medical history of anxiety and depression presented with one day history of nontraumatic, unremitting back pain, progressively worsening bilateral lower extremity weakness and fever to 102.8 degrees Fahrenheit. The patient also had bowel and bladder incontinence. Magnestic resonance imaging of the entire spine revealed enlargement and signal abnormality in the cervical and upper thoracic spinal cord at many levels (C1 to T8) consistent with longitudinal extensive transverse myelitis (LETM), with the patient's spinal cord exam revealing that she was a T1 AIS (ASIA impairment scale) B incomplete paraplegic. Further work up showed that the patient was positive for rheumatoid factor, antinuclear antibody (ANA), SSA, SSB, all consistent with suspected Sjogren's syndrome. However, other tests including Lyme, HIV (human immunodeficiency virus), HTLV (human T-lymphotropic virus), AFB (Acid- Fast Bacilli), fungal, various viruses, ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme), and vitamin B12 were negative. A biopsy of the patient's lip was pending. The patient denied any sicca symptoms prior to the presentation of her paraplegia and did not have any optic neuritis findings during her hospital course. Three weeks later, the patient was found to have elevated neuromyelitis optica (NMO) antibodies. The patient was then transferred to Neurology for treatment of NMO spectrum disorder with Sjogren's syndrome. Setting: Spinal cord injury inpatient rehabilitation unit. Results or Clinical Course: Under Neurology, the patient underwent 2 courses of plasmapharesis and intravenous methylprednisolone with return of sensation to pinprick at the S4-5 levels, but no improvement in the patient's lower extremity motor function. Discussion: LETM as the initial neurologic manifestation of Sjogren's is rare, with the presence of NMO, even more atypical. There appears to be an overlap between the presence of both conditions, but their relationship, effect on treatment, and impact on prognosis remains poorly understood and unclear. Conclusions: NMO spectrum disorders should be considered in the differential of patient's testing positive for autoimmune conditions such as Sjogren's presenting with paraplegia secondary to longitudinal transverse myelitis.
