N2  - Introduction: Necrotising fasciitis (NF) of the head and neck is a rare, destructive soft tissue infection with an innocuous early presentation, associated with high mortality and morbidity. Aims: (i) to present patient incidence and data encountered in NUHS; and (ii) to propose an algorithm encompassing diagnosis, inpatient treatment, and postdischarge management. Methods: A retrospective review of all patients treated for NF of the head and neck in NUHS from January 2000 to June 2013. An algorithm was formulated following review of our patients and existing literature. Results: A total of 8 patients were treated; 7 had NF of the neck, 1 of the head. Typical presentation; rapidly progressing erythema, swelling, and pain. Seven had poorly controlled diabetes mellitus. Possible aetiology included tonsillitis, insect bite and acupuncture. All received emergency surgical debridement, intravenous antibiotics, and multidisciplinary supportive management. Six required reconstructive surgery. Two patients passed away. Morbidity included dysphagia, renal failure, facial palsy and depression No recurrences were recorded. Discussion & Conclusion: Our findings show: (i) the head and neck is rarely affected, consisting 4.2% (8 of 190) of all NF in NUHS during the study period; (ii) poorly controlled diabetes mellitus was a significant comorbidity; (iii) imaging and investigations should not delay aggressive surgical and medical therapy; (iv) patients suffer numerous complications; from the condition itself, prolonged hospitalisation (e.g. infections), debridements (e.g. functional impairment, wound cosmesis), and psychologically (e.g. depression); (v) a multidisciplinary approach is crucial to treat the condition and complications; (vi) postdischarge follow-up is essential for sequelae management; and (vii) a comprehensive algorithm was formulated.
