N2  - Introduction: Ten years after a local survey revealed major gaps between COPD recommendations and practice (Decramer et al. Monaldi Arch Chest Dis 2003;59:62-80), we re-evaluated alignment of COPD patient care by primary care physicians (PCP) with the GOLD guidelines. Methods: One-visit, cross-sectional, non-interventional study among randomly selected PCP, who completed a questionnaire on management of patients recently diagnosed with COPD. Results: 160 PCP enrolled 1132 patients (mean age 65±11 yrs; 2.6±1.9 comorbidities). PCP self-reported good compliance with guidelines (83%), appropriate step-up treatment (89%) and systematic screening for comorbidities (57% cardiac insufficiency, 21% osteoporosis, 8% depression). Two-thirds (65%) of PCP had followed spirometry training and 56% were actually performing it, though often (7/10) limited to prebronchodilation testing. Only half of COPD diagnoses were based on spirometry, mainly performed in second line care (7/10). Spirometry values necessary for COPD staging were recorded in only 5% of patients. Most (86%) gave a correct definition of exacerbations, which were managed with systemic corticosteroids (44%), antibiotics (60%) and/or mucolytics (81%); 6% required hospitalisation. A smoking cessation program was proposed to 96% of patients; pulmonary rehabilitation to 7%. Influenza and pneumococcal vaccination were performed in 84% and 72% of patients respectively. Conclusion: Despite PCP's increasing awareness and knowledge of certain aspects of COPD, large gaps in knowledge of the GOLD guidelines persist, especially with regard to spirometry for diagnosis and staging of COPD.
