N2  - Several epidemiologic studies have demonstrated an association of a lifetime history of depression with an increased risk of unprovoked epileptic seizures, of epilepsy and of a worse-response to treatment of the seizure disorder to pharmacotherapy and epilepsy surgery. However, if depression plays an actual pathogenic role in the development of epilepsy, its own neurobiologic pathogenic mechanisms would be expected to impact the epileptogenic process in some form. In this presentation we review data derived from animal models in support of this hypothesis. To that end three major categories of pathogenic mechanisms of depression will be reviewed. These include (i) endocrine disturbances, manifested by a hyperactive hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis (HPAA) resulting in high serum cortisol levels, (ii) neurotransmitter disturbances, including serotonin (5HT), norepinephrine (NE), glutamate and gamma-amino-butyric-acid (GABA), and (iii) inflammatory processes. Pretreatment of rats with corticosterone prior to rapid kindling of amygdala has shown an accelerated kindling process. Furthermore, this phenomenon was reproduced in animal models of depression associated with increased serum corticosterone. By the same token, animal models of epilepsy using the pilocarpine-lithium model in Wistar rats demonstrated an increased secretion of corticosterone, which correlated with the severity of the equivalent symptoms of depression in the rat. Decreased serotonergic and noradrenergic neurotransmissions have been one of the pivotal pathogenic mechanisms of depression. Lowering of serotonergic and noradrenergic transmission has been found to facilitate the occurrence of seizures in several animal models of epilepsy; this effect was blocked with the use of serotonergic and noradrenergic effects. Of note, high cortisol levels have been associated with a decreased expression of 5HT1A mRNA. Animal models of depression have revealed increased CNS glutamatergic activity, mediated by a decrease in the expression of glutamatetransporter proteins; by the same token, NMDA antagonists have been found to have an antidepressant effect in animal models of depression. In addition, animal models of depression have revealed a decreased GABAergic neurotransmission, resulting in increased cortical hyperexcitability. An increase in pro-inflammatory cytokines has been found to be an important pathogenic mechanism of depression. Among this, interleukin 1 beta (IL-1β) has been found to have pro-convulsant properties. For example, seizures triggered in rats with kainic acid and bicuculline are exacerbated with intracerebral injection of IL-1β, while administration of its naturally occurring antagonist (IL-!RA) has been found to show anticonvulsant activity. Furthermore, IL-1β, its receptor type 1 (IL-1R1) and IL-IRAwere upregulated in the brains of rats which underwent electrically and chemically induced seizures. Clearly, several of the neurobiologic pathogenic mechanisms of depression demonstrate an impact on the epileptogenic process in several animal models.
