N2  - Purpose: We have previously shown that, of 35 639 patients in the UK who were prescribed low-dose acetylsalicylic acid (ASA; 75-300 mg/day) in 2000-2007 for the secondary prevention of cardiovascular disease, approximately 33% discontinued therapy for at least 90 days. In the present study, we have followed up these patients to assess re-prescription rates (therapy re-initiation) and associated factors. Methods: This was a retrospective database study using The Health Improvement Network, a UK primary care database. Patients (N = 11 565) were followed up from the first day after their initial 90-day period of discontinuation (start date) until therapy re-initiation, death or the end of the study period (31 December 2010). Hazard ratios (HRs) for factors associated with therapy re-initiation were calculated using Cox regression models. Results: The cumulative incidence of therapy re-initiation was 85.2% over the entire follow-up period, and 63.5% of re-initiations occurred in the first 6 months after the start date. Factors significantly associated with a reduced likelihood of therapy re-initiation included: cardiovascular comorbidities recorded before the start date, in particular atrial fibrillation (HR: 0.45; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.42-0.49); gastrointestinal comorbidities recorded before the start date (HR: 0.90; 95% CI: 0.86-0.94 for overall high gastrointestinal risk); and having experienced an adverse drug reaction during therapy, particularly to ASA or other salicylates (HR: 0.26; 95% CI: 0.19-0.36). The use of gastroprotective medications or drugs used to treat cardiovascular diseases (most notably warfarin, HR: 0.28; 95% CI: 0.25-0.31) before the start date was also associated with a reduced likelihood of low-dose ASA re-initiation, as was older age (75-84 years relative to 49-64 years) and frequent use of healthcare services in the year before the start date (specialist referral/hospitalization, or ≥12 primary care practitioner visits relative to <6). Factors significantly associated with an increased likelihood of therapy re-initiation included: having a body mass index ≥ 30 kg/m2 (relative to 20-24 kg/m2); having diabetes mellitus, stable angina or depression recorded before the start date; or using non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs before the start date. Conclusion: Approximately 85% of patients who discontinued low-dose ASA therapy were subsequently re-prescribed this treatment within the study period, and about two-thirds of these re-initiations occurred within 6 months of the end of an initial 90-day period of discontinuation.
