N2  - Background and Aims: Exacerbations of COPD are associated with major disease and economic burden in patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). Therefore, we conducted a study in a primary care electronic medical records database from England&Wales to elucidate frequency of and traits associated with exacerbations in COPD patients. Methods: Retrospective cohort study of prevalent COPD patients identified from primary care data in the Clinical Practice Research Datalink (CPRD GOLD) who were aged ≥40 years and had ≥1 diagnostic code for COPD recorded on or after 1st April 2009 with ≥12 history before and from the start of observation (date of diagnosis = cohort entry) and an FEV1/FVC ratio <0.7. Occurrences of moderate (medical diagnosis or concurrent treatment of antibiotics [ATB] and oral corticosteroid [OCS]) or severe (requiring hospital admission) exacerbations were collected 12 months before and following cohort entry. Medical Research Council (MRC) dyspnoea score and GOLD stage of airflow limitation nearest to cohort entry, comorbidities any time before, and co-medications during the 12 months prior were also flagged. Results: The cohort consisted of 58,589 COPD patients, 47% females, mean age 69 yrs (SD 10yrs) and mean FEV1 60% pred (SD 20%). During the 12 months follow-up, 53% (n=31,049) of patients reported none, 24% (n=14,189) one, and 23% (n=13,351) two or more moderate-severe exacerbations. Patients with frequent exacerbations (≥2/year) compared to those with none during the 12 months follow-up (Table) were more likely to be female (51% vs. 44%), had poorer lung function (mean FEV1 %pred.: 54% vs. 63% ), more dyspnoea (MRC≥3 58% vs. 36%), more often diagnosed with comorbidities (heart attack, heart failure, depression), were more often managed with maintenance treatment in the past 12months (≥1Rx LABA/ICS 68% vs. 38%, ≥1Rx LAMA 53% vs. 28%), and had a higher rate of exacerbations in the prior 12 months (rate per person/per year 1.7 vs. 0.4). Frequency of COPD exacerbations increased with GOLD stage of airflow limitation overall (r=0.18, p<0.001); however, 27% of patients without exacerbations had poor lung function (GOLD stage ≥3). Further, 53% of frequent exacerbators had mild or moderate airflow limitation (Figure). Conclusions: In this UK primary care based cohort we observed that patients with frequent moderate-severe exacerbations (≥2 events/year) differed from those who did not seek treatment for any exacerbation over 12 months. Exacerbation frequency increased with decreasing level of airflow limitation. (Figure Presented).
