N2  - INTRODUCTION: Compared to nosocomial Methicillin Resistant Staphylococcus Aureus MRSA, Community- Associated MRSA (CA-MRSA) poses additional virulence factors including exotoxins like Panton-Valentine leukocidin (PVL) toxin that causes necrotizing pneumonia. Early recognition and appropriate antibiotics has substantial influence on survival in these cases. CASE PRESENTATION: A 41 year old Caucasian female with history of intravenous (IV) drug abuse and 27 pack year smoking history, she presented with one week history of progressive dyspnea, cough, yellowish sputum, hemoptysis, rigors and right sided pleuritic chest pain, all proceeded by one week history of influenza like illness. Physical exam revealed temperature of 97.7 F, heart rate 132, respiratory rate 24, blood pressure 99/47 mmHg, right upper chest crackles and SpO2 98% on 2 L Nasal Cannula. Labs and imaging studies showed WCC: 11.8 th/ul with 37% bands, lactic acid 1.8 mmol/L, and right upper lobe infiltrate on chest radiography. Patient was started on ceftriaxone and levaquin and admitted to regular medical floor. Within 48 hours, patient was transferred to the ICU with hypoxic respiratory failure refractory to conventional mechanical ventilation, shock, leukopenia, worsening multi-lobar infiltrate with right upper lobe cavitary lesions, acute renal failure, myocardial depression and encephalopathy. Antibiotic rigimen was changed to Vancomycin, piperacillin/tazobactam, and levaquin. Influenza A antigen came back positive, sputum and blood culture showed Gram positive cocci in clusters that later grew CA-MRSA. Bronchoscopy revealed inflamed right lung with purulent secretions. VA ECMO was utilized for six days and then discontinued successfully after improvement in oxygenation and hemodynamics. Three days later, hypoxia and hemodynamics became refractory again, antibiotic regimen was changed to Linezolid, Daptomycin and Nafcillin. High Frequency Oscillatory Ventilation HFOV was tried with no significant improvement, at this point family elected to withdraw care. DISCUSSION: CA-MRSA pneumonia is characterized by distinctive clinical features including young age, IV drug use, influenza like illness, rigors, hemoptysis, leukopenia, cavitary lesions and multi-lobar infiltrate. Case reports and experts suggest utilizing antibiotics that control toxin production rather than Vancomycin alone. CONCLUSIONS: Vancomycin alone might be inadequate for CA-MRSA pneumonia, adding agents active against CA-MRSA toxins might add clinical benefit.
