N2  - In the last few years it became evidence that glia play a central role in modulating neural and behavioral processes under both physiological and pathological conditions. Under normal quiescent conditions, glia respond to environmental stimuli and positively regulate the remodeling of synaptic connections and neural circuits, promoting memory consolidation, hippocampal long-term potentiation (LTP) and neurogenesis. These beneficial physiological effects are mediated by complex interactions among glia, neurons, neural precursor cells (NPCs) and peripheral immune cells, involving the responsiveness of glia to classical neurotransmitters and hormones, as well as the secretion and responsiveness of neurons and glia to low levels of “inflammatory” cytokines. For example, we recently reported that the microglial-derived cytokine interleukin (IL)-1 is expressed in the hippocampus during learning acquisition and activates IL-1 receptor-mediated signaling in astrocytes, which is critical for memory consolidation and LTP. Furthermore, we discovered that hippocampal microglia number and morphology are altered by rearing in enriched environment, and these alterations substantially contribute to the beneficial effects of environmental enrichment on memory functioning and neurogenesis. In conditions under which glia are strongly activated by infection, injury, neurodegeneration or exposure to severe/chronic stressful conditions, they change their morphology and functioning, and secrete high levels of pro-inflammatory cytokines that disrupt the delicate balance needed for their normal neurophysiological actions, producing direct detrimental effects on memory, neural plasticity and neurogenesis. Consistently, we found that blockade of microglia activation and IL-1 signaling, either by minocycline or by intra-hippocampal transplantation of NPCs with transgenic over-expression of IL-1ra, rescues the cognitive, emotional and neurogenic disturbances associated with mouse models of Alzheimer's disease and chronic stress-induced depression.
