N2  - Introduction: Methotrexate is a folic acid analog acting by inhibiting the dihydrofolate reductase leading to perturbation in DNA bases synthesis principally in rapid division cells like bacteria, neoplasic, hematopoietic and digestive cells. This drug has large indications and is used especially at low doses in some rhumatological diseases. It exposes to many adverse reactions mainly hematological or digestive. We reported here a case of a woman who presented psychiatric symptoms during methotrexate treatment. Observation: A 45-year old woman was admitted to hospital in psychiatric department, in April 2012, for severe psychiatric disorders with straight aggressiveness in a context of neighborhood conflict. Indeed, police forces have to intervene after she threw stones over dividing wall. She did not present psychiatric symptoms in her history excepted depressive symptoms leading to two previous consultations. At this time, psychologist concluded to a poorly structured and fragile personality without psychotic element. Besides, she was treated by sulfasalazine followed by etanercept for an ankylosing spondylitis, since 2008. Since March 2012, methotrexate was initiated because of loss of efficacy of previous treatments. On arrival in the department, the woman was sthenic, then she quickly calmed down. She was in denial of the facts and also presented elements of persecution to her neighbor. Clinically, she was neither tachypsychic, nor logorrheic. However, she presented graphorrhea with maladjusted scripts. Antipsychotic and sedative therapies were initiated but were low efficient. Sodium divalproate was then added without significant improvement. In front of this atypical case, responsibility of methotrexate was evoked. According to rhumatologists, methotrexate was stopped and replaced by sulfasalazine. Few days later, psychiatric symptoms significantly improved but articular pains occurred evoking acute attacks of her inflammatory disease inducing initiation of etanercept. Discussion: Concerning drug imputability in occurrence of these psychiatric symptoms, the chronology seems to be in favor of methotrexate responsibility because of significant improvement of symptomatology after its withdrawal. In the literature, mood disorders are described with methotrexate but, at our knowledge, there are no reports of paranoid delirium. Although this effect is unusual, clinicians should be aware of possible occurrence of severe psychiatric disorders during methotrexate therapy.
