N2  - Objective: Affective disorders (major depressive disorder, bipolar disorder) are associated with an increased risk of cardiovascular disease, which, at least partly, appears to be independent of psychopharmacological treatments used to manage these disorders. Reduced heart rate variability (SDNN) has been shown to increase the risk of death after myocardial infarction. In addition heart rate variability has been shown to be reduced in unmedicated physically healthy major depressive disorder patients. Less is known about heart rate variability in patients with bipolar disorder. Therefore we set out to investigate heart rate variability in euthymic patients with bipolar disorder. In this context particular attention is given to the omega-3 index (red blood cell levels of eicosapentaenoic acid plus docosahexaenoic acid, expressed as a percent of total fatty acids) as this index has been shown to be inversely related to the risk of cardiovascular disease. Methods: We assessed heart rate variability (SDNN) in 90 euthymic patients with bipolar disorder (Bipolar-I, Bipolar-II) on stable psychotropic medication, free of significant medical comorbidity and in 62 healthy controls. Heart rate variability was measured from electrocardiography under a standardized 30 min resting state condition. Besides the omega-3 index, age, sex, BMI, smoking, alcohol consumption and caffeine consumption as potential confounders were assessed. Results: Heart rate variability (SDNN) was significantly lower in patients with bipolar patients compared to healthy controls (35.4 msec versus 60.7 msec; p < 0.0001), whereas the omega-3 index did ot differ significantly between the groups (5.2% versus 5.3%). In a linear regression model, only group membership (bipolar disorder versus healthy controls) and age significantly predicted heart rate variability (SDNN). Discussion: In this study heart rate variability (SDNN) was significantly lower in euthymic patients with bipolar disorder compared to healthy controls. Therefore heart rate variability may provide a useful tool to study the impact of interventions aimed at reducing the increased risk of cardiovascular disease in euthymic patients with bipolar disorder.
