N2  - Background: The intestinal microbiota plays a role in metabolic, nutritional, physiological and immunological processes in children. Lack of normal microflora and presence of pathogenic microorganisms in the bowels can result in different gastroenteric diseases, allergy, depression of immunity. The development of culture-independent molecular techniques has provided new insights in the composition and diversity of the intestinal microbiota. Aim: To estimate quality and quantitative composition of gut microflora in new-born infants at age from 3 days to 1.5 months of life with culture-based and culture-independent molecular techniques. Patients and methods: We investigated 75 newborn infants at the age of 3 days to 1.5 months of life. We included neonates born vaginally at more than 36 weeks of gestation, all newborns from mothers who hadn't been treated with antibiotics, exclusive breastfeeding. Group-specific and strain-specific primers were created for detection of anaerobic microorganisms related to the groups of Bifidobacterium, Lactobacillus, Clostridium coccoides, Clostridium leptum, Atopobium, Prevotella which could be hardly identified with cultural methods. Results: Monitoring of gut microflora in neonates was conducted by classic and molecular-genetic methods. It was determined that the gut microbiocenosis of clinically healthy infants at the end of neonatal period was characterized with the high level of maintenance of anaerobic bacteria, 100% has Bifidobacterium, representatives of Bacteroides 55% dominant, Clostridium coccoide in 55%, Clostridium leptum in 30%, Atopobium rectifying in 25% children. The representatives of allochthonous bacteria were identified with high-frequency of Klebsiella (72.7%), Enterobacterium cloacae (17%), Staphylococcus aureus (45%), lecitinaze-positive Clostridium (22%) and yeasts Candida - in 12% of infants. Typical lactose-positive E.Coli and Enterococci were detected in all children.
