N2  - Objective: To analyse complications from a single neurosurgeon/ neurologist STN DBS implanting team (286 patients/569 electrodes). Background: DBS was first performed in Australia in 1997. Since then over 1300 DBS procedures have been performed with high DBS rates per capita compared to world data. Complications of surgery are well documented in the literature. Here we present our experience. Methods: Retrospective analysis of clinical records on 250 consecutive STN DBS patients, with follow-up to 6 months, were reviewed. Complications were classified as related to surgery, hardware or ongoing stimulation/medical management, and separated by timing with respect to surgery - less than 6 weeks; and 6 weeks to 6 months. Efficacy of DBS was established on 2 retrospective audits. Results: There were no deaths. Major procedural complications were rare- one haemorrhagic venous infarct (0.4%) and one perioperative seizure (0.4%). Six DBS leads (1.2%) were repositioned due to suboptimal positioning and 6 leads required replacement due to lead fracture. One further lead was replaced due to a suspected manufacturing problem. With one exception, lead complications occurred in the first 30 patients treated. The most common problem in the postoperative phase related to mild neuropsychiatric disturbance which ranged from mood elevation to anxiety, depression and apathy. Symptoms settled spontaneously or with supportive management in the majority. Acute psychosis requiring psychiatric admission occurred in one patient (0.4%). Speech disturbance, usually mild and characterised by dysarthrophonia, was common. There were a total of six infective complications (2.4%), more than 50% occurring late after implantation. Average presentation was at 13.4 months post implantation (0.5-33 months). Infections were treated with wash out of the IPG pouch and long term antibiotics (1), total (2) or partial (3) system ex-plantation with reimplantation (5) at 3 months - all without re-infection. Conclusions: Mild complications following STN DBS are common. Whilst patients must accept a long term device infection risk, the major morbidity of the procedure is low in experienced centres.
