N2  - Introduction: Several studies have shown the benefits of recombinant human activated protein C (APC) in countering sepsis-induced cardiac depression and organ injury. The clinical benefit of APC was associated with early administration of the drug and, experimentally, best results were obtained when APC was given upon induction of sepsis. The purpose of this study was to determine time-related effects of APC in countering sepsis-induced cardiac changes and in improvement of survival. Methods: Sepsis was induced in Wistar rats by cecal ligation and puncture (CLP) and APC was administered at 0, 6 or 12 hours post- CLP (groups ACP0, APC6 and APC12). Sham-operated animals (SHAM) and sepsis induced animals without APC administration were used as negative and positive control groups. All sepsis-induced animals were volume resuscitated and treated with antibiotics. Survival was evaluated for 48 hours. Further evaluation at 48 hours also included echocardiography, plasma cardiovascular profile (IL-6, tumor TNF-alpha, PAI-1, troponin I and BNP) and myocardial histopathology. Statistic analysis was performed by one-way ANOVA followed by Tukey's test or Kruskal-Wallis test when adequate. A P value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Results: APC did not improve survival. Groups ACP0 and APC12 presented higher mortality than SHAM. End-diastolic volume decreased in rats with CLP and without APC. BNP increased in groups APC0 and APC12. IL-6 levels were increased in CLP, APC0 and APC12 groups. PAI-1 levels were increased in all groups compared to SHAM rats. No alterations were observed in myocardial histopathology. Conclusion: Both early and delayed APC increased mortality in this model of septic shock. Moreover APC showed no benefits in myocardial function.
