N2  - Homocysteine (HCY) is a naturally occurring sulfur-containing amino acid. People with hyperhomocysteinemia (an elevation of HCY level in the blood) may get blood clots in either the veins (e.g. deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism) or arteries (e.g. stroke and heart attack). This review covers the risks associated with elevated homocysteine levels, screening for hyperhomocysteinemia, and the evidence in favour or against in evaluating the use of vitamin supplements that lower homocysteine levels. Currently, hyperhomocysteinemia is a well-known risk factor for variety of vascular diseases and neurodegenerative disorders namely dementia, depression, peripheral vascular diseases, erectile dysfunction, malignant diseases, pregnancy complications and osteoporosis. Common causes of hyperhomocysteinemia include kidney disease, lack of B vitamins (such as folate, vitamin B12, and vitamin B6) in the diet, hypothyroidism, alcoholism, certain medications as well as genetic defects. These diseases are associated with arterial, venous thrombosis and atherosclerosis. Treatment may consist of giving supplements of folic acid, vitamin B12, and/or vitamin B6. It also may include anticoagulant medications (blood thinners), such as aspirin, clopidogrel, heparin, low-molecular weight heparin, or warfarin, to prevent blood clots. But these vitamins supplementation do not reduce the risk/susceptibility of atherosclerosis in severe and hereditary hyperhomocysteinemia. Further, combination of vitamins B6, B12 and folic acid with other vitamins should be explored to decrease elevated HCY levels in blood. Experts are of the opinion that HCY measurement may be helpful for identifying subjects at greater risk of disease but lowering homocysteine levels has not been consistently shown to prevent heart attacks and strokes. © Pharmacie Globale.
