N2  - Introduction. Data on risk factors for the development of uncomplicated peptic ulcer disease (uPUD) are currently limited. We have therefore conducted a retrospective, observational study of potential risk factors in the UK general population. Methods. Data were collected from The Health Improvement Network, a large UK primary care database. Analysed patients were aged 40-84 years between January 1997 and December 2005, and had no history of PUD. Patients were followed up until they received a first diagnosis of uPUD or met an exclusion criterion (including developing complicated PUD), or until the end of the study period. A control group of 10 000 individuals was identified, frequency-matched to patients with uPUD by age, sex and year of index date. For nested case-control analysis, adjusted unconditional logistic regression models were used. Results. The overall incidence of uPUD (n=3923) was 0.75 per 1000 person-years, was higher in men than in women (p ,0.001), and increased with patients' age (p,0.001). Other factors positively associated with the disease were smoking (odds ratio [OR]: 1.90; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 1.72-2.10), gastrointestinal (GI) symptoms (OR: 1.88; 95% CI: 1.72-2.04), current use of dual antiplatelet therapy (DAT, combined ASA and clopidogrel; OR: 2.62; 95%CI: 1.12-6.11), non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs; OR: 1.70; 95%CI: 1.49-1.94), acetylsalicylic acid (ASA; OR: 1.54; 95% CI: 1.37-1.74), selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs; OR: 1.37, 95%CI: 1.09-1.72) or tricyclic antidepressants (OR: 1.29; 95% CI: 1.05-1.57), long-term use of oral corticosteroids (> 3 years; OR: 1.92; 95%CI: 1.04-3.53), stress (OR: 1.23; 95% CI: 1.03-1.47), depression (OR: 1.23; 95% CI: 1.11-1.36), and frequent visits to the primary care practitioner (OR: 1.48; 95% CI: 1.31-1.67 for 6 or more visits) or specialist referrals (OR: 1.34; 95% CI: 1.16-1.56 for 3 or more referrals). The effect of Helicobacter pylori infection status could not be analyzed, because data regarding this were not available for patients in the control group or for 57% of patients with uPUD. The current use of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) was positively associated with uPUD development relative to nonuse (OR: 2.05; 95% CI: 1.77-2.36), probably as a result of the use of these drugs to treat PUD symptoms. Relative to past use (ending 31-365 days before the index date), however, current use had a protective effect (OR: 0.66; 95% CI: 0.54-0.81). Conclusions. In the UK general population, the development of uPUD is associated with sex, age, smoking, GI symptoms, use of DAT, NSAIDs, ASA, SSRIs or tricyclic antidepressants, long-term use of oral corticosteroids, stress, depression and frequent use of healthcare services. Current use of PPIs protected against uPUD development relative to past use.
