N2  - Background: Prevalence and incidence of Lyme disease vary greatly depending on geographical location; in the United States, individuals residing in the Northeast and mid-Atlantic region are at increased risk for contraction of the disease. The detection of Lyme disease may present a particular diagnostic challenge to practitioners treating individuals who are comorbidly diagnosed with schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder (SZ). Clinical manifestations and related symptoms of both the early and late stages of Lyme disease may resemble those of SZ or of the side effects associated with psychotropic medications used in the treatment of these disorders. Methods: We evaluated the presence of Lyme disease at baseline in a double blind clinical trial of minocycline for schizophrenia. All study participants have a DSM-IV diagnosis of SZ, are between the ages of 18-65 years and have been taking a stable dose of clozapine for at least six months. Participants undergo a Lyme Titer, and all suspected cases are then confirmed using the enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay ELISA and the Western Blot. Results: A total of 29 participants were evaluated for the presence of Lyme disease. Two of these participants tested positive, representing 6.9% of tested participants. The first case (Lyme IgG/IgM Titer: 5.01, confirmed with Lyme Ab IgM by Western Blot) was a 27-year-old white/American Indian male, diagnosed with Schizophrenia, Paranoid Type. He reported mild dizziness, mild sedation, mild malaise and depression prior to the Lyme Titer. The second case (Lyme IgG/IgM Titer: 6.93, confirmed with Lyme Ab IgM by Western Blot) was a 55-year-old white male diagnosed with Schizoaffective Disorder, Bipolar Type. He reported moderate dizziness, severe sedation, moderate malaise, mild headache, mild sore throat, depression, arthritis and other physical side effects prior to the Lyme Titer. Conclusion: Current data illustrates the important role that healthcare practitioners have in emphasizing Lyme disease as a major health concern, as well as the opportunity to promote strategies to prevent patient contraction. There is limited information available on the relationship between Lyme and SZ; further research may consider investigating the phenomena, while practitioners in high-risk areas may consider routine testing as a part of their standard of care.
