N2  - Background: Irritability often occurs during interferon-α (IFN-α) therapy, independently from depression. ω-3 fatty acids could influence the adverse effects of this inflammatory cytokine. Methods: Eighty-eight non-depressed adults started pegylated IFN-α therapy for hepatitis C. Using an HP 6890/5973 GC/MS detector, we assessed two ω-3 fatty acids (docosahexaenoic acid -DHA 22:6n-3 and eicosapentaenoic acid - EPA 20:5n-3), and two omega-6 fatty acids (arachidonic acid - AA 20:4n-6 and dihomo-γ-linolenic acid - DGLA 20:3n-6). Changes in the Anger, Irritability, and Assault Questionnaire subscales were assessed using repeated-measure mixed effect analyses. We similarly compared 44 subjects with a history of major depression in remission - either not taking SSRIs (n=26) or stably taking SSRIs (n=18). Results: Irritability in those with high AA/DHA+EPA went from 10.2 +/- 0.8 to 11.6 +/- 1.0 within 2 months of IFN-α, while those with low AA/DHA+EPA decreased from 8.3 +/- 0.8 to 6.7 +/-1.0 (F5,38.7 = 2.7 p<0.05). As expected, SSRI use did mitigate increases in Montgomery-Asperg Rating Scale scores (F5,7.2 = 118 p<0.0001). But irritability in those not taking SSRIs went from 10.0 +/- 0.5 to 9.8 +/- 0.7, while those on SSRIs went from 10.6 +/- 1.2 to 13.2 +/- 1.5. Conclusions: Low baseline ratios of AA/DHA+EPA were associated with both decreased depression and low irritability during treatment. Conversely, SSRIs may prevent depression during IFN-α therapy but may worsen irritability. This suggests that ω-3 supplementation may be effective for preventing both depression and irritability in people exposed to elevated inflammatory cytokines.
