N2  - Background: The serotonin system and the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis are involved in the biological vulnerability to suicidal behaviour. Altered levels of dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) and its sulphate ester DHEAS have been reported in neuropsychiatric conditions such as depression and posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Methods: The purpose of this study was to investigate CSF levels of 5-Hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA) and CSF and plasma levels of cortisol and DHEAS in 28 medication free suicide attempters and 19 healthy volunteers. Furthermore, we assessed the relationship between neuroendocrine measures, childhood trauma and depression severity in suicide attempters. All patients were followed up for cause of death. As the study design includes a longitudinal part, with the mean follow-up time of 21 years, we investigated whether CSF cortisol, 5-HIAA or DHEAS would predict subsequent suicide in suicide attempters. Results: Suicide attempters had higher CSF and plasma cortisol levels compared to healthy volunteers. Male suicide attempters had higher CSF DHEAS levels and female suicide attempters had lower CSF 5-HIAA levels compared to male and female healthy volunteers respectively. In male suicide attempters CSF 5-HIAA showed a positive correlation with CSF cortisol/DHEAS ratio. Exposure to interpersonal violence as a child showed a negative correlation with CSF cortisol/ DHEAS ratio adjusted for age, gender and depression severity in a regression analysis. Low CSF 5-HIAA was a predictor of suicide whereas high CSF cortisol showed a trend to be a predictor. Conclusions: The results underlie the important role of the serotonergic system and HPA axis in suicidal behaviour.
