N2  - A 43-year-old Brazilian man reported lesions on his palms and soles that had been present for 8 years. The lesions were asymptomatic and had increased in number over time. The patient denied a history of previous trauma or problems with his nails or teeth. He was employed as a painter. Being adopted, he was unaware of a family history of similar skin conditions, but did state that his adolescent son seemed to be developing similar appearing lesions. Physical examination revealed areas of punctate, palmoplantar crater-like keratoses, each 2-to 5-mm in diameter. These covered the majority of the surfaceofthe palms and soles. A punch biopsy specimen was obtained from the palm of the hand that showed a compact orthokeratotic column with underlying hypergranulosis and an abrupt transition to the normal orthokeratotic stratum corneum at the borders of the hyperkeratotic column. This was consistent with type I punctate palmoplantar keratoderma (Buschke-Brauer-Fischer disease). The patient was educated about the familial inheritance of the disease and was asked to have age-appropriate screenings with his primary care provider for malignancy. The patient was offered the option of systemic or topical treatment and opted for topical treatment. He was given salicylic acid to apply to affected areas in the evenings and 40% urea cream to apply to affected areas every morning. This resulted in softening and thinning of his keratoses. Punctate palmoplantar keratoderma (PPPK) is an autosomal dominant inherited trait with an incidence of 1 per 100,000. The typical presentation is multiple 2-to 8-mm keratoses on the palms and soles appearing during adolescence or after 20 years of age. The lesions begin as pinpoint, firm, translucent papules that develop a central, keratotic core that leave a central depression when removed. On histology, PPPK lesions show compact columns of orthohyperkeratosis over a sharply limited area of the epidermis. Despite its rarity, it is imperative to identify PPPK, because some studies have reported associations between PPPK, cancers of the colon, pancreas, kidney, and breast, and lymphomas. Patients with PPPK should be offered malignancy screenings. Moreover, because of its autosomal dominant inheritance, other family members should also be screened for this disease. The treatment is aimed at softening the individual lesions through the use of keratolytics and retinoids.
