N2  - AIM: To describe a case of intracranial hypertension in a 72-year old woman after the long term use of citalopram. BACKGROUND: Pseudotumor cerebri (PTC) is a disease characterized by intracranial hypertension without ventriculomegaly, in the absence of a mass lesion or meningeal process.1 The most important manifestation of the disease is papilledema, which may lead to progressive optic atrophy and severe loss of visual field and central visual acuity. PTC is subdivided in idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) or intracranial hypertension from a secondary cause, depending upon whether or not an etiology is established. Various exogenous agents have been implicated in the development of intracranial hypertension from a secondary cause, including Vitamin A and several antibiotics. METHODS: Observational case report. RESULTS: A 72-year old non-obese woman was referred due to an incidental finding of bilateral papilledema. For many months prior to referral the patient had experienced intermittent headaches, transient visual obscurations and dizziness. The patient was in good general health and her only medication was Citalopram, which she had been using for 25 years at a daily dose of 40 mg. The visual acuities were 0.8, the visual fields showed enlarged blind spots and the neurologic examination was unremarkable. ESR and CRP were normal. An MRI of the brain was normal, and a normal CT venography ruled out a cerebral venous sinus thrombosis. An elevated CSF opening pressure of 35 cm of water was measured on an otherwise unremarkable lumbar puncture. Despite acetazolamide treatment, the headaches worsened, a pulsatile tinnitus developed, the papilledema persisted and the visual field defects deteriorated. Accordingly, after 6 months of progression, a ventriculo-peritoneal shunt was inserted after which most symptoms as well as the papilledema resolved. IIH in a non-obese elderly woman being highly unlikely, a secondary cause was investigated. Citalopram was suspected to be the aetiologic factor, and a gradual withdrawal of the drug was initiated. CONCLUSION: A case of IIH in a 72-year old woman is presented. Citalopram, a highly used drug for the treatment of depression and anxiety disorders, is likely to have been the secondary cause. There have been no previous reports of this connection.
