N2  - Introduction: In the elderly population with dementia, the inadvertent ingestion of foreign bodies is a serious risk. The increased prevalence of dental prosthetics in this age group increases this risk, which can result in dysphagia and esophageal obstruction, and even lead to perforation 1. Case: An 86 year old male veteran with history of vascular dementia, PTSD, major depression, atrial fibrillation, type 2 DM and left submandibular adenocarcinoma s/p resection presented to the hospital for evaluation of increasing confusion and diarrhea. In the ED, he was agitated; to safely obtain a CT scan of the head and lumbar puncture he received intravenous lorazepam and haloperidol worsening his hyperactive delirium. Further work up revealed pneumonia and antibiotics were initiated. During his hospitalization, he complained of new-onset dysphagia, odynophagia and gurgling voice with a strong cough reflex. He denied eating anything out of the ordinary. Modified barium swallow was performed, revealing two metallic density foreign bodies in the pharynx. ENT was consulted emergently and nasopharyngolaryngoscopy showed the presence of a partial upper denture plate in his glottis. It was subsequently removed in the OR, with complete resolution of dysphagia symptoms. Discussion: The accidental swallowing of dentures can have several different presentations, which can result in a challenging diagnosis. The most frequent symptom is dysphagia; symptoms arise based upon the time passed since ingestion and progression of the denture down the digestive tract 1. More serious complications can include compromise of airway through esophageal obstruction, vascular erosion, and perforation 2. In the elderly with dementia or cognitive impairment, suppression of the cough reflex in sedation and delirium pose serious risk factors for increased aspiration and thus ingestion of dental prostheses 3 .Therefore, the recognition of early signs and symptoms along with timely intervention is important in reducing the risk of complications.
