N2  - Objective. Epidemiological studies show that, in ∼25% of patients with irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), bacterial gastroenteritis precedes the onset of the disorder. The multi-herbal drug STW 5 (Iberogast®) was effective for treatment of symptoms in patients with IBS or functional dyspepsia, therefore triggering the question for the mechanisms of action involved. Methods. STW 5 and its components were studied in inflamed small and large intestinal preparations of the rat. The inflammation was induced by intraluminal instillation of 2,4,6-trinitrobenzene sulfonic acid (TNBS, 0.01 M). Contractions were measured isometrically. Gene expression was determined using RT-PCR. Results. Preincubation of STW 5 (64-512 μg/ml) or its components in equivalent concentrations together with TNBS prevented the inflammation-induced disturbances in the muscle wall and hence the depression of acetylcholine (ACh)-induced contractions. Cytotoxicity testing revealed a cell protective effect of STW 5. STW 5 inhibited the increased TNF-α gene expression in ileum but not in colon whereas the gene expression of IL-10 was increased in both preparations by STW 6 (Iberis amara), a component of STW 5. Adenosine A2A receptors was found to be involved in the regulation of TNF-α gene expression by STW 5, whereas the cucurbitacins E and I in STW 6 could be responsible for activation of the IL-10 pathway. Conclusion. STW 5 reveals significant anti-inflammatory properties which contribute to the reduction of morphological and contractile disturbances. Its mode of action seems to be twofold, inhibition of the TNF-α and activation of the IL-10 pathway, giving a clear-cut correlation with symptom improvements in clinical trials and highlighting the relevance of STW 5 as a therapeutic approach in IBS.
