N2  - Introduction: Any disruption of the skin barrier function can result in wound cellulitis. Thermal burns and donor sites are prone to these infections, but the incidence of cellulitis is not documented in the burn literature. At our burn center, patients do not receive prophylactic systemic antibiotics for acute burns. Wounds, which develop redness extending beyond the wound margin, are empirically treated with antibiotics directed against gram-positive organisms unless previous cultures suggest another type of organism. We reviewed all patients treated at our burn center for a three-year period to evaluate the incidence and risk factors of burn associated cellulitis. Methods: After IRB approval, all adult burn injuries treated at our facility, both in the hospital or as outpatients in the clinic or ER from January 2009 through December 2011, were reviewed. We included thermal, chemical and electrical injuries. We excluded patients who received prophylactic antibiotics from referring facilities, antibiotics for an indication other than cellulitis, and deaths <24 post-injury. Demographic data collected: age, sex, burn size and location, mechanism of injury, burn depth and comorbidities. Time from injury to onset of cellulitis and timing of burn wound excision were also included. Logistic regression analysis of comorbidities was performed. Results: There were 416 burn patients remaining after exclusions (347 inpatients, 69 outpatients). Overall median burn TBSA was 3.1%. Burn wound cellulitis was treated in 144 patients, for an overall incidence of 34.6%; 35.4% of hospitalized and 30.4% of outpatients. There were 26 (11.4%) donor site infections. Odds of developing cellulitis in lower extremity/foot burns are three times greater in comparison to all other body areas (OR = 3.1086, 95% CI 2.23-4.55, p<0.001) Two comorbidities were associated with an increased risk of developing cellulitis in at least one burned area: Obesity (OR = 2.07, 95% CI 1.06- 4.06, p=0.03), and depression/anxiety (OR = 2.06, 95% CI 1.22 - 3.49, p=0.007). No other demographic or timing data yielded significant correlations. Conclusions: These data demonstrate a high incidence of cellulitis associated burn injury and treatment. Burns involving the lower extremities, obese patients and those with depression/ anxiety are at higher risk. A prospective randomized trial investigating the use of prophylactic antibiotics in this high-risk cohort may be warranted.
