N2  - Objective: Supplementation with eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA) has been shown to modulate production of pro-inflammatory cytokines in healthy adults. In pregnancy, maternal dietary supplementation with DHA may reduce several inflammation-associated complications, including early preterm birth <34 weeks, neonatal seizures, and neonatal brain injury. Our objective was to investigate the effect of prenatal DHA- and EPA-rich fish oil supplementation on maternal and fetal cytokine production. Methods: We enrolled 126 women at risk for depression in a trial of prenatal supplementation with EPA-rich fish oil (1060 mg EPA plus 274 mg DHA), DHA-rich fish oil (900 mg DHA plus 180 mg EPA), or soy oil placebo. Maternal blood was collected at enrollment (12-20 weeks gestation) and after supplementation (34-36 weeks gestation). Umbilical cord blood was collected at delivery from 102 neonates. Plasma aliquots were prepared by centrifugation and were stored at -80 degrees C. We analyzed 341 plasma specimens for 16 human cytokines using multiplex immunoassays. Results: Prenatal supplementation had no significant effect on maternal cytokine profiles. By contrast, supplementation with EPA- or DHA-rich fish oil significantly lowered cord plasma concentrations of the pro-inflammatory cytokines IL 1α (P<0.05), IL 1β (P<0.01), IL6 (P<0.05), and IFN γ (P<0.05). Conclusions: Maternal fish oil supplementation significantly reduced production of several pro-inflammatory cytokines in the fetal compartment. Future research should investigate the pathways underlying this immune-modulating effect.
