N2  - BACKGROUND: P-glycoprotein (P-gp), an ABC transporter expressed in liver, gut, kidney and peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs), modulates tacrolimus pharmacokinetics, intracellular pharmacology, and adverse effects (AE) impacting overall patient response. No data assessing P-gp function in PBMC during tacrolimus immunosuppression over a 12-hour dosing interval in African American (AA) and Caucasian (C) male (M) and female (F) renal transplant recipients (RTR) has been reported. METHODS: This pharmacology study assessed P-gp function in PBMC in 22 AAM, 14 AAF and 16 CM and 16 CF stable RTR receiving tacrolimus and mycophenolic acid immunosuppression. The P-gp function was quantified by flow cytometric measurement of cyclosporine (CYA, 2.5 μM) -reversible efflux of the P-gp substrate, DiOC2(3) by % change Mean Fluorescent Intensity (MFI) with CYA (% ΔMFI-CYA) at tacrolimus trough (0 hours), 4, 8 and 12 hours. A 50% increase in %ΔMFI-CYA correlates with significant P-gp function based on P-gp-expressing cell line models. Selected TAC AEs including tremor, myopathy, headache, insomnia, and depression were assessed using objective rating criteria. RESULTS: The % ΔMFI-CYA reported as mean (standard deviation) is below: The % ΔMFI-CYA was a significant covariate (P=0.007) for selected tacrolimus AE. No difference over dosing interval was found. CONCLUSION: P-gp function in PBMC demonstrated race and race x sex influences among AA and Caucasian RTR. Measurement of P-glycoprotein function may be a clinical surrogate marker to assess interpatient variability in intracellular and extracellular tacrolimus pharmacology and enhance clinical monitoring. (Table presented).
