N2  - Background: Down syndrome (DS or T21) is a neurodevelopmental disorder and the commonest cause of genetic intellectual disability. T21 is caused by a whole or a part of a third chromosome 21 (HSA21) and it is only recently that basic research has provided some hope for identifying some pharmacotherapeutic targets by the study of few HSA21 candidate genes and their potential roles in neurodevelopment and aging. The numerous new T21 murine models provide the possibility to study the efficiency of old or new drugs for some cognitive deficits. These studies target at: a) individual HSA 21 genes such as DYRK1A; b) phenotypic consequences of trisomy such as degeneration or inflammation (AchE inhibitors, minocycline), neurotransmission (piracetam, PTZ); c) perturbations pathways (serotonine, NMDA, oxidative pathways) or neuroprotective pathways (NAP). Results: We will present a summary of these various targets and emphasize on Dyrk1A gene target and its inhibition by EGCG (epigallocatechingallate). Dyrk1A phophorylates many targets such as Tau and APP proteins involved in the neurohistopathology of both Alzheimer disease (AD) and T21. Dyrk1A overexpression induces behavioural and biochemical deficits in T21 murine models which can be reversed by EGCG. These results have led to the first pilot trial conducted in Spain by Dr. M. Dierssen on young adults and will be followed soon by two others trials in France and Spain. Conclusions: DS persons have very variable cognitive deficits and share with AD patients some early clinical symptoms such as poor smelling, sleep deficits, aphasia, poor motor coordination which could also be reversed by EGCG. Thus we suggest to add to the cognitive tests, an evaluation of smelling and sleep recovery (even through questionnaires). Since persons aged 30-50 with T21 are more vulnerable to depression, cognitive decline and dementia for some of them only, EGCG trials should also be beneficial to them.
