N2  - Major depression with melancholic features is consistently associated with a pro-infl ammatory phenotype characterized by elevated cortisol and the cytokines tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF α ) and interleukin (IL)-6. The acute phase protein CRP, whose elevation is reported to increase the risk of cardiovascular disease, is also frequently elevated in depression. Several large scale prospective studies indicate that patients with major depression have a significantly increased risk of cardiovascular disease. This increased risk is seen in both men and women and is present when risk factors such as cigarette smoking, poor diet and a lack of exercise are controlled for. A diet high in the polyunsaturated fatty acid ω 3 is associated with a reduced risk of cardiovascular disease and depression. We tested the hypotheses that in major depression (1) higher ω 6: ω 3 ratios are associated with a pro-infl ammatory cytokine profile (2) ω -6: ω 3 fatty acid ratio is greater in antidepressant non-responders than responders. We found that depressed patients had elevations in arachidonic acid (AA) levels. The eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA):AA ratios were as follows: controls 0.08 ± 0.01; treatment responders 0.08 ± 0.01; treatment nonresponders 0.04 ± 0.01. These differences are signifi cant (p < 0.001). AA and IL-6 were highly correlated. Our studies show that depressed patients have increases in intra-abdominal fat content and elevated platelet Gp 1b receptor numbers. Both of these factors increase the risk of ischaemic heart disease.
