N2  - Long-term potentiation (LTP) is a common mechanism of memory storage in CNS. LTP at C-fiber synapses in spinal dorsal horn is induced by intensive electrical stimulation (at 100Hz or 2Hz), nerve injury or tissue inflammation. LTP-inducible stimulation leads to lasting hyperalgesia in rodents and in human. Accordingly, the spinal LTP is considered as a synaptic model of pathological pain. Accumulating evidence has demonstrated that activation of microglia regulates synaptic plasticity by releasing pro-inflammatory cytokines. In hippocampus activation of microglia inhibits LTP, while in spinal dorsal horn we found that microglia inhibitor minocycline dose-dependently depressed the synaptic strength at C-fiber synapses. Minocycline at 50 μM did not affect spinal LTP of C-fiber evoked field potentials induced by high frequency stimulation (HFS), at 100 μM complete blocked the spinal LTP and at 200 μM HFS induced long-term depression (LTD). Following spinal LTP induction Src-family kinases (SFKs) were activated only in microglia, but not in astrocytes and neuron and SFKs inhibitors also enabled HFS to induce LTD. The effects of minocycline and SKFs inhibitors are completely reversed by pretreatment with TNF-αlpha, indicating that activated microglia may contribute to LTP by releasing TNF-α. In hippocampus TNF-α inhibits LTP via activation of NF-κB, JNK and p38 signaling, while in animals with neuropathy TNF-α induced spinal LTP via the same signaling. In naive animals TNF-α was essential for spinal LTP induction. Therefore, the drugs targeting at microglia and TNF-α may not only attenuate chronic pain but also improve memory in hippocampus.
