N2  - Therapeutic use of interferon-alpha (IFN-a) a proinflammatory cytokine for chronic viral hepatitis and malignant diseases, causes depression in about 30% of patients, which frequently interrupts the treatment. IFN-a-treated animals also show depression-like behaviors, but their mechanisms remain largely unknown. Neuronal progenitors in the dentate gyrus of hippocampus continuously generate new neurons in the adulthood. Previous studies suggest that decreased neurogenesis in the hippocampal dentate gyrus is involved in the neuropathology of several psychiatric diseases including depression. Here, we investigated the effect of IFN-a on the hippocampal neurogenesis and depression-related behaviors in rodent (mouse) and primate (common marmoset) models. IFN-a treatment for over 4 weeks significantly suppressed the proliferation of neuronal progenitors, followed by decrease in the number of new neurons in the dentate gyrus and induction of depressive behaviors, in both mice and common marmosets. In addition, the IFN-a treatment activated microglia and increased expression of proinflammatory cytokines in the hippocampus. Co-administration of minocycline, an antibiotic known to suppress microglial activation, completely cancelled the IFN-a-induced defects in neurogenesis and behavior in mice. These results suggest that IFN-a suppresses hippocampal neurogenesis through microglial activation, which might be involved in the neuropathology of IFN-a-induced depression.
