N2  - Purpose: A 48-year-old female with a past medical history of depression, deep venous thrombus, and subarachnoid hemorrhage secondary to a ruptured intracranial aneurysm presented to the emergency department with jaundice and altered sensorium. She had undergone craniotomy with hematoma evacuation two months prior at which time she was started on valproic acid. Her other medications included propranolol, coumadin and duloxetine. She denied alcohol and illicit drug use. On examination, she was hemodynamically stable. She had scleral icterus without splenomegaly, ascites or skin changes suggestive of chronic liver disease. She was confused and noted to have asterixis. Initial laboratory data revealed total leukocyte count of 6.2 x 103/uL, platelets 205×103/uL and hematocrit 37.1%. Liver function tests revealed: AST 232 u/L, ALT 270 u/L, total bilirubin 25.3 mg/dL, direct bilirubin 17.2 mg/ dL, albumin 2.4 g/dL, prothrombin time 43.5 seconds, INR 3.9 and ammonia 120 umol/L. A detailed serological work-up for etiology of liver failure was negative. The patient was managed conservatively for hepatic encephalopathy with lactulose and empirically started on prednisone 40mg daily for probable autoimmune hepatitis. Her hospital course was complicated by progressively worsening mental status and hyperbilirubinemia. She underwent transjugular liver biopsy revealing submassive hepatocellular necrosis strongly suggestive of a drug/toxin-induced etiology. No viral cytopathic effects or lymphoplasmacytic inflammatory infiltrates suggestive of autoimmune hepatitis were identified and iron stain was negative. Valproic acid-induced acute liver failure was diagnosed and she underwent orthotropic liver transplant with an uneventful postoperative recovery. Valproic acid is a known hepatotoxic agent but acute liver failure is rare. Our current interpretation of the natural history and outcome of transplantation in these cases is based on case reports and small case series in the pediatric population. Interestingly, children transplanted for valproic acidassociated liver failure have a significantly lower one-year survival probability compared to other anti-epileptic or drug-induced acute liver failure. It has been postulated that valproic acid-induced acute liver failure in children represents an unmasking of underlying mitochondrial disease but it is unclear if the same holds true for the adult population. We report this case not only for its rarity but also to address gaps in understanding the clinical presentation, progression and outcome of this potentially fatal condition in the adult population.
