N2  - Purpose: Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) Syndrome is a rare delayed drug hypersensitivity reaction. Hepatitis is one of the most frequent visceral manifestations, while pancreatitis occurs in <5% of cases. We report two cases of DRESS syndrome with both hepatitis and pancreatitis. Case 1: A 23-year-old female with history of borderline personality disorder and depression presented to the ED with fever, body aches, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain and rash. Past medical history was noticeable for her starting on lamotrigine 10 days prior to presentation. In the ED, the patient was hypotensive. The rash was diffuse, maculopapular, erythematous, non-blanching and non-vesicular. Laboratory investigations showed normal WBC count but with eosinophilia (9.8%), elevated liver transaminases (AST 1333 IU/L, ALT 305 IU/L) and elevated lipase (962 IU/L). Infectious work-up including blood cultures, chest x-ray and urine cultures were negative; viral hepatitis panel was negative; gall bladder ultrasound showed no gall stones. On admission, lamotrigine was discontinued; she was maintained on IV fluids; and antibiotics were started empirically until all cultures came back negative. Skin biopsy revealed epidermis with patchy and superficial mixed acute and chronic dermal inflammation. The patient was diagnosed with DRESS syndrome with acute pancreatitis and hepatitis due to lamotrigine. Her symptoms, liver enzymes and lipase continued to improve during the hospitalization and eventually normalized. Case 2: A 58-year-old male with history of gout presented to the ED with malaise, fever, abdominal pain and rash. Past medical history was significant for patient starting allopurinol 3 weeks prior to presentation. The rash was diffuse, erythematous and pustular. Laboratory investigations revealed elevated WBC count (12.5 K/μL) with eosinophila (10%), elevated liver enzymes (AST 873 IU/L, ALT 699 IU/L, bilirubin 4.3 mg/dL), elevated amylase (359 IU/L) and lipase (345 IU/L). Infectious work-up including blood cultures, chest x-ray and urine cultures were negative; viral hepatitis panel was negative. On admission, allopurinol was discontinued; the patient was maintained on IV fluids and started on high dose steroids (prednisone 100 mg/day). Skin biopsy showed neutrophilic dermatitis with acute suppurative folliculitis. The patient was diagnosed with DRESS syndrome due to allopurinol. His symptoms, liver transaminases and lipase improved with steroids and fluids. The patient was discharged on steroid taper regimen. Conclusion: DRESS syndrome is a rare clinical entity that should be considered in the differential diagnosis of pancreatitis and hepatitis in the setting of new medications.
