N2  - Purpose: Mycoplasma pneumoniae has been linked with hepatitis in children. In adults, hepatic involvement is considered a rare presentation of M. pneumoniae infection. We report 2 adult patients having hepatic disease from M. pneumoniae without the lung involvement. Case 1: A 51-year-old Caucasian female presented with fever, right upper quadrant pain and night sweats for 5 days. Patient had a history of CAD, hyperlipidemia and depression. She denied alcohol or illicit drug use. Physical examination was unremarkable except for a temperature of 102 F. Patient was found to have neutropenia (ANC < 600) and elevated liver enzymes as follows: AST 3400 U/L (5-37), ALT 3700 U/L (5-41), and alkaline phosphatase 600 U/L (20-140). Total bilirubin, CXR and urine analysis were normal. The following studies were performed and were normal/ negative: acute viral hepatitis panel; serologies for EBV, CMV, HSV 1 and 2, and parvovirus; nasal swab for influenza A and B; HIV screen; Quantiferon Gold; Legionella urinary antigen; and ANA, ASMA, and AMA assays. Abdominal sonogram was also normal. However, her M. pneumoniae IgM serology was positive. Patient was initially started on meropenem and levofloxacin for febrile neutropenia. She was later switched to levofloxacin for 7 days. Her LFT's normalized within 1 month. Case 2: A 46-year-old African American male presented with shortness of breath and right sided chest pain for 2 days. Patient had a history of depression, diabetes mellitus and hypertension. He denied alcohol or illicit drug use. Physical examination was unremarkable. Patient was found to have leukocytosis of 23,000/μL and elevated liver enzymes as follows: AST 661 U/L (5-37), ALT 1307 U/L (5-41). His alkaline phosphatase, bilirubin, CXR and CT scan of chest were normal. The following studies were performed and were normal/negative: acute viral hepatitis panel; serologies for EBV, CMV, and HSV 1 and 2; HIV screen; and ANA. Abdominal sonogram was also normal. Lastly, his M. pneumoniae IgM serology was positive. Patient was started on levofloxacin for 7 days and his LFT's normalized. Extrapulmonary features of M. pneumoniae may occur in the skin, cardiovascular, neurologic and hematologic systems, but few cases of infection in adults with liver involvement and no respiratory tract infection have been reported. Our cases reinforce the fact that acute hepatitis can occur in M. pneumoniae infection in adults. Rapid recovery of LFT's after initiation of antibiotics suggests direct hepatocyte infection over an immune mediated mechanism. Our cases help raise awareness of the possibility of M. pneumoniae infection, with or without lung involvement, as a part of the evaluation of undetermined hepatitis.
