N2  - Purpose: MK is a 31-year-old male with a history of HIV/AIDS with a CD4 count of 153, anxiety, depression and methamphetamine use who presented with diffuse abdominal pain, bloating and watery diarrhea for one week. A CT of the abdomen demonstrated distal colonic inflammation with mild wall thickening in the descending colon. He was placed on broad spectrum iv antibiotics. A flexible sigmoidoscopy demonstrated deep ulceration in the rectum. He subsequently began to have hemorrhaging of blood from his rectum and emergently underwent a subtotal colectomy and later, removal of the rectum due to continued bleeding. Pathologic analysis of the colon demonstrated fulminant colitis with extensive ulceration. An EBER1 stain demonstrated focal high-density clusters of large mononuclear cells in the lamina propria as well as scattered EBER1 positive cells in the deeper layers of the bowel wall, suggesting EBV as the pathologic cause of colitis. Aft er an extended post-operative recovery, complicated by a partial small bowel obstruction and TPN-associated cholestasis, the patient did well and was discharged home. EBV infections of the GI tract are rare. Only six cases have been reported in the literature that have been associated with immunodeficiency due to chemotherapy. Only two have been described in patients with normal immunity. EBV is a linear, double-stranded DNA that persists in >90% of humans worldwide. It is often implicated in self-limiting mononucleosis and associated with lymphoma. EBV-associated lymphoma in the gastrointestinal tract is well-known, but, involvement in the tract without overt lymphoma is rare. Infections of the GI tract often present with fever, diarrhea and rectal bleeding. Clinical features that differentiate EBV-associated colitis from other causes include dense lymphoid infiltrates in the lamina propria as well as positive immunohistochemical staining. In summary, EBV infections of the GI tract are rare, and can possibly lead to fulminant colitis. Reported cases are almost always associated with immunodeficiency. However, these cases are usually associated with immunosuppressive therapy. It is therefore important to consider EBV-related disease when evaluating severe colitis of an unknown origin, especially in a patient with immunocompromise from any etiology.
