N2  - Purpose. Neonatal myocardial infarction (MI) is a rare event, with a high mortality rate (90%). We describe two different cases of newborns with normal heart and normal coronary pattern, survived to myocardial infarction. Methods. First case: a full term neonate presented with severe asphyxia after cesarean delivery. He was pale, hypotonic and polipnoic. He was treated with oxygen administration. Heart rate was of 180 beats minute and the noninvasive blood pressure was of 40/30 mmHg. Nasal cPAP ventilation was started and hypoglycemia and metabolic acidosis were corrected. One hour after admittance he presented a wide QRS tachycardia with a heart rate of 250 beats minute. The 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG) showed a ventricular tachycardia (VT) (Fig. 1) with morphology of right ventricular outflow tract and inferior axis that spontaneously resolved after few minutes. A transthoracic echocardiogram (TTE) showed a structural normal heart, normal origin of coronary arteries, severe global hypokinesia of the left ventricle (LV) ejection fraction (EF) of 10%, second degree mitral regurgitation (MR) and mild tricuspid regurgitation with an estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 50 mmHg. Systemic blood flow was maintained by the persistence of a wide patent arterial duct (PDA) with a large shunt from the pulmonary artery and descending aorta. After some hours the ECG showed that ST tract was elevated in D1 mm and aVL and a marked ST segment depression from V1 to V6 (Fig. 2). Baby was treated with low doses of inotropic infusional therapy and furosemide maintaining an adequate cardiac output. Second case: a full term female neonate, born by normal vaginal delivery with birth weight 2.880 kg and history of maternal smoking during pregnancy, referred at our institute for suspicious of myocardial ischemia at ECG. On admission she was hypotonic with erythrosic and dry skin, heart rate was of 150 beats minute and the noninvasive blood pressure was of 50/30 mmHg. Blood examination showed a very high haematocrit level (Htc) 80%, metabolic acidosis and normal C-reactive protein, normal platelet count. Echocardiography showed hypocontractile LV with EF of 35%, moderate MR, trivial aortic regurgitation, small PDA with left to right shunt. The ECG showed inferiorlateral wall myocardial infarction (Figs. 3 and 4). Infective agents were excluded at laboratory test and thrombophilia screening, urinary amino acids and glycogenesis tests were negative. She was successfully treated with inotropic support, i.v. heparin, dual oral antiplatelet therapy (acetyl salicylic acid, 5 mg/kg, and clopidogrel, 0.2 mg/kg) and vasodilatators. After 7 days, she developed severe myocardial hypertrophy, probably due to compensatory mechanisms. Conclusions. Neonatal myocardial infarction is a rare event in the newborn with normal heart and normal coronary pattern, with very high mortality. Thromboembolism, coagulopathy, birth asphyxia, erithroblastosis and enteroviral myocarditis are the most common causes. Early diagnosis and management in a tertiary centre is necessary. We describe two different cases of neonatal myocardial infarction. The first one had MI and VT after a birth asphyxia with natural arterial duct assistance and low drug assistance; it represents a rare case in terms of treatment and long-term follow-up. The other one had MI after a severe secondary polycythemia, with very high Htc, treated with medical therapy. MI and sudden death are more common in older patients with polycythemia and underlying coronary artery disease. Nimit et al. described a case of a 25-year-old patient with spontaneous aortic thrombosis causing a left main coronary occlusion after secondary polycythemia. In neonates it is undescribed case. Both children are still alive: the first after 46-month follow-up, the second after 2-month follow-up. (Figure presente).
