N2  - The cause and treatment of bipolar disorder (BD) are not agreed on. Inheritance appears polygenic, with evidence of neurotransmission imbalance, disease progression, and neuroinflammation [1-3]. Recent rat studies suggest that proven effective BD drugs commonly target the brain arachidonic acid (AA) cascade. Thus, chronic administration of lithium, carbamazepine, valproate, or lamotrigine to rats downregulated AA turnover in brain phospholipids, prostaglandin E2 formation, and expression of AA cascade enzymes, including cytosolic phospholipase A2, cyclooxygenase-2 and acyl-CoA synthetase-4. Changes were selective for AA, since docosahexaenoic or palmitic acid metabolism was unaffected; topiramate, ineffective in BD, did not change AA metabolism. Downregulation of the AA cascade by mood stabilizers corresponded to inhibition of AA neurotransmission via dopaminergic D2-like and glutamatergic NMDA receptors in rats. Chronic atypical antipsychotic olanzapine and clozapine reduced brain AA metabolism to some extent, by reducing plasma AA availability through their liver effect. Antidepressants that increase switching of BP depression to mania (fluoxetine, imipramine) upregulated rat brain AA metabolism; non-switching bupropion did not. These observations suggest that the brain AA cascade is a common target of effective anti-BD drugs, and that bipolar symptoms, particularly mania, are associated with an upregulated cascade and excessive AA signaling via D2-like and NMDA receptors. The observations are consistent with increased expression of AA metabolizing enzymes and markers of neuroinflammation/ excitotoxicity, and downregulated synaptic markers, in postmortem BD frontal cortex, and efficacy of chronic aspirin as adjunctive therapy to lithium in a pharmaco-epidemiological study.
