N2  - Purpose: Stress has been recognized as a risk factor for cardiovascular disease and depression. The correlation between them is not well understood however, inflammation has a crucial role. Since tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-alpha is a major cytokine in the activation of neuroendocrine, immune and behavioral responses; we aimed to explore the effects of TNF-alpha inhibition on endothelium-dependent vascular reactivity, blood pressure and e NOS immunoreactivity in unpredictable chronic mild stress (UCMS) model of depression in rats. Methods: Animals were divided to the following study groups: Control animals not exposed to UCMS, animals exposed to UCMS, etanercept (a TNF inhibitor) treated group (0.8 mg/kg, weekly, during 8 weeks) while exposed to UCMS. UCMS groups with or without treatment were subjected to different types of stressors (restraint for 4 h, cage tilting for 24 h, wet bedding for 24 h, swimming in 4°C cold water for 5 min, swimming in 45°C hot water for 5 min, pairing with another stressed animal for 48 h, level shaking for 10 min, nip tail for 1 min and inversion of the light/dark cycle for 24 h. These nine stressors were randomly applied for 56 days, and each stressor was applied 6-7 times during this time period. Rats received one of these stressors per day and the same stressor was not applied for 2 days so that the animals could not predict the occurrence of stimulation. The stress procedure did not involve any food or water deprivation. The control groups receiving no stress had free access to food and water. At the end of the 8-week period of UCMS, systolic blood pressure was recorded by tail cuff method, the aortas of the rats were removed and placed in the organ chambers for isometric tension measurements. Also, some part of aortas were fixed to 10% neutral buffered formalin after the routine tissue tracking phases by taking 3 mm sections, cells in the aorta were examined with e NOS antibodies immunohistochemically. All data were expressed as a mean value ± Standard error of the mean. The contractile force was expressed as milligrams of developed tension. The relaxant effects of agonists were expressed as a percentage of the precontraction to phenylephrine. Significance was tested by one-way analyses of variance (ANOVA) with a posthoc Tukey's-Kramer test. Probabilities of less than 5% (P <0.05) were considered significant. Summary: UCMS led to decreased vascular reactivity demonstrated by a vasorelaxation response to carbachol; and sodium nitroprusside-induced relaxation did not changed in stressed animals; etanercept treatment improved carbachol-induced endothelium dependent relaxations seen in UCMS. Also, no significant change was observed in the systemic blood pressure of animals between all groups and e NOS immunoreactivity decreased in UCMS group, whereas there were no significant change in etanercept treatment group. Conclusions: UCMS may impair endothelium dependent relaxation of vascular smooth muscle. The impaired relaxations of vascular smooth muscle can be markedly improved by administration of etanercept. This observation strongly supports the concept that decreased production and or availability of nitric oxide contribute to vascular dysfunction in UCMS.
