N2  - Background: Immune activation induces pro-inflammatory cytokines which enhance the tryptophan (TRP) degradation into kynurenine (KYN) and imbalance in kynurenine. This mechanism has been reported to be involved in pathophysiology of major depression or depressive episodes after treatment with proinflammatory cytokines. It is hypothesized that this imbalance in kynurenines occurs in the immune cells in the brain, such as astrocytes and microglia and induces depression. Aim: To understand the mechanisms of antidepressants in relation to immune activation and TRP metabolites changes, the effects of different antidepressants on immunologically challenged mouse astrocytes cultures were investigated. Methods: The astrocytes from the 2-day old C57BL/6JRccHsd mice brains were cultured with medium alone or with interferon-g (IFN-γ), a pro-inflammatory cytokine which activates the immune system; and with and without different antidepressants (fluoxetine, reboxetine, venlafaxine, imipramine) or anti-inflammatory celecoxib for 72 hours at 37°C. TRP metabolites in the culture supernatants were analyzed using high performance liquid chromatography. Cytokines and signaling pathways were analyzed using commercial ELISA kits from USCN and Abnova. Results: When medications were added to baseline culture without immune stimulation, only unclear disturbance of the baseline level was observed on nuclear factor-κB (NFκB) expression. With IFN-γ there is high expression of NFκB which is suppressed by all antidepressants and celecoxib. IFN-γ stimulation resulted in increased TNF-α levels and there was no reversal effect of medications. In contrast, all drugs resulted in a significantly decreased secretion of pro-inflammatory cytokine Interleukin-1b (P = 0.024). Without IFN-γ challenge, medications reduced TRP except fluoxetine. In terms of KYN there were unclear fluctuations without stimulation. In contrast, without immune activation, the antidepressants increased kynurenic acid (KYNA) and reduced 3-hydroxykynurenine (3-HK). IFN-γ induced a strong breakdown of TRP. However, KYN level itself was not increased. Most probably, it was further metabolized to KYNA and 3-HK since both metabolites showed increase after the stimulation. The reversal effects of drug treatments were observed only in terms of TRP, KYN and 3-HK. Conclusions: The results of this study indicated that antidepressants have some anti-inflammatory effects as observed in anti-inflammatory compound celecoxib. Moreover, antidepressants change KYNA and 3-HK levels when there is no immune challenge. In case of IFN-g stimulation TRP levels were reduced since IFN-g activated the enzyme Indoleamine-2,3-dioxygenase. Antidepressants could reverse the effect on TRP. KYN levels were also reduced. This could be explained by the fact that IFN-g activated enzyme kynurenine-3-monoxygenase leading to high 3-HK level. It was also due to further degradation to KYNA which was indicated by increase in KYNA level. In terms of 3-HK, all antidepressants showed partial reversal effect. These antiinflammatory effects and effects on kynurenine pathway might be one of the mechanisms of those antidepressants. In addition it can be concluded that unnecessary treatment of culture without immune challenge with medications can disturb the basal physiological processes as it was observed in NFúB (P = 0.027).
