N2  - Purpose: This article is to present a case with burning mouth syndrome treated with quetiapine. Methods: A 64-year-old, right handed Korean female patient was evaluated by Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-IV Axis I Disorder (SCID-I), Hamilton Depression Rating Scale, Hamilton Anxiety Rating Scale and Beck Depression Inventory tests. She had magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain and gastrofiberscope. Her blood work was included complete blood count (CBC), erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), liver function test (LFT), hepatitis B surface antigen and antibody (HBsAg and Ab), Electrlyte, folic acid, vitamine B12, random glucose level, thyroid hormone level (T3, T4), thyroid stimulating hormone (TSH). Result: This patient with 2 years history of primary insomnia, presented in 2011.11.8 with painful and burning sensation of mouth which was very disturbing for her. She woke up without pain and noted increasing symptoms throughout the day and into the evening. She was consulted with Department of dentistry, gastroentorology, ENT, neurology, dermatology and gynecology; and no organic cause was found. Endoscopic evaluation of the gastrointestinal tract presented mild gastritis only. CT of brain and EEG results were within normal limits. She was examined in psychiatry inpatient clinic by psychiatrist. She only met the criteria for somatoform disorder not otherwise specified in DSM-IV by CIDI. Her distressful life events (cerebral infarction of her husband, poor economic state, marital dysharmony with her husband for a long time) were noticed during psychiatric examination. Her Hamilton depression and anxiety scores were 15 and 18, subsequently. Symptoms were unresponsive to Nortriptyline (Sensival) 70 mg, NSAID (Ibuprofen) 600 mg, Alprazolam (Xanax) 1.5 mg and then also Risperidone 2 mg-3 mg, SSRI (Prozac 20 mg, Lexapro 10 mg), but the administration of Quetiapine (Seroquel) 400 mg (200 mg to 400 mg) significantly reduced oral burning sensation. The present case illustrates the effectiveness of quetiapine as a treatment of burning mouth syndrome. This case is noteworthy because the patient was refractory to benzodiazepine and tricyclic antidepressant, SSRI, risperidone therapy. The patient responded to a medication currently indicated only for psychosis in schizophrenia and acute mania in bipolar disorder. Conclusion: Burning mouth syndrome (BMS) is a disorder characterized by a burning sensation of the oral cavity in the absence of local or systemic abnormalities. In the literature a variety of terms are used to describe similar symptoms, such as glossodynia, glossopyrosis, stomatodynia and oral dysestesia. Depression, anxiety disorder, hypochondriasis and conversion disorder may be considered among the psychiatric disorders responsible for this phenomenon. BMS predominantly affects women in the fourth and fifth decades of life, with an overall female-male ratio of 7:1. A variety of causes have been proposed for BMS including oral mucosa disease, menopause, medication and psychosomatic disorders. There is still no single effective treatment regimen for BMS. In this article, we report our experience with quetiapine as a beneficial therpy. This is the first reported case treated with quetiapine of the symptoms of glossodynia (burning mouth syndrome).
