N2  - Purpose of the study: Immune activation induces proinflammatory state or cytokines which enhance the tryptophan (TRP) degradation into kynurenine (KYN) and then KYN in 3-hydroxykynurenine (3-HK). It is hypothesized that increased TRP breakdown occur in the immune cells in the brain such as astrocytes and microglia and this induces decreased serotonin synthesis and impaired the balances between different tryptophan downstream pathway metabolites, 3-hydroxykinurenine and kynurenic acid (KYNA). This mechanism, KYN-pathway imbalance, has been reported to be involved in pathophisiology of major depression or depressive episodes after treatment with proinflammatory cytokine such as interferon-a, and also in anxiety like behavior in rodents. Here, the effects of different drugs whit anxiolytic action (benzodiazepines: lorazepam and diazepam; antidepressant compounds: imipramine, escitalopram venlafaxine; and one antiepileptic: pregabalin) and anti-inflammatory compounds (celecoxib and aspirin) on immunologically challenged mouse astrocytes cultures were investigated. Methods: The brains of 2-day old C57BL/6JRccHsd mice were dissected and collected in the RPMI medium. The brain tissue was homogenized and seeded into culture flasks. On day 12 the microglia were removed by shaking the culture flasks at 220 rpm for two hours. Then the astrocytes were cultured (approximately 60,000 cells/well in one ml RPMI culture medium alone or with LPS (bacterial toxin) or PolyI:C (viral mimetic) and with and without different anxiolytic (lorazepam, diazepam, imipramine, escitalopram, venlafaxine, pregabalin) and anti-inflammatory (celecoxib, asprin) compounds for 72 hours at 37°C. TRP metabolites in the culture supernatants were analyzed using UPLC whit XBridge C18 (Waters) column, and florescent detector for TRP and KYN and UV for 3HK and KYNA. Results: Treatment with diazepam and celecoxib could be beneficial to tryptophan availability in case of LPS challenge. Treatment whit diazepam, celecoxib and aspirin could reduce tryptophan breakdown in astrocites culture stimulated with LPS. Challenge with LPS could induce somewhat decrease in kynurenic acid (KYNA) and increased in 3-hydroxykynurenine (3HK) and obvious increased in 3HK/KYN ratios. Reduction in KYNA seemed to be well corrected by diazepam whereas none of these compounds could correct the increase in 3HK. Therefore, the 3HK/KYN ratio is also increased. Conclusions: We found increased tryptophan breakdown and decrease of KYNA after LPS challenge, we could demonstrate the imbalance in the downstream kynurenines after challenge with LPS which might be due to faster degradation of kynurenines into 3HK. This increase of neurotoxic compound after LPS challenge could not be corrected by any of the tested anxiolytic or antiinflammatory compounds. However, tryptophan breakdown was reduced by diazepam, celecoxib and aspirin. Diazepam and celecoxib could also prevent the reduction of tryptophan levels by LPS. Moreover diazepam and venlafaxine could increase KYNA levels. The results suggest that the anxiolitic effect of diazepam might be partly due to the effect through kinurenine pathway, and that celecoxib and aspirin could be beneficial in case of inflammatory state induced anxiety.
