N2  - Purpose: Delirium is a neuropsychiatric syndrome with core features of acute onset and fluctuating course, attentional deficits and generalized severe disorganization of behavior [1]. It is known that delirium is common among elderly patients and is associated with poor clinical outcomes, including prolonged hospital stay time and mortality [2]. As a number of elderly patients have been increasing in emergency department, the prevalence of delirium has been on the rise. However, delirium has been likely to be under-recognized by emergency medical teams [3]. Our study was designed to evaluate the frequency, the clinical effects and risk factors of delirium of hospitalized elderly patients in emergency department. Methods: We retrospectively reviewed the 414 cases of elderly patients admitted to emergency department and stayed over 24 hours between January 2008 and December 2009. We examined the frequency and assessed the clinical effects and risk factors. The clinical effects were assessed by hospital stay time and mortality. The candidates of risk factors included personal history (age, sex, alcohol abuse, smoking), transfer from nursing home, cognitive disorder (dementia, depression), neurologic disorder (stroke), medical condition (malnutrition, dehydration, infection, hypoxia, metabolic derangements, electrolyte imbalance, hemodynamic instability, visual impairment, hearing loss), drugs (3 or more medications, anesthetics, analgesics, sedatives, antibiotics, anticholinergics, antihistamines, bronchodilators, cardiac medications, diuretics, steroids). Each of the candidate risk factors were examined using independent sample t-test and Pearson's chisquare test. Next, multivariate logistic regression was developed to identify independent risk. The candidate variables which have statistical significance were included to logistic regression analysis and the results were expressed with odds ratio and 95% confidence interval. Results: A total of 42 patients (10.1%) developed delirium in emergency department over 12 month period studied. Among the candidate variables, male, dementia, depression, stroke, malnutrition, dehydration, hypoxia, metabolic derangements, electrolyte imbalance, hemodynamic instability, 3 or more medications, analgesics, sedatives, antibiotics, bronchodilators, cardiac medications, and diuretics were risk factors for delirium (p<0.05). Independent risk factors of delirium by logistic regression model were stroke (4.78, 2.09-11.00, P<0.01), metabolic derangements (3.87, 1.76- 8.51, p<0.01), dementia (3.65, 1.27-10.50, p = 0.01), hemodynamic instability (3.34, 1.43-7.81, p<0.01), depression (2.62, 1.07-6.40, p<0.01). The development of delirium in emergency department was associated with significant poor outcomes including increased hospital mortality (19.0% vs. 8.1%, p = 0.04) and prolonged hospital stay time (+3.5 days, p <0.01). Conclusion: Deliriums were prevalent, despite it is difficult to recognize delirium because of distractibility and short observation time in emergency environment. Since the risk factors of delirium are very common in emergency department, emergency department physician should know the risk factors and effect to clinical outcome of delirium, and try to recognize delirium earlier.
