N2  - Monoaminergic, glutamatergic and GABAergic pathways and hypothalamus-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis overactivity have been implicated in the development of mood disorders. However, there is renewed interest in, and increasing evidence supporting, the role of several other pathways and their interconnectedness in the pathophysiology of bipolar disorder and major depressive disorder (MDD) (e.g., inflammatory pathways, neurogenesis and oxidative stress). Of particular note, elevated levels of pro-inflammatory cytokines and prostaglandin (PG) E2 in the blood and cerebrospinal fluid have been observed in patients with MDD. Pro-inflammatory cytokines stimulate the HPA axis and increase cortisol levels, thereby desensitising central glucocorticoid receptors and reducing the negative feedback control of cortisol secretion [1]. Moreover, pro-inflammatory cytokines and PGE2 are responsible for neuronal damage and normal repair mechanisms involving brainderived neurotrophic factor that are inhibited by elevated cortisol levels. Furthermore, patients with MDD have elevated serum levels of potent neurotoxins (3-hydroxykynurenine and quinolinic acid) that are end products of the kynurenine pathway induced by pro-inflammatory cytokines. N-Acetylcysteine (NAC) is a precursor of glutathione, which is the brain's predominant free radical scavenger and recently interest has been expressed in its utility for treating bipolar I or II depression [2]. The putative antidepressant effects of NAC may be exerted via its effects on glutamate neurotransmission, neurogenesis and inflammation. Increased knowledge of the brain-body interconnection and how immune and endocrine systems affect the brain has led to a better understanding of the impact of these systems on neuronal networks affected in mood disorders. This may, in turn, herald future pharmacotherapeutic vistas.
