N2  - Recommended treatment options for bipolar mania include lithium, anticonvulsants and atypical antipsychotics; however, there are clear differences in their evidence base for bipolar depression, the predominant mood symptom of the disorder [1]. Among recommended agents, a robust evidence base supports the efficacy of quetiapine and quetiapine XR in not only acute bipolar mania but also acute bipolar I or II depression and maintenance treatment. There is a paucity of well-controlled data to support the use of monoamine-based antidepressants (SSRIs and SNRIs) in treating bipolar depression despite their recommended first-line use in patients with major depressive disorder (MDD). Potential limitations to SSRI or SNRI use in MDD include inadequate response, induction of mood switch, slow onset of action and tolerability concerns, particularly with long-term use. Thus, the available armamentarium of treatments for mood disorders is suboptimal and new pharmacotherapeutic options are needed. Several novel mechanisms/pathways are emerging that may yield potential antidepressant options in patients with MDD including multimodal serotonergic agents, triple uptake inhibitors, neurokinin-based agents, glutamatergic agents, nicotinic receptor-based therapies, neurogenesis-based agents and antiglucocorticoid treatments [2]. Some of these pathways are also being investigated using existing drugs from other indications that are repurposed for antidepressant use in mood disorders (e.g., ketamine [NMDA receptor antagonist], ceftriaxone [enhanced glutamate uptake], varenicline [nicotinic receptor partial agonist], mifepristone [glucocorticoid receptor antagonist] and infliximab [anti-TNF]). It is hoped that ongoing and planned research into novel and repurposed drugs will yield effective future pharmacotherapies, forming part of an individualised, targeted-treatment strategy in patients with mood disorders.
