N2  - Purpose: Determine the prevalence, clinical characteristics, and treatment of residents with a diagnosis of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) in nursing homes and examine the relationship between medication use and shortness of breath (SOB) in a cohort of cognitively impaired (CI) residents. Methods: Deidentified Minimum Data Set (MDS) 2.0 and prescription data for residents with a COPD diagnosis were linked and analyzed. Exacerbations were defined by a proxy of a short course of respiratory antibiotics and/or corticosteroids. Descriptive statistics were performed. Results: The prevalence of COPD was 21.5%. Approximately 18% of residents were ≥ 85 years; 14% were < 65 years of age. Common comorbidities included depression (50%), congestive heart failure (38%), dementia (37%), anemia (31%), and anxiety (23%). Moderate to very severe CI was present in 42% of residents; 33% had SOB. Use of inhaled short-acting agents was very common (> 80%); about 60% received long-acting agents. Use of nebulized short-acting agents was also frequent; nebulized long-acting agents were used minimally. Exacerbations were noted in 20% of residents. Use of handheld devices to deliver short-acting beta-agonists (SABA) and long-acting beta-agonists (LABA) was common in these residents (∼50%). Nebulized LABA use was minimal. Inhaled SABA monotherapy was received by 40.9% of CI residents; however, those who received LABA monotherapy had a 56.4% lower incidence of SOB. Conclusion: The use of SABA monotherapy is highly prevalent in nursing home residents with CI, who frequently exhibit SOB. Improvement in the use of long-acting bronchodilators would better align therapy with established guidelines and may improve SOB and reduce exacerbations in this population.
