N2  - Objectives: Health-related quality of life (HRQoL) as a patient-reported outcome in rheumatoid arthritis (RA) has become an important tool in the clinical evaluation of patient preferences and perceptions of individual health. The objective of this study was to compare the validity of the HRQoL with respect to depression in RA patients treated with infliximab, etanercept, adalimumab or tocilizumab. Methods: One hundred seven RA patients (infliximab, 47 patients; etanercept, 17 patients; adalimumab, 25 patients; tocilizumab, 18 patients) were assessed prior to treatment and after 30 weeks therapy initiation. The age, gender, and dosage of steroid were obtained for each participant and the HRQoL questionnaire [modified health assessment questionnaire (mHAQ) and Short Form (SF)-36] and depression scale [Japanese version of the structured interview guide for the Hamilton Depression rating scale (HAM-D) and the Self-rating Depression Scale (SDS)] were administered to patients. The primary endpoint was the assessment of clinical improvements, and the secondary endpoints were assessments of the changes in the HRQoL and depression scale. Results: After 30 weeks of tocilizmab administration, score of DAS28 was lower than adalimumab treatment (P < 0.05). There were no significant difference in the percentage of low disease activity and remission. Although no significant differences were found for the HAMD and SDS score, mHAQ, delta role physical, body pain, vitality, social function, role emotion and mental health of SF-36 were significant difference between four groups. Particularly, etanercept treatment was significantly improvement of HRQoL as compared with adalimumab and tocilizumab therapy. On the other hand, there were no significant differences of age, gender, dosage of steroid, DAS28 (before treatment) and physical function and general health of SF-36. ConclusionsIn this study, etanercept treatment was significantly improved as compared with other drugs in many items of the HRQoL evaluation without relation to the efficacy of drug. Although factor of mental health in SF-36 was improved, HAM-D and SDS were not improved between four groups.
