N2  - Ethanol, as other drugs of abuse, stimulates the dopamine (DA) neurons in the posterior ventral tegmental area (VTA). However, this augment of the VTA DA neurons activity seems to be the result of a balance between activating and depressant effects that simultaneously occurs after ethanol administration. In a normal condition (i.e. without any pharmacological intervention), the net effect observed is the activation of the DA neurons. However, the use of adequate pharmacological strategies can help us to uncover the existence of these to opposite tendencies. Recent studies have provided strong evidences about this duality in the effects of ethanol. Very recent published data on this topic show that acetaldehyde (ACD) or its derivative, salsolinol, could be the responsible for the activation of the VTA DA neurons observed after acute administration of ethanol whereas the non-metabolized fraction of ethanol could account for the reduction of the activity of these neurons. In the present study, we explore the possible implication of the γ-aminobutyric acid (GABA) receptors, in particular the subtype A, in the depressant effects elicited by the nonbiotransformed fraction of ethanol. To do that, we evaluate the effect of the GABAA receptor antagonist bicuculline (0.0175 nmol) on the motor depression elicited by the intra-VTA injection of 35 nmol of ethanol in rats pre-treated with 50 mg/kg ip of D-Penicillamine (DP), an ACD sequestering agent. DP was ip injected, as in our previous studies, 30 min prior to intra-VTA ethanol microinjections. Bicuculline microinjections in the VTA were performed 5 min prior to ethanol administration. After ethanol injection, the spontaneous activity of rats (a behavioral index of the activity of the VTA DA neurons) was monitored for 20 min. Results show that the blockade of the GABAA receptors with bicuculline prevents the motor depression evoked by the ethanol microinjection. These data support (i) the importance of the brain-generated ACD in the stimulant effects of ethanol (ii) the involvement of the non-biotransformed fraction of the ethanol in the depressant effects observed when ACD is sequestered, and (iii) the implication of the GABAA receptors as the target through which this fraction of ethanol could act reducing the VTA DA neurons activity.
