N2  - Objectives: Tryptophan is metabolized to several bioactive molecules, including serotonin, kynurenine and kynurenic acid. Altered levels of these various metabolites have been implicated in organic CNS degenerative disease, depression, and inflammation. Our objective is to validate a method of quantifying these analytes in plasma research samples. Design: Validation of an ultra-performance liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry (UPLC-MS/MS) method according to Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute protocols. Methods: Samples/standards are deproteinized with methanol (containing deuterated internal standards) and injected without derivatization. UPLC (Waters Acquity UPLC HSS T3 column) with initial conditions of 70:30 (0.12% formic acid in H2O:100% methanol) and a linear gradient to 30:70 in 2.75 min at 250 μL/min is performed. Detection is by a Waters Acquity MS/MS in multiple reaction monitoring mode. Results: Limit of quantitation (LOQ), imprecision (total), and linearity summary data are shown in the table. Reference interval data was not generated as this will be application specific. (Table presented) Conclusions: In the present study, a sensitive and rapid UPLC-MS/MS method for the determination of tryptophan and its metabolites (serotonin, kynurenine and kynurenic acid) in plasma samples has been developed and validated. This method is suitable for research uses that include appropriate control subjects.
