N2  - Meningococcemia evolves rapidly into shock, adrenal insufficiency, DIC, and purpura fulminans. Below described is a case of fulminant meningococcemia with severe myo-pericarditis that promptly responded to treatment. A literature review from 1947 to 2011, with over 137 cases of meningococcal myocarditis and/or pericarditis will also be detailed. A 31 year old male, with no PMH, was admitted for altered mental status, fever, headaches, weakness and vomiting. Patient was febrile, tachycardic, tachypneic, hypotensive, and responsive only to painful stimuli. He had petechiae, a positive Kernig's and Brudzinsky's sign. EKG showed diffuse convexe upward ST elevations, and his troponins were at 40 ng/ml. Head CT was unremarkable. LP had an opening pressure exceeding 40 cmH2O, WBC of 5040 with 90% neutrophils, glucose of 23 mg/dl, and proteins greater than 300 mg/dl. CSF and blood cultures were positive for Neisseria meningitides W135 antigen. Echocardiogram showed no tamponade, however an EF of 40%. Antibiotics were started empirically then deascalated to ceftriaxone after sensitivity results. He markedly improved in 24 hours with resolving EKG changes, and normalizing troponin levels and EF. Extrameningeal/systemic manifestations of meningococcemia such as pneumonia, pleuritis, episcleritis, sinusitis, arthritis and pericarditis has an incidence of 17 to 19%. Pericarditis itself accounts for one third of these manifestations. Severe meningococcal infection has been associated with acute interstitial myocarditis, endocarditis, pericarditis, myocardial infarction and heart failure with increased levels of interleukin-6 as a mediator of myocardial depression. Evidence of myocardial dysfunction on serial echocardiograms predicts worse outcome. Meningococcal pericarditis can be classified into three categories: a local manifestation of disseminated meningococcal disease (DMP), an immunoreactive pericarditis (IRP), and a primary meningococcal pericarditis (PMP). The meningococci causing PMP are usually of serotype C, while the serotype causing DMP is usually W 135. PMP, defined as purulent pericarditis without disseminated meningococcemia is rare and difficult to diagnose. It rapidly progress into cardiac tamponade requiring drainage. IRP have a late immune-complex etiology. It occurs 4 to 10 days after systemic disease and is associated with recrudescence of fever and leukocytosis. Treatment is based on s teroids and NSAIDS. On the other hand, management of meningococcemia relies on early and aggressive antimicrobial and supportive treatment. Some cases have a resistant prolonged post-meningococcal inflammatory syndrome, so a long term follow-up is warranted. Intensivisit should be familiar with myo-pericarditis as serious complications of meningococcal infections. A differenciation between primary, diffuse or late reactive pericarditis is warranted, as treatment noticeably diverge. (Figure Presented).
