N2  - Multiple myeloma is a systemic disease that seldom affects the lung. Pulmonary manifestations include opportunistic infections, direct plasma cell infiltration and toxicities related to therapy. We describe a novel manifestation. A 69 year-old woman with a past medical history of depression and schizophrenia presented to the hospital with confusion and dehydration. There were bi-basilar rales on chest examination. Chest radiograph (CXR) was unremarkable. Admission labs revealed: elevated creatinine (6.4 mg/dL); hypercalcemia (11.7 mg/dL); elevated total protein and low albumin (8.5 and 2.9 g/dL); normocytic normochromic anemia (hemoglobin 7.7 g/dL). Bone marrow biopsy confirmed multiple myeloma with 40% monoclonal, kappa light chain-restricted plasma cell infiltration. Serum beta-2-microglobulin was 14.2 mg/L with serum immunofixation IgG of 6030 mg/dL. Pulse dose dexamethasone therapy was initiated. After her third cycle of dexamethasone, she developed fever of 102.2 F, hypoxia, non-productive cough, and wheezing. CXR demonstrated progressive bilateral patchy infiltrates. Computer-tomography of the chest revealed diffuse ground glass opacities with areas of consolidation in the right middle and upper lobes. The patient was started on empiric broad spectrum antibacterial and fungal coverage and required non-invasive ventilatory support. Multiple respiratory and blood cultures revealed no growth. She failed to improve and underwent open lung biopsy of the right middle and upper lobes. Pathologic examination of the lung parenchyma showed diffuse patchy intra-alveolar macrophages with accumulation of round eosinophilic globules. Stains for bacteria, fungus and amyloid were negative. Electron microscopy revealed amorphous globular structures within the cytoplasm of histiocytes without fine ultrastructure of lattice or linear parallel configuration. Immunohistochemical stain for κ and λ light chains showed background staining. Polyclonal plasma cells were confirmed by immunohistochemistry and in-situ hybridization. Macrophage globules were strongly positive for κ light chain and weakly positive for λ light chain. Repeat review of the bone marrow biopsy showed similar eosinophilic globules in macrophages adjacent to areas of monoclonal plasma cells. The clinical and pathologic manifestations were characteristic of non-crystalline histiocytosis associated with multiple myeloma. High dose steroid therapy for multiple myeloma was continued and antibiotics were deescalated. Her radiographic findings and hypoxia improved. Further outpatient chemotherapy was planned. We describe a case of non-crystalline histiocytosis associated with multiple myeloma. Crystal-storing histiocytosis is an extremely rare manifestation of lymphoprolierative disease. Only one other case of pulmonary non-crystalline histiocytosis complicating multiple myeloma is described in the literature. Early open lung biopsy should be considered in patients with multiple myeloma and non-resolving pulmonary infiltrates.
