N2  - Objective: Serotonin syndrome(SS) is a spectrum of autonomic dysfunction,mental status changes and neuromuscular abnormalities. Through our case series we illustrate the varied clinical manifestations and etiologies of this disorder. We report a rare case of autopsy findings in SS. Background: Serotonin syndrome is a predictable drug reaction resulting from excess serotonergic agonism of central nervous system and peripheral serotonin receptors following the use of serotomimetic agents. It is characterised by mental status change, agitation, incoordintaion, myoclonus, hyperreflexia, diaphoresis, shivering, tremor, diarrhoea or fever. Methods: A retrospective chart review of 3 patients who developed SS in our institution was undertaken, focusing on aetiology, treatement and in once case, autopsy findings. Results: Case 1: 35yo F with depression. Medication: Sodium Valporate, Phenelzine. Took over the counter cough syrup (containing phenylephrine hydrochloride) for a cold. 3 days later found comatosed with floppy arms and rigid legs which exhibited rhythmic jerking movements. She was flushed, dry and hypotensive. Treated with fluids and pressor support-recovered over 4 weeks. Case 2:69yo F with idiopathic Parkinson's disease. Patient taking own supply of venlafaxine-not noted. Prescribed amitryptiline for insomnia on day 3. Next day: pyrexia, flusheing, rigidity, confusion, elevated CPK, renal impairment and hypotension. responded to rehydration, removal of the tricyclic antidepressant and Venlafaxine and the addition of Bromocriptine and Cyproheptadine. encephalopathy slowly resolved over 6 months. Case 3: 57yo F with depression presented to ED with pyrexia, decreased level of consciousness and lower limb rigidity and shaking. Seized in ED and began bleeding from nose and mouth. Diagnosed with SS due to interaction of duloxetine and amitryptiline complicated by encephalopathy, DIC, rhabdomyolysis and multiorgan failure. Prolonged ICU stay-succumbed to ventilator associated pneumonia. Autopsy performed. Conclusions: The increasing use of serotomimetic agents is likely to result in increasing numbers of patients developing SS. The subtle signs and symptoms of mild cases and general ignorance of the medical profession as to its existence contributes to its delayed or even misdiagnosis. One must keep SS in mind when using not only SSRI's and TCA's but also opioids, antibiotics, anti-retrovirals, anaesthetics and methylene blue.
