N2  - Introduction: Invasive fungal infection (IFI) is a serious and common complication in hematological patients. The incidence of IFI correlates with the severity of immunosuppression, which in turn is associated with the intensity of treatment. Case: An eighteen-year-old boy with a severe refractory cytopenia and concomitant trisomy 8 underwent a Bone Marrow Transplant. Conditioning chemotherapy included Thiotepa, Fludarabine, Treosulfan and antithymocyte globulin (ATG), followed by a 3 × 108 nucleated cells Kg-1 infusion. Only Cyclosporin-A was used for Graft Versus Host Disease (GVHD) prophylaxis. At day +13, a complete engraftment was observed but a grade I GVHD occurred. At day +16, the patient developed a severe acute hepatic, intestinal and cutaneous GVHD, treated with steroids, and, at day +19, with infliximab, followed by basiliximab, mycophenolate mofetil and budesonide. At day +31, after the onset of massive gastrointestinal haemorrhage, the boy was treated with an ATG cycle and with two mesenchymal stem cells infusions with no benefits. Intestinal haemorrhage resolved with thalidomide. At day +60, the clinical situation was complicated by a Candida albicans sepsis, resolved with anidulafungin. At day +70, the patient experienced an isolated episode of depression with normal EEG. However, the symptoms of depression were exacerbated and a brain Computer Tomography scan showed a left parietal lesion. The boy went into coma with GCS 7-8 and a decerebrate posture. A brain and brainstem Magnetic Resonance Imaging showed multiple rounded lesions compatible with fungal localizations. High levels of ß-D-glucan and mannan were documented and a systemic diffusion of Candida was observed. Patient was treated with liposomal amphotericin in high doses, with voriconazole in combination with anidulafungin and with posaconazole without any effect. The antibiogram performed showed total resistance to azoles and to polyenes, and a high MIC for echinocandins. The patient later died. Conclusion: IFI carries great morbidity and mortality and the casefatality rate exceeds 50% in cases. Echinocandins are now being used increasingly as therapy for invasive candidiasis but reports of in vitro resistance and loss of echinocandin activity following prolonged use have been observed. However, the hematologic malignancy, severity of immunosuppression, and the presence of indwelling devices probably represent determinant factors for the poor outcome more than any antifungal therapy.
