N2  - Background: Patients with Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) have frequent psychiatric comorbidity, such as anxiety and depression. Animal studies have shown that mild to moderate gut inflammation can trigger anxiety-like behavior, which is normalized by anti- TNF treatment. Some IBD patients on infliximab treatment have anecdotally reported a feeling of euphoria within hours of receiving the infusion, before mucosal healing could occur. The aim of this study was to investigate whether infliximab infusion improves mood in patients with IBD and whether this correlates with changes in gut inflammation. Methods: 34 patients with IBD attending the McMaster University infusion clinic were invited to participate, 18 patients (11 male, Crohn's disease=15, ulcerative colitis=2, indeterminate colitis=1, mean age 31.5 yrs, mean duration of disease 14.5 yrs) were enrolled. Patients were examined and scored using the Modified Mayo Score, questionnaires assessing mood (HAD, STAI) and IBD activity (SIBDQ) were administered. Serum CRP and kynurenin/ tryptophan levels were assessed before (day 0), 24 hrs (day 1) and 7 days (day 7) after the infusion. Results: Most patients had mild anxiety/depression. Mean HAD score for anxiety decreased from day 0 to 1 from 7.5±4.4 to 5.65 ±4.3 (p=0.05) and remained lower at day 7 (6.12±4.61), while depression score did not change. Similarly, scores for STAI decreased significantly at day 1 and day 7 (47.06±17.67 vs 38.18±12.27 vs 36.00±12.58, p=0.04, and p=0.03, respectively). Mayo score, SIBDQ score and CRP levels were low and similar at day 0, 1 and 7. However, kynurenin levels slightly decreased at day 1 (from 772.77±267.82 to752.62 ±189.37). The decrease in kynurenin levels was more marked and statisitically significant when data was analysed excluding 3 patients who were on antidepressant treatment. Summary and Conclusions: Anxiety scores (HAD and STAI) decreased significantly at day 1 and remained lower at day 7 after infliximab infusion. This was not accompanied by changes in colitis and SIBDQ scores, or CRP levels, indicating that most patients' disease was in remission. However, kynurenin levels decreased in parallel with anxiety scores. We propose that infliximab treatment induces changes in mood likely via modulation of tryptophan metabolism and through neutralization of circulating cytokines that may influence CNS function. This effect should be considered as being independent from anti-inflammatory effects of infliximab on intestinal inflammation.
