N2  - Background: Omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFA) appear to have efficacy in treatment of depressive disorders. This antidepressant effect may involve dopaminergic functioning, which in rat models is profoundly affected by omega-3 deficiency. Less is known about PUFA-dopamine relationships in humans. One marker for dopaminergic functioning is plasma prolactin, since dopamine and prolactin operate in a homeostatic feedback loop. We examined plasma PUFA and prolactin levels in major depression. Methods: Medication-free adults in a major depressive episode (n=29) had psychiatric assessments; plasma levels of docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA), arachidonic acid (AA), and prolactin were measured. Plasma phospholipid levels as a percentage of total phospholipids (DHA%, EPA%, AA%), were entered separately as independent variables into a regression model with plasma prolactin levels as the outcome measure. Age, sex, smoking status, depression severity, and suicide attempter status were tested as covariates. Results: Prolactin levels correlated negatively with DHA% (F=16.8, R2=0.38, p<0.001) and EPA% (F=5.0, R2=0.16, p=0.034) but not AA% (F=0.061, R2=0.002, p=0.806); and positively with the AA/DHA ratio (F=14.9, R2=0.36, p=0.001). There were no effects of age, sex, smoking, depression severity, or suicide attempt history. Conclusions: Low omega-3 PUFAs were strongly associated with elevated prolactin in major depression. An explanatory model is hypothesized involving PUFA regulation of the dopaminergic system and how dysregulation may contribute to depression pathophysiology. (Graph Presented).
