N2  - Background: The cause and treatment of bipolar disorder (BD) are uncertain. Inheritance appears polygenic, with evidence of neurotransmission imbalance and disease progression. Patients often take multiple agents with incomplete efficacy. The “arachidonic acid (AA) cascade” hypothesis may explain actions of mood stabilizers and antidepressants in BD, and disease mechanisms. Methods: To test this hypothesis, we examined brain fatty acid kinetics and enzyme expression in rats treated chronically with mood stabilizers or antidepressants. Results: Chronic lithium, carbamazepine, valproate, or lamotrigine in rats downregulated AA turnover in brain phospholipids, prostaglandin E2 formation, and expression of AA cascade enzymes, including cytosolic phospholipase A2, cyclooxygenase-2 and/or AA-preferring acyl-CoA synthetase. Changes were selective for AA, since docosahexaenoic or palmitic acid metabolism was unaffected, and topiramate, ineffective in BD, did not modify brain AA metabolism. Downregulation of the AA cascade by the mood stabilizers corresponded to inhibition of AA neurotransmission via dopaminergic D2 or glutamatergic NMDA receptors. Unlike the mood stabilizers, antidepressants reported to increase switching of bipolar depression to mania upregulated the rat brain AA cascade. Conclusions: Observations suggest that the brain AA cascade is a common target of mood stabilizers, and that bipolar symptoms, particularly mania, are associated with an upregulated cascade and AA signaling via D2 and NMDA receptors. This suggestion agrees with evidence that expression of AA metabolizing enzymes and neuroinflammation/excitotoxicity markers are upregulated, while synaptic markers are downregulated, in postmortem BD frontal cortex; and that chronic aspirin reduced morbidity in patients taking lithium.
