N2  - Background: The use of isotretinoin is reserved due to its teratogenic effects and reported association with depression and suicide. Isotretinoin has also been tied to inflammatory bowel disease. In consideration of adverse effects (AE) and medicallegal risks, clinicians seem to exhaust other treatment modalities prior to initiating isotretinoin. Objective: The purpose of this descriptive study was to examine use of previous oral antibiotic (OA) regimens, patient attributes, and OA side effects among patients treated for acne with isotretinoin in an academic dermatology practice setting. Methods: Medical charts were reviewed from 200 patients treated with isotretinoin by the Saint Louis University Department of Dermatology (SLUDerm) between 2005- 2010. Results: 55% of subjects were female; 81.5% were white, 10% were black. 66.5% were between ages 14-25 years. 92% of patients reported improvement with isotretinoin. 55.5% of patients had recently been or were currently treated with OA upon presentation to SLUDerm, and 52.3% of these patients were treated again with OA before isotretinoin initiation. 73% of patients with no previous OA exposure were treated with OA by SLUDerm before isotretinoin (P <.001). 35.8% of all patients prescribed OA before isotretinoin by SLUDerm received ≥2 OAs. 75% of patients treated with ≥2 OAs were female. 20% of patients prescribed OA reported an AE: GI upset/gastric reflux (43%), vertigo/headache (21%), rash (18%). 45% of patients receiving ≥2OAs had an AE versus 6% of patients prescribed 1OA. Leading OA choices: minocycline (43%), doxycycline (25%), and trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (16%). The logistic regression model describing the association between prior OA use and direct start on isotretinoin revealed an OR of 2.5 (95% CI, 1.3-4.6). Gender was observed as an independent predictor of direct start on isotretinoin, OR: 2.4 (95% CI, 1.4-4.4). Males were 2.4 times more likely to receive direct start isotretinoin, while adjusting for age, prior OA use and ethnicity. Limitations: Small sample size, single site, retrospective design. Conclusion: Practitioners often postpone using a medicine that has a higher likelihood of success, especially in females. It is more likely for a female to be treated with ≥2 OA before isotretinoin initiation. Females are less often started directly on isotretinoin for acne. OA also carry a risk of side effects, some serious.
