N2  - Alitretinoin (9-cis retinoic acid) is the first systemic agent licensed to treat severe chronic hand eczema (CHE). The Scottish Medicines Consortium approved its use in February 2009, without the additional dermatology life quality index restrictions or ''stopping rules'' that were introduced later in guidance for prescribing in England and Wales. The aim of the study was to audit the use of alitretinoin in Scotland. All departments across the country were invited to record their experiences in a webbased retrospective questionnaire. One hundred completed patient records were available for analysis. The mean age was 51.7 years (range, 23-82 years). The main indication for alitretinoin was CHE (93%). Seven patients with alternative diagnoses were treated, including psoriasis (5), discoid eczema (1), and pompholyx eczema (1). Sixty-six patients had been patch-tested, with positive reactions in 43. Almost all patients had been treated with potent topical steroids (95%) and over half (57%) had been treated with phototherapy. Baseline bloods were checked in all patients: mean total cholesterol was 5.28 mmol/L and triglycerides 1.92 mmol/L. During treatment, elevated total cholesterol was noted in 33 patients, and raised triglycerides in 27 patients. Alitretinoin was felt to be responsible for around 60% of the lipid abnormalities. Six patients developed raised liver enzymes and two developed raised thyroid-stimulating hormone. Six patients described low mood; one of whom who had a documented background history of depression, and one patient with a history of bipolar affective disorder. In the total 100 patients analyzed, the response was described as either ''clear or almost clear'' in 49%, and improvement to mild or moderate was noted in 24%. Twenty-two percent of patients experienced no improvement. Thirty-eight patients had concomitant treatment, including 25 who had topical steroids and emollients; 4 who had oral steroids; 2 who had azathioprine; and 2 who received cyclosporin. This ongoing audit suggests that alitretinoin is being used in an appropriate manner in Scotland, with nearly half of all patients analyzed achieving either clear or almost clear hands. The therapy appears to be well-tolerated. It is unsurprisingly associated with lipid abnormalities. It may be useful to develop local protocols for the initiating and monitoring of this drug as we continue to develop experience and familiarity in using it in the United Kingdom.
