N2  - Trigeminal trophic syndrome (TTS) is a rare entity in which nasal ulceration occurs following sensory impairment in the trigeminal area. A 60-year-old man presented with persistent, asymptomatic ulcer over the right ala nasi for the past 1 month. The patient was a clinicohistopathologically diagnosed case of borderline tuberculoid leprosy on multibacillary multidrug therapy for the last 5 months. Three months back he started having paraesthesias over the right side of his nose and cheek. He indulged in self-picking at the nose because of the paraesthesias, which subsequently resulted in ulceration on the right side of nose. He denied any history of topical applications. Examination revealed a sickle-shaped, well defined, ulcer measuring 2 × 2.5 cm present over the right ala nasi with some extension on to the philtrum. Neurologic examination revealed decreased level of pain and thermal perception over the ophthalmic and maxillary divisions of the trigeminal area, as well as a diminution of corneal reflex. Biopsy revealed nonspecific changes. Antinuclear antibody (ANA) assay by indirect immunofluorescence and antineutrophilic cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA) test, and VDRL done on serum were negative. To rule out dermatitis artefacta, a psychiatry opinion was obtained according to which he was not suffering from anxiety and/or depression. Patient was followed-up every week with provision of protective gloves, topical antibiotics, and normal saline bandaging of the ulcer at each visit. The ulcer healed completely over a period of 2 months with a remnant scar In TTS, majority of the ulcers occur following destruction of the Gasserian ganglion after alcohol injection for trigeminal neuralgia or rhizotomy. Other neurologic associations include vertebrobasilar insufficiency, acoustic neuroma, and syringobulbia. Trigeminal nerve is probably the second most common cranial nerve to get involved in leprosy. To our knowledge, there is a single report in which Thomas et al described a case of selfinflicted nasal ala ulceration as a result of trigeminal nerve involvement in a case of leprosy. The management of trigeminal trophic syndrome is often difficult. Treatment comprises antidepressants and antipsychotics. Occlusive dressings and protective gloves can reduce trauma of the affected area.
