N2  - Background: Morphea en coup de sabre can produce permanent, disfiguring induration and depression over the affected area, leading patients to seek cosmetic improvement. Surgical repair has been a commonly employed method for correction, with narrow lesions treated with simple excision and primary closure. Wider lesions may require extensive reconstruction with a variety of flaps and/or use of implants or autologous dermal fat or bone transplantation. Because of the invasive nature of these procedures, the use of filler substances has generated interest as a treatment modality. Recent techniques have included injection of an allograft tissue matrix or autologous dermal fat or tissue cocktail injections.We present two cases of morphea en coup de sabre corrected with hyaluronic acid dermal filler. To our knowledge this is the first such report in the English literature. Methods: Two patients were referred for evaluation of morphea en coup de sabre. They each had stable, burnt-out disease and a depressed, linear plaque extending from the glabella to the frontal hairline. After review of available treatment options, hyaluronic acid filler injection was selected. The first patient, a Hispanic woman, was filled with 0.6 mL of JuvédermUltra. Patient two was a 25-year-old white woman with a>10-year histology of linear morphea. Her lesion was corrected with a total of 1 mL of Restylane-L. Injections were performed starting at the hairline and proceeding distally, using small volumes of hyaluronic acid (0.25 to 0.5 mL at a time) injected in the superficial to mid-dermis as the needle was withdrawn to minimize the risk of cutaneous necrosis. There were no complications of the procedure. Contour deformity was well corrected in both patients with good patient satisfaction. Patient 1 was seen in follow-up 6 months after injection and has maintained full correction. Conclusion: Hyaluronic acid dermal fillers have long been used for soft tissue augmentation. Attractive features of this filler material include low immunogenicity, ease of administration, and reversibility. In the case of morphea en coup de sabre, the use of hyaluronic acid is a simple, minimally invasive procedure that can provide contour restoration. It is a faster, less invasive treatment option compared with other commonly employed procedures. In the two cases presented here, correction was performed with no adverse effects and excellent patient satisfaction.
