N2  - More than other disorders, Bipolar disorder (BD) is subject to frequent use of experimental therapies. The main reasons are that: Either there is no, or we don't understand yet, a clear-cut neurobiology in BD which can be specifically targeted by a design medication. Traditionally, and starting with lithium, the usefulness of almost all BD medication was discovered accidentally or they were first used in other indications. Yet treatment outcomes, especially in bipolar depression and long term recovery, are still unsatisfactory and new approaches are needed In mania, experimental treatments include the use of atypical antipsychotics as clozapine, zotepine or amisulpride, which were not subjected to pivotal licensing studies, and thus are off-licence, but where we have reasonable trust that they work. More experimental studies target second messenger pathways with calcium antagonists or more specifically PK-C , e.g. with tamoxifen, as a new neurobiology-based approach In bipolar depression, only one medication (quetiapine) is specifically licensed in this indication; thus, any other treatment (mood stabilizer, antidepressants) is strictly speaking experimental. Newer approaches include experimental use of atypicals with some similarities to quetiapine, e.g.. asenapine, and targeting the glutamaergic-gabaergic system, e.g. by using N-acetyl-cysteine, D-cycloserine or NMDA-and mGlu receptor mixed antagonists. In prophylactic treatment, there is again a frequent off-label use of antidepressants and some antipsychotics, based on observations that short term efficacy often predicts also long term efficacy. More truly experimental and targeting a presumed neurobiology is, again, the use of calcium antagonists, N-acetyl cysteine, or 3 fatty acids. There are also specific symptoms as cognitive decline over time which is experimentally treated with substances lowering homocysteine, e.g. B-vitamine complex and folic acid, or antioxidants as vitamin C. Besides psychopharmacological treatment, there is also work ongoing to apply physical treatments useful in unipolar depression as VNS, DBS or rTMS in BD, and the same is true for different psychological techniques , but with often conflicting results.
