N2  - Background: It has been hypothesized that there may be depletion of plasma omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, particularly eicosapentaenoic acid and docosahexaenoic acid in depression. Aims: To study plasma omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acids in depressed subjects and normal controls. Method: Plasma omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acid levels of 30 depressive patients and 30 healthy matched control subjects were measured. Result: There was no signifcant difference in plasma omega-3 fatty acid levels between the study group and control group. The plasma omega-6 fatty acid levels of the study group were signifcantly less than that of the control group. Conclusion: The present study is an initial attempt to investigate the link between omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acids in patients of depression in a clinical setting of a tertiary care hospital in India. This comparative study with normal controls did not etiologically link these polyunsaturated fatty acids in this sample of depressive disorder. We conclude, however, that research efforts must continue in our country by large and well designed comparative study taking into consideration all confounding variables to investigate if rates of depression vary within India between populations consuming diets with high or low omega-3 fatty acids.
