N2  - Objective: To review the respiratory consequences of chemical warfare agents. Discussion: Chemical warfare agents (CWA) are weapons that work principally through toxicity, causing death, permanent harm or temporary incapacitation. They are classified into six groups: 1. Nerve agents: sarin, soman, sabun and VX. 2. Vesicants: sulfur mustard (SM) and lewisite. 3. Choking or respiratory agents: chlorine and phosgene. 4. Blood agents: cyanide. 5. Psychotropic agents: quinuclidinyl-benzilate. 6. Non-lethal agents including riot controllers e.g. tear gases. Chlorine and SM were largely used during World War I. SM, tabun and sarin were widely applied during the Iraq-Iran war in 1983-88.1 They have induced a lot of morbidity and mortality, mostly due to pulmonary consequences. However, their mechanisms of toxicity are different and thus the respiratory complications are varied. Nerve agents: the nerve agents (NA) are extremely potent organophosphorus compound cholinesterase inhibitors, and are the most toxic of the known chemical weapons.2 The main effects of NA include cholinergic (muscarinic and nicotinic) syndromes and CNS toxicity. However, respiratory tract is the most affected organ, either as a result of the neurotoxic effects inducing pulmonary oedema, respiratory depression and even respiratory muscle paralysis due to the intermediate syndrome. The Iraqi army used tabun in Majnoon Island (1984) and sarin in Halabjah (1988) which induced a lot of morbidity and mortality due to respiratory failure.2,3 Sarin was also used in terrorist attacks in Matsomoto (1994) and in the Tokyo metro (1995) with high respiratory involvement as rhinotracheobronchitis, respiratory depression, pulmonary oedema leading to death in a few patients.3 Antimuscarinic (atropine) and antinicotinic (oximes) are well known antidotes for OP compounds and have been used for more than six decades, but their therapeutic effects are controversial.4 Sulfur mustard: SM is an alkylating agent that initially reacts with ocular, respiratory and cutaneous tissues resulting in acute poisoning and even death. Acute toxic effects of SM appear after variable periods of latency (minutes to weeks) depending on the dose, mode of exposure, environmental temperature, and on the individual. The most targeted organ is the lungs with several pathologies, ranging from acute bronchitis to bronchopneumonia, ARDS and even pulmonary emboli, which induce mortality particularly in the second week after SM exposure.5 Delayed toxic effects of SM in Iranian veterans 16 - 20 years after SM exposure were found in the lungs, peripheral nervous system, immune system, skin and eyes,6 which are different from the early toxic effects7. The late respiratory complications were chronic bronchitis, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, bronchiectasis, asthma, large airway narrowing, and pulmonary fibrosis.6,7 Immunosuppression has been a main problem for the moderate to severely SM poisoned patients since their exposure which has caused recurrent infections and even mortality.6-8 We have observed delayed complications on the upper respiratory tract, even with malignancies.8,9 Lewisite: This arsenical vesicant (2-chlorovinyldichloroarsine) was developed during World War I, but was not used. Lewisite toxicity is similar to that of SM, resulting in dermal and mucous membrane damage with conjunctivitis, airway injury, and vesiculation, but less secondary infection. Its specific known antidote is British anti- Lewisite.1 Pulmonary agents: Although both pulmonary agents (chlorine and phosgene) were not used in large scale after World War I (WWI), the risk of exposure remains because of their extensive use in industry, or possibly as a terrorist weapon. When released on the battlefield, chlorine forms a yellow-green cloud with a distinct pungent odour detectable at levels that are not immediately dangerous. Phosgene is either colourless or seen as a white cloud as a result of atmospheric hydrolysis. Both chlorine and phosgene produce injury by hydrolysis to hydrochloric acid in the lungs. Battlefield exposure induces cough, chest discomfort, dyspnoea, lacrimation, and asphyxiation. Acute tracheobronchitis, bronchopneumonia and pulmonary oedema may develop causing respiratory failure and death. Prolonged observation after phosgene exposure is the rule, as some casualties had initially appeared well and had been discharged, but unfortunately returned in severe respiratory distress a few hours later during WWI. The average estimated recovery time was 60 days for chlorine and 45.5 days for phosgene. There is no known effective antidote for chlorine, but N-acetyl cysteine was described as an antidote for phosgene.1 Respiratory consequences of the other CWA agents are not prominent to be described here. Conclusion: Respiratory system is the target organ for most CWA particularly chlorine, phosgene, SM and Lewisite. Delayed respiratory complications of SM have been major health problems for the veterans of WWI and the Iraq-Iran war.
