N2  - Earlier, we proposed that the brain metabolic changes mediating the late-life depression may result from three interacting causes: 1) Aging (aging-related inflammatory responses) or disease (vascular)-related factors; 2) Frontolimbic abnormalities serving as predisposing factors; and 3) biological responses to chronic experience of stress. We used this model as a guide for studies aiming to clarify contributors to late-life depression with the explicit goal to develop targeted interventions. For this reason, among biological abnormalities likely to characterize geriatric depression, our studies focus on those sustaining its symptoms. Dr. Gunning will report on the relationship of task-related activation of emotional and cognitive control systems and the expression and persistence of late-life depression. Further, she will present novel findings on relationships among structural indices of brain aging, activation of control systems, and the expression of depressive symptoms and antidepressant response. Dr. Alexopoulos will present findings on functional connectivity at rest (FC). Reduced FC in the cognitive control network may distinguish depressed from normal elders and be associated with low remission rate, persisting apathy, and dysexecutive behavior. High FC at the “default network” may distinguish depressed from normal elders, be correlated with pessimism, but not influence the outcomes of depression. Based on these findings, we developed two experimental treatment strategies: 1. Dr. Morimoto will discuss neuroplasticity-based cognitive remediation aimed to optimize cognitive control functions, 2: The anti-inflammatory agent minocycline as an augmentation based on the assumption that aging-enhanced inflammatory responses contribute to resistance of late life depression to antidepressants. Dr. Kiosses will present findings on two novel psychosocial interventions aimed to reduce stress responses contributing to the depressive syndrome. The first, “Problem Adaptation Therapy” (PATH; conducted by Kiosses et al), integrates problem solving and environmental adaptation strategies intended for homebound depressed elders, who live under chronic distress because of their executive impairment. The second, “Ecosystem Focused Therapy” (EFT; conducted by Alexopoulos et al) is intended for patients with post-stroke depression who are exposed to acute distress because of a sudden disability and an upheaval in their support system.
