N2  - Introduction: Depressive polarity has been neglected for a considerable time. Today much data supports the idea that depression (the major depressive relapses or the subdepressive chronic syndrome) worsens the long-term outcome of the bipolar disorders (even for type I) and impairs the patient's quality of life. Methods: We will overview the attempts to define Resistant- Treatment Bipolar Depression (TRBD). The Sach's definition of the unipolar treatment-resistant depression (resistance to two courses of antidepressant treatment) was extended by Gitlin et al. to mood-stabilisers. Yatham et al. preferred to limit the definition of the TRBD to lithium resistance. More recently, Pacchiarroti et al. proposed to define the TRBD as a six-week lithotherapy resistance or 6 week lamotrigine combined with an other moodstabiliser resistance, or a resistance to 600 mg of quetiapine treatment which has been administrated for at least 6 weeks. Discussion: According to the latest guidelines (CANMAT and ISBP) we will also discuss the therapeutic strategies (for acute and maintenance management) including: the association of moodstabilisers, the place of antidepressants (IRSS, IMAO, TCA), of antipsychotic drugs (first and second generation), of Electroconvulsivotherapy and of the novel therapeutics agents (such as bupropion, topiramate, eicosapentaenoic acid, riluzole, Nacetyl-cysteine, omega3-fatty-acids).
