N2  - Sleep disorders are frequently associated with Autism Spectrum Disorders (ASD), and are often a major concern for the patients and their families. Low melatonin levels have been reported in patients with ASD in several studies, and melatonin treatment could be efficient as a treatment for sleep disorders. In a previously published mutation screening of genes coding the melatonin receptors, we identified a mutation (I49N) in MTNR1A receptor in a male patient with high-functioning autism and depressive symptoms [1]. The patient displayed a delayed sleep phase syndrome (mean time of sleep onset determined by actimetry at 0:52). The mutation was inherited from the father and was not found in 362 controls. This amino acid change severely impaired melatonin binding in vitro. MTNR1A receptor is expressed in humans in the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus and could regulate melatonin synthesis by feedback. MTNR1A is also expressed in corticotropin-releasing hormone neurons and in the pituitary gland [2], and is thought to regulate cortisol secretion. In addition, MTNR1A is widely expressed in peripheral tissues. Here we have investigated the possible consequences of this MTNR1A mutation on the biological rhythms of the patient in vivo. The nyctohemeral cycles of melatonin and cortisol were assessed from saliva samples collected at 7 time points throughout the day, for 2 days. As a marker of peripheral rhythms, the transcriptional cycles of 2 clock genes, PER3 and BMAL2, were assessed after RNA extraction from saliva samples, collected at 4 time points throughout the day for 2 days. The results obtained for the patient carrying the MTNR1A mutation were compared with those obtained from the parents and the two unaffected brothers not carrying the mutation. The patient displayed an unusually long duration of melatonin secretion, with a marked increase of salivary melatonin starting around 8 pm (despite delayed sleep onset) and still detectable around 10 am (the usual duration of melatonin peak is 11-12h [3]). This could reflect a deficient feedback regulation of melatonin on its own secretion. However, this feature was not found in the father, also carrying the MTNR1A mutation. Considering cortisol secretion, the patient displayed high daytime cortisol levels, whithout a detectable peak in the morning. This could be the consequence of a deficient nyctohemeral regulation of cortisol secretion, and/or a feature associated with the depressive symptoms. In contrast, the father's cortisol profile was similar to that obtained for the mother carrying no MTNR1A mutation. Finally, regarding peripheral transcriptional rhythms of clock genes, no difference of phase or amplitude of variation was seen between the patient and his two brothers. Thus, circadian alterations could be observed in our patient with MTNR1A mutation, affecting melatonin and cortisol secretion, in addition to the clinical delayed sleep onset. However, his father, also carrying the mutation, did not seem to display any clinical or biological circadian disturbance. The impact of this heterozygote MTNR1A mutation may vary according to the presence of additional risk factors and/or compensatory mechanisms. Importantly, melatonin treatment was efficient in attenuating the patient's delayed sleep phase syndrome.
