N2  - Immune activation induces pro-inflammatory state or cytokines which enhance the tryptophan (TRP) degradation into kynurenine (KYN). This KYN-pathway has been reported to be involved in pathophysiology of major depression (Myint et al., 2007) or depressive episodes after treatment with interferon- a (Wichers et al., 2005). It is hypothesized that increased TRP breakdown occur in the immune cells in the brain such as astrocytes and microglia and this induces decreased serotonin synthesis and in turn induces depression. Here, the effects of anti-inflammatory medication, cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) inhibitor, celecoxib and different antidepressants were investigated. The astrocytes from the 2-day old C57BL/6JRccHsd mice brains were cultured with and without LPS, PolyI:C, IFNc, antidepressants and anti-inflammatory celecoxib. TRP metabolites were analyzed using UPLC Tandam quadropole MS. Activation of Toll-Like receptor pathways (NFjB and IRF3) and related cytokines Interleukin (IL)-1b and Interferon-b were analyzed using ELISA. Treatment with celecoxib, venlafexine, reboxetine, imipramine and fluoxetine to astrocyte cultures without any immune challenge induces enhancement in TRP breakdown. Challenge with LPS does not induce change in TRP concentration or breakdown whereas IFNc and PolyI:C induces reduction in TRP concentration and increase TRP breakdown. Only celecoxib could reverse the enhanced TRP breakdown in astrocyte culture induced by IFNc. Celecoxib and antidepressants in LPS challenged culture: induces even enhanced TRP breakdown. Celecoxib in PolyI:C challenged culture: induces reversal of TRP breakdown to basal level. Antidepressants could not reverse this increase in TRP breakdown induced by PolyI:C. The increase of TRP breakdown after addition of celecoxib and antidepressants into culture without any immune challenge indicated that unnecessary medications might be hazardous to the TRP metabolism in astrocytes. Based on those findings, it could be concluded that in subgroups of depressed patients whose immune system would be in pro-inflammatory state - simulated here with IFNc or PolyI:C, treatment with celecoxib could be beneficial not only to peripheral immune system but also to the metabolism in the astrocytes. Results from TLR3/4 signaling cascade will also be discussed more detailed.
