N2  - Background: Reduced muscle performance, general fatigue and nutritional deficienciesand can occur in patients with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) impairingtheir work and daily lives. Decline of muscle strength and physical functioncould be a cytokine-mediated process as observed in advancing age and severalchronic disorders. Anti-TNF-a medication markedly improves several IBD symptoms, however, it is not known if also affects can muscle strength.AIMS AND Methods: Therefore, the aim of the current study was to assess theeffect of infliximab on muscle function and physical performance in IBD patients.Twenty-three IBD outpatients were included and their nutritional status (BMI), diseaseactivity (CDAI), body composition (bioelectrical impedance analysis), musclefunction (knee extensor and handgrip strength, fatigue resistance tests) and physicalperformance chair stands test and habitual and maximal gait velocity were evaluated.Fatigue, and quality of life were investigated using specific tools (fatigue visualanalogical scale and Inflammatory Bowel Disease Questionnaire (IBDQ). Results: Of the total patients, 78.3% had CD and 21.7% UC. At baseline, most ofthe patients were well nourished and 34.8 % with FFM depletion. Impaired handgripand quadriceps strength were present in 65.2% and 52.2 % of patients. Timeto perform sit up test was 8.42 ±.22 s. All patients had reduced quality of life, frequently with fatigue (91%) with predisposition to depressive feelings (69.6%).After infliximab infusion, an improvement in sit up test (p=0.026), fatigue(p=0.007), and quality of life(p= 0.0005) associated to decrease of depressivesymptoms (p=0, 003). Sit up test (worse performance) has an inverse correlationwith quadriceps strength (r=- 0. 460, p=0, 027), depression symptoms(r= 0, 738, p= <0.001) and positive with to TNF-α levels(r= 0.480, p= 0.032). Conclusion: Infliximab treatment improved physical performance, fatigue, depressive feelings and quality of life in IBD patients. Muscle strength anddepressive symptoms were related to physical performance.
