N2  - Purpose: Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) experts have highlighted a “topdown” (using the most effective biologic or immunosuppressive agent) approachas a treatment option for IBD in order to minimize the >50% of patients thateventually become “steroid dependent” or “steroid resistant.1, 2 This study willquantify comorbid disease associated with Crohn's Disease (CD); describe the frequencyand severity of disease flares; and characterize the use of corticosteroidtreatment patterns and steroid burden. Methods: The National Health and Wellness Survey, a nationally representativeinternet-based survey was administered during the 4th quarter of 2010 to individualsaged ≥18 years with a diagnosis of Crohn's Disease (CD). Chi-Square tests and Student'st-tests were performed to test for differences between groups. Results: A total of 782 individuals with CD completed the survey (65% female;average age = 47.9 years). Common comorbid conditions of individuals with CDincluded depression (29%), sleep difficulties (28%), anxiety (25%), anemia (30%), osteoporosis (9%) and osteopenia (10%). The incidence of osteoporosis washigher (17%) in those individuals reporting being treated with steroids. The meantime from first symptoms of CD to diagnosis was 3.3 years. Individuals with moderateor severe disease reported CD flares occurring (≥1/week) significantly(p<0.05) more often (31% and 13% respectively) as compared to 3% of individualswith mild disease during in the past 12 months. The mean number ofdisease flares reported by individuals with CD during the previous 12 monthswas 36.7 (range=15.8-142.5) with flares lasting a mean of 6.9 days. Individualswith moderate or severe disease had a significantly (p<0.05) greater numberof mean disease flares as compared to individuals with mild disease. Frequentlycited causes of flares included stress (55%), diet (35%), and infection(14%). The most common treatment(s) of flares by individuals included 'addinga steroid' (32%) and 'increasing a dose of a current medication” (20%), including an antibiotic, 5-aminosalicylic acid agent, immunomodulator, biologic, or other. Steroid utilization of ≥3 times/year occurred in 21% of allCD individuals and increased significantly (p<0.05) in those with moderatedisease. Stockpiling of steroids occurred in 28% of all CD individuals withsignificantly (p<0.05) more persons with moderate or severe disease reportingstockpiling. Reasons for 'stockpiling' of steroids included 'having as amaintenance medication' (41%), 'avoiding future flares' (33%) and 'treatmentof current flares' (31%). Conclusion: Individuals with CD often have multiple comorbid conditions thatinclude psychological and inflammatory conditions. Furthermore, the frequencyof disease flares varied by disease severity and resulted in increased steroid burden.Additional research is needed to examine the factors and outcomes associatedwith these behaviors and differences.
