N2  - Introduction Invasive Aspergillosis(IA) has been shown to complicate SARS, but often when steroids have been given. We present a case of IA in an atypical patient group - a patient with asthma admitted to our Intensive Care Unit with H1N1 pneumonia. We avoided steroids (this would almost certainly have resulted in her death without voriconazole), but she still got IA. In October 2009, a 46 year old female attended A+E department at our Trust with increasing shortness of breath (SOB) and two weeks history of flu-like symptoms . She was complaining of non-productive cough, aches and sweats, tight chest, sore throat and back pain. She had received amoxicillin from GP with no improvement. There was no acute pain, calf swelling or previous clots. She was known to have mild asthma and depression and was on citalopram, salbutamol and seretide. She was a smoker, lived with partner and was attending college. She had been adopted as a child. On examination her temperature was 37.3C, pulse was 108 and BP 106/65. Her respiratory rate was 28 and her SaO2 39% on air which improved to 89% on 15L O2. Her airway was patent and she was able to speak in full sentences. She had a wheeze and crepitations all over her chest. Her GCS was 15/15. She was admitted to ITU, intubated and needed BIPAP on propofol and alfentanyl. Her FiO2 was 80%, sats 97%. Her temperature rose to 38.4C.Throat swabs for H1N1 and full septic screen including blood cultures were sent. She was given enoxaparin, pantoprazole, Coamoxiclav and Clarithromycin. Scientific findings Admission H1N1 throat swabs and bacterial screens were negative. She was on Coamoxiclav and Clarithromycin and Oseltamivir was added. Her WCC was 2.3x 106 and CRP 179mg/L. Four days after admission her sputum culture grew Aspergillus fumigatus and Candida albicans. Six days after admission her bronchial lavage specimen was H1N1 positive and also grew A.fumigatus and Candida spp. in multiple samples. She was started on liposomal amphotericin B and antibiotics changed to Tazocin and clarithromycin. Her chest radiograph showed diffuse consolidation throughout the lungs, suggestive of ARDS. Her CT thorax showed bilateral multiple lung cavities with extensive ground glass opacification and consolidation, compatible with infection; possibly fungal, atypical or tuberculosis. Discussion At the time of worsening markers and chest signs, there was some concern about whether this was bronchiolitis obliterans and discussion about starting steroids. However expert advice sought from the National Aspergillus Centre in Manchester indicated that the combination of cavities, ground glass consolidation and positive cultures of A. fumigatus was more likely to represent IA. She showed signs of failure on Liposomal amphotericin and antifungals were changed to Voriconazole and Caspofungin. The patient showed slow and steady improvement from this point, monitored by serial CT and Voriconazole levels. She had Caspofungin for three weeks, Vorconazole for 10 weeks follwed by six weeks Itraconazole. Conclusions Lessons learnt in this case of IA in an atypical patient group- 1. Cultures positive with A. fumigatus in ICU have ∼50% positive predictive value for IA, and have a 40% sensitivity compared with a BAL galactomannan (∼85% sensitivity). 2. Voriconazole is a preferred primary therapy. She reported severe hair loss while on voriconazole treatment, however we were not sure if this was a direct side-effect of the drug or telogen effluvium. 3. We believe that by seeking expert opinion on appropriate antifungals and avoiding steroids in her acute phase of illness, this patient survived the IA.
