N2  - Introduction The development and progression of intracranial tuberculomas whilst on appropriate antituberculous therapy is well recognised. The reported interval between the start of antituberculous therapy and the neurological manifestations of paradoxical tuberculomas range between 10 days to 27 months. Corticosteroids are routinely used in tuberculous meningitis and in cases of tuberculomas with surrounding oedema. When maximal medical therapy with steroid and antituberculous agents have failed to control the progression of tuberculomas, other immunomodulatory agents have been tried with variable success. We report 2 cases of progression of paradoxical tuberculomas in HIV negative patients and their management with thalidomide and infliximab. Thalidomide is a Tumour Necrosis Factor α (TNF α) inhibitor and has been shown to be beneficial in animal models with some anecdotal evidence of benefit in children and adults with refractory intracranial tuberculous abscesses. Infliximab is a monoclonal antibody, which also acts to inhibit TNF α. Treatment with inflimixab is more usually associated with the development of de novo tuberculous, rather than as an adjunct in the management of paradoxical tuberculomas. Case 1: A 37 year old man presented with headaches, confusion and clinical findings of meningism. CT head showed basal enhancement, supratentorial tuberculomas and hydrocephalus. Fully sensitive Mycobacterium tuberculosis was cultured from CSF. He was started on 1st line antituberculous therapy, high dose dexamethasone. Ten months into antituberculous therapy he developed a right homonymous hemianopia with radiological evidence of progression of cerebral tuberculomas. He was commenced on thalidomide, which led to clinical and radiological improvement. (Images will be presented). Scientific findings Case 2: A 29 year old female presented with right arm spastic weakness. She was in the 12th month of treatment for fully sensitive tuberculous meningitis and spinal TB. MRI showed new lesions at C2/3 with cord compression and new tuberculomas around the brain stem. CSF was acellular with negative ZN stain and culture. She was commenced on high dose dexamethasone and continued 1st line antituberculous therapy. Steroid weaning resulted in neurological deterioration; tetraparesis and respiratory depression. She received pulsed methylprednisolone and infliximab. This was followed by neurological and radiological improvement of the tuberculomas. (Images will be presented). Discussion The development of paradoxical tuberculomas in patients receiving appropriate antituberculous therapy is thought to be due to an exaggerated inflammatory response in the context of immune reconstitution and changing immunological milieu following antituberculous therapy. We found the adjunctive use of thalidomide and infliximab to be beneficial in patients with paradoxical tuberculomas, causing severe neurological compromise. Only anecdotal reports of the benefit of both these drugs in this clinical scenario have been published with no large trials. One randomised controlled trial on thalidomide used in children was discontinued due to serious adverse effects and deaths in the treatment arm. Conclusions There may be a role for immunomodulatory agents in the management of paradoxical tuberculomas in patients who have received maximal medical therapy with high dose steroids and appropriate antituberculous agents. The use of immunomodulatory agents should be considered and may be of benefit as an adjunct to corticosteroids if clinical or radiological improvement has not occurred following maximal medical therapy, if there is on-going clinical deterioration or if there is critical neurological deficit or loss of vision. Further research into the use immunomodulatory agents in management of paradoxical tuberculomas is called for.
