N2  - Introduction and Objectives: Chronic emotional stress is associated with high cortisol release and metabolism disorders like hypertension, high blood glucose level or increase of metabolic syndrome. Although acute stress is associated with an altered urinary composition that promotes calcium oxalate stones, no studies have evaluated the influence of chronic stress on this kind of stone and its recurrence. Material and Methods: A total of 128 patients were enrolled in this case-control study over a period of 20 months. All the cases were calcium oxalate stone formers with a recent stone episode (<3 months); 31 were FS and 33 RS formers. Controls were matched by sex and age. The absence of stone in controls was confirmed by clinical history and by ultrasound examination of the kidneys. All dimensions of chronic stress were evaluated in all patients by self-reported validated questionnaires measuring stressful life events, perceived stress, anxiety, depression, burnout and satisfaction with life. An ad hoc self-reported questionnaire was designed to evaluate stress related to stone episodes in cases. Blood and eight-hour urine samples were collected to determine cortisol levels and urinary composition. In addition, epidemiological data, socioeconomic aspects, diet and incidences of metabolic syndrome was reported. Results: There was no significant difference between cases (64) and controls (64) in any of the individual questionnaires evaluating each dimension of chronic stress. 43% of FS formers and 56% of RS formers were male, and median age was 46 (20-74) and 47 (26-70) years, respectively. The number of stressful life events (p < 0.001) and the intensity of perceived stressful live events (p < 0.001) were significantly higher in RS than in FS patients. In contrast, there was no difference between the two groups in perceived stress, stress related to stone episodes, anxiety, depression, burnout, or satisfaction with life. Higher glucose (p < 0.1), uric acid (p < 0.05), blood cortisol (p < 0.05), and urine calcium levels (p < 0.05) were observed in RS than in FS. Low economic levels and incidences of metabolic syndrome were higher in RS patients (p < 0.05, p < 0.05). Conclusions: Despite the limited number of patients included in this study, increased stressful life events and intensity of perceived events were observed in RS in comparision to FS. These differences correlate with certain blood and urinary changes and with metabolic disorders, which may represent a potential mechanism relating chronic stress and risk of recurrence.
