N2  - Purpose: AIDS cholangiopathy occurs due to billiary obstruction from infection-related strictures of the biliary tree. The organism most commonly implicated is Cryptosporidium parvum. Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) is a rare cause of AIDS cholangiopathy. Disseminated MAC occurs almost exclusively in AIDS patients with a low CD4 count and is characterized by bacteremia and infection of multiple organs. Ultrasound abdomen showing thickened gallbladder wall. Methods: We present a 25-year-old African American male who presented with generalized body pain and headache for 3 months and loose, bloody stools for 1 month. His medical history was significant for depression and AIDS diagnosed 6 years previously with a CD4 count of 2. He had been non compliant with highly active anti retroviral therapy (HAART) which he stopped completely a year previously. Review of systems was significant for pruritus, weight loss of 42 pounds over 6 months, fever, dysphagia, odynophagia, and nonproductive cough. He denied having any nausea, vomiting or abdominal pain. At the time of admission he had tachycardia and was febrile with a temperature of 102 degrees Fahrenheit. He was catechetic with icterus, dry mucous membranes and oral thrush. Abdomen was soft, with no distension or tenderness and normal active bowel sounds. Rectal examination was painful and showed brown stool without blood on the examining finger. Results: Laboratory data was significant for leucopenia and anemia with a total billirubin of 5.4 and direct billirubin of 3.5. Alkaline phosphatase was 732, AST 434, ALT 93 and albumin 2.3. Broad spectrum antibiotics and Nystatin swish and swallow were initiated. Ultrasound of the abdomen showed mild hepatomegaly and probable fatty liver infiltrate but no biliary dilatation. Chest x-ray was normal and blood and urine cultures drawn at admission showed no growth. Stool studies for ova and parasites, acid-fast stain for isospora and microspora, cytomegallovirus IgG and IgM and hepatitis panel were negative. Clostridium dificile toxin was detected and patient was started on metronidazole. However, he continued to have a fever and alkaline phosphatase increased to 1300, thus he had magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP) followed by endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) with stent placement and biliary sphincterotomy for a distal bile duct stricture. His blood cultures for Mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) was positive and he was treated with rifampin, azithromycin and ethambutol with subsequent clinical and biochemical improvement. Conclusion: This is an unusual presentation of disseminated MAC in a severely immunocompromised patient. In AIDS patients MAC presents as extrapulmonary and disseminated infection. A high index of suspicion is needed to make the diagnosis.
