N2  - Purpose: Case: We report a case of a 34 year old female with h/o recurrent abdominal pain presented with c/o LLQ pain associated with nausea and night sweats. She stated pain was similar to the diverticulitis pain which she had before. She denied any other complaints. PMHx is significant for depression for which she takes Zoloft and recurrent diverticulitis. She drinks alcohol socially and smokes a pack a day. She was afebrile and vitals stable during presentation. CBC, BMP, LFT were within normal limits. CT showed acutely inflamed diverticulum and a linear foreign body most probably a pill within and left sided ovarian cyst. Surgery and OBGYN consult were obtained and both suggested conservative management with iv fluids with antibiotics and f/u as outpatient. Patient did well with conservative management and was discharged the next day. She was advised to get a CT of abdomen as outpatient and see surgery of the foreign body persists. CT done after 10 days showed foreign body in the same region but she was lost to follow up. Discussion: Acute Diverticulitis is the most common colon emergency for the acute care surgeon. Since the incidence of diverticulosis is high in western population, diverticulitis in very common and is one of the leading causes for ER visits. While most cases are due to obstruction due to fecolith, clinician should be aware of other causes of diverticulitis like foreign bodies particularly in the setting of recurrent disease.
