N2  - Introduction: The 41 amino acid neuropeptide corticotropin releasing factor (CRF) initiates the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal (HPA) axis response to stress, and has been the subject of intense investigation in the pathophysiology and treatment of depression and anxiety disorders. In the mammalian brain, the CRF system comprises two G-protein-coupled CRF receptor subtypes (CRF1 and CRF2 receptors). In addition to a major projection from the paraventricular nucleus of the hypothalamus (PVN) to the pituitary corticotropes, CRF-containing neurons and receptors are also found in brain areas involved in stress responses, including the amygdala, the lateral septum, the locus coeruleus and the brainstem raphe nuclei. In this context, it was postulated that CRF receptor antagonists may represent novel agents for the treatment of stress-related disorders. As an example, the CRF1 receptor antagonist, SSR125543, has been reported to induce anxiolytic- and antidepressant-like effect in several animal models in rodents [1]. Aim: The aim of the present study was to evaluate the effects of SSR125543 in an animal model of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) in mice, which involves the assessment of cognitive performance following acute inescapable stress exposure and to verify whether this effect is mediated by an action on the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis. The prototypical 5-HT reuptake inhibitor, paroxetine, was used as a positive control. Methods: Swiss mice received two unavoidable electric foot-shocks (1.5 mA/2s). Two weeks later they were placed in the shock apparatus context and fecal corticosterone metabolites were measured by enzyme-immunoassay. One day after re-exposure to the context, their cognitive performance were evaluated using the object recognition task which is traditionally used to assess short-term visual episodic memory, following the administration of SSR125543 (10 mg/kg, i.p.) or paroxetine (20 mg/kg, i.p.). To assess the involvement of the HPA axis in the drug effects, a separate group of animals was subjected to the same procedure and drug regimen, but was treated with dexamethasone (s.c., 500 mg/kg) to blunt the HPA axis. Results: Stressed mice had higher levels of corticosterone metabolite following re-exposure to the shock context and displayed impaired cognitive performance in the object recognition task as compared to control animals. This latter effect was significantly attenuated by SSR125543 and paroxetine, whether the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis was blunted or not. Intriguingly, paroxetine produced deleterious effects on cognition in control animals, an effect which was further potentiated by prior administration of dexamethasone. Conclusion: These findings confirm further that the CRF1 receptor antagonist, SSR125543, is able to attenuate the deleterious effects of stressful exposure. Importantly, the observation that these effects were still present in dexamethasone treated mice indicates that this action does not necessarily involve pituitary adrenal axis blockade, thereby suggesting that extrahypothalamic CRF1 receptors may play a role in these effects.
