N2  - Objectives: α-synuclein and Lewy bodies detected throughout the brain in Parkinson disease (PD) patients might play a crucial role in the development of motor (dopaminergic) symptoms in PD, but also in the plethora of nonmotor symptoms which dominate the clinical picture in advanced disease. Parallel to tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia and postural instability, other symptoms may co-exist such as depression, anhedonia, hallucinations, sleep disorders and autonomic, gastro-intestinal and sensory dysfunctions [1]. Even closely related to amyloid plaque in Alzheimers Disease, common PET tracers employed to image these plaques fail to image α-synuclein and Lewis bodies. Polyphenols including rosmarinic acid show besides properties such as anti-inflammatoric, anti-oxidant or anti-viral effects [2] affinity towards α-synuclein aggregates. We decided therefore to evaluate the [11C]methyl ester of rosmarinic acid (Fig.1) as potential in vivo probe for α-synuclein aggregates [3]. (Figure presented) Methods: All procedures involving animals were performed under a license granted by the Danish Ministery of Justice. Non-radioactive rosmarinic acid methyl ester was synthesized applying a literature method using commercially available rosmarinic acid and methanol and Amberlyst cation exchange resin [4]. Radiolabeling was achieved in a GE Tracerlab FX C Pro system by reacting [11C]methyl iodide with rosmarinic acid (1 mg) in 300 μl DMSO at 90 °C in the presence of 1 μl of 3 M NaOH within 2 min. Product was isolated on a semi-preparative C- 18 column with ethanol : 0.1 % H3PO4 (37:63); flow: 2.5 ml/min; UV: 280 nm. Before injection into rats the collected product was diluted with saline to an ethanol content of 10 %. Log P7.4 was measured. In vivo evaluation was performed in male Lewis rats both in a Concorde MicroPET R4 system as well as in organ distribution studies (rats sacrificed at 15, 30 and 45 min). Arterial bloodsamples (2, 5, 10, 15 min) and brain extracts (15 and 30 min) were analyzed for parent compound and metabolites. Results: The applied radiolabeling procedure yielded rosmarinic acid [11C]methyl ester within 30 min in radiochemical yields of 60 -70 %, based on [11C]methyl iodide. Acidic esterification used for the synthesis of the reference material was too slow to be used. Radiochemical purity was ≥ 95 % with a mean specific radioactivity of 120 GBq/μmol (EOS). Log P7.4 was measured to 1.2. In vivo evaluation revealed a very fast metabolism with basically no residual parent compound left in arterial blood sample after 2 min. Analysis of rat brain extracts did not show rosmarinic acid [11C]methyl ester. The observed brain uptake within the μPET scanning (see Fig. 2) is therefore solely contributed to radioactive metabolites entering the brain. Conclusion: The very fast metabolism and the brain entry of radioactive metabolites unfortunately excludes rosmarinic acid [11C] methyl ester as potential PET tracer. Currently, efforts are made to incorporate the radioactive label into the backbone of a rosmarinic acid like structure yielding a metabolically more stable compound.
