N2  - Introduction: The immune defect of DiGeorge Syndrome (DGS) typically affects only the T-cell system. As patients age, some may also exhibit attrition of the B-cell system. We describe a patient with DGS who began to demonstrate clinical symptoms and laboratory evidence consistent with antibody deficiency syndrome. Additionally, he developed pulmonary nodules, lymphadenopathy, hepatomegaly, and massive splenomegaly. A lymph node biopsy revealed noncaseating granulomas, more commonly associated with Common Variable Immune Deficiency (CVID) but rarely reported in DGS. Case Report: A 22-year-old Asian-American male with DGS characterized by congenital heart disease, hypocal- cemia, mild T-cell depression, and 22q11.2 deletion on FISH chromosomal analysis at birth began to suffer from recurrent otitis media and sino-pulmonary infections during childhood. He required mastoidectomy and cholesteotoma removal. He underwent further immune evaluation, which illustrated low levels of IgG and IgA and decreased humoral response to vaccinations, and was started on IVIG. He then began to develop restrictive pulmonary disease, hepatosplenomegaly, pancytopenia, and cervical and inguinal lymphadenopathy. Pulmonary nodules were evident on CT scan. Biopsy of the inguinal lymph nodes revealed small, non-caseating granulomas, often seen in CVID. Bone marrow biopsies and evaluation for oligoclonal expansion were negative. His T-cell function remained unchanged, but his B-cell system began to undergo progressive attrition. The patient was treated with several immunomodulators including oral corticosteroids and infliximab with minimal improvement. A liver biopsy revealed moderate lymphohistiocytic inflammation both in clusters and single cells without well-defined granulomas. A spleen biopsy has not been performed but would presumptively reveal infiltration with granulomas. The patient also recently developed significant elevations in IgM levels without a clear etiology. Discussion: There have been few long-term studies assessing changes in the immune system of patients with DGS. As a result of improvements in cardiac surgery, the lifespan of these patients has increased, providing the opportunity to study changes in their immune function over time. With prolonged survival, additional defects in the immune system are becoming apparent, as seen in this patient with mild, stable T-cell defects and evidence of attrition of the B-cell system. The presence of non-caseating granulomas is also rarely seen in DGS. The mechanism underlying these changes in humoral immunity remains unclear, but potential involvement of T-cells including oligoclonal expansion and dysfunction of regulatory T cells should be considered.
