N2  - The high prevalence of comorbid depression in patients with chronic inflammatory disorders is caused by the propagation of peripheral inflammation to the brain. The proinflammatory cytokines that are expressed in the brain by activated macrophage-like cells and microglia during inflammation are normally responsible for why we feel sick and behave in a sick way when we are ill. Sickness behavior wanes off when healing takes over inflammation. This is an active process that is dependent on the production of anti-inflammatory cytokines and the modulatory role of glucocorticoids and neuropeptides such as vasopressin, alpha-melanotropin, and insulin-like growth factor 1. When the inflammatory response is too intense and/or lasts too long, symptoms of depression can develop on top of sickness. The emergence of depression is mediated by activation of the tryptophan-degrading enzyme indoleamine 2,3 dioxygenase (IDO) and production of neuroactive kynurenine metabolites mainly represented by 3- hydroxy kynurenine and quinolinic acid. These metabolites act as agonists of the NMDA receptor. A role for cytokine-induced IDO activation in the pathogenesis of depression is supported by converging clinical and experimental data. The possibility that the same mechanisms account for other forms of depression than depression associated with inflammation is an active line of research. There is evidence that non-immune stressors can also activate inflammatory processes and IDO. In addition, treatmentresistant depression has been found to be associated with biomarkers of inflammation. However, the points of convergence between immune and non-immune aspects of depression still remain to be established. Supported by Grant R01 NIH MH079829 to RD and R01 NIH AG029573 to KWK.
