N2  - The omega (n)-3 fatty acid, eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) has been recently used to treat depression. Since inflammation plays an important role in etiology of depression and other psychiatric diseases, one of the mechanisms by which EPA attenuates mood disorders and neurodegeneration-like changes is by reducing inflammation and modulating neurotransmission. Peripheral immune responses or many brain conditions, such as injuries, ageing and malnutrition of essential fatty acids may activate glial cells to trigger neuroinflammation, which induces psychiatric disorders and their associated neurodegeneration. However, the effect of EPA on glial activity and on glial production of neurotrophins is unknown. The present study aimed at studying the potential neuroprotective effects of EPA on the activation of glial cells, the expressions of neurotrophin brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) and BDNF receptors, as well as brain fatty acid concentrations in a rodent model of neuroinflammation induced by interleukin (IL)-1β. Male Long-even rats were fed with EPA or with palm oil and received saline or IL-1β injection. After 42 days dietary supplementation and 7 days consecutive injections, the hippocampus has been dissected and collected. The results showed that microglia activation was increased after IL-1 administration, which was associated with the increase in the expression of tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α in the hippocampus. By contrast, astrocyte activity was decreased, which was associated with the decrease in BDNF and TrkB receptor expressions. EPA supplement effectively attenuated these changes. Moreover, EPA ameliorated the IL-1β-induced increase in p75NTR receptor expressions, and normalized IL-1β-induced decrease in EPA and DPA contents in the hippocampus. Our results indicated that EPA may provide neuroprotective effects by reducing glial activation, inhibiting inflammation, and modulating BDNF function and other brain fatty acids.
