N2  - Basic research has established that the long chain omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA), docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), plays a fundamental role in brain structure and function. Epidemiological and cross-sectional studies have also identified a role for long chain omega-3 PUFA in the etiology of depression. In a meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials of omega 3 in depression, it was concluded that symptoms of depression were not significantly reduced in 3 studies using pure DHA or in 4 studies containing >50% DHA. In contrast, symptoms of depression were significantly reduced in 13 studies using supplements containing >50% eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and in 8 studies using pure ethyl-EPA. Recently, it has been suggested that the efficacy of antidepressant treatments might be increased by the co-administration of anti-inflammatory agents. In this context, the mechanisms that have been invoked to account for the benefits of long chain omega-3 PUFA in depression include reductions in proinflammatory cytokines, reductions in prostaglandins derived from arachidonic acid, leading to decreased brain derived neurotrophic factor levels and/or improvements in blood flow to the brain.
