N2  - Alcohol-related brain damage (ARBD) has serious health, social, and financial consequences, and can occur independently of any major nutritional deficiency. The mechanisms that mediate ARBD are largely unknown, although accumulating evidence suggests that intercellular inflammatory cascades, involving astrocyte interleukin-18 (IL-18), microglia quinolinic acid and neuronal oxidant status and sphingosine-1-phosphate efflux are crucial. Here it is proposed that intercellular inflammation drives a spreading neurodegeneration, akin to current models of α-synuclein and hyperphosphorylated tau in Parkinson and Alzheimer disease. The driving of tryptophan down the kynurenine pathway results in a decrease in serotonin, N-acetylserotonin, and melatonin, contributing to comorbid depression in alcoholism. Alcohol induction of cortisol contributes to a decrease in neurogenesis and an increase in IL-18, thus exacerbating the inflammatory cascade and spreading neurodegeneration. It is suggested that melatonin, and perhaps agomelatine, would have efficacy as long-term maintenance therapies in the treatment of alcoholism; off-setting the likelihood of ARBD, especially when alcoholism is comorbid with psychosis. Copyright © 2011 by Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.
