N2  - Nitrosative stress can be defined as the pathological state (e. g., inflammation) that involves a massive formation of nitrogen oxides (NOx). Because of the high reactivity of NOx, the nitrosative stress condition is often associated with chemical modification of genes, proteins and/or lipids, which may ultimately lead to the alteration in the physiological function of target biomolecules such as transporters. It was reported that patients with bacterial meningitis [i. e., inflammation in the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)] were associated with an elevated level of benzylpenicillin in the CSF and the kinetic change appeared to be related to the altered elimination of the drug from the CSF to the systemic circulation. Based on series of in vivo and in vitro studies, the altered elimination was apparently related to over-production of NOx by the inflammation. Similarly, the functional activity of rOAT3, a transporter for the penicillin, was significantly depressed by exogenous NOx and the depressed activity reversed by superoxide dismutase, indicating that the inhibitory effect requires an activation, probably by the formation of peroxynitrite, with superoxide. When tyrosine residues (i. e., an amino acid that is known to be nitrated by peroxynitrite) in rOAT3 were switched to phenylalanine, certain variants exhibited a significant decrease in the function. Furthermore, a depression in the function was also noted when the similar mutagenesis was carried out for the two of three crucial tyrosine residues that were conserved in human OAT3, suggesting that other OATs having the tyrosine may be functionally sensitive by NOx.
