N2  - Background: Dietary induced-tryptophan (TRP) deficiency has been proposed as an animal model for depression. Studies have mostly tested acute TRP deficiency (ATD) with inconsistent results. Sub-chronic TRP deficiency (SCTD) may prove more sensitive. The aim was to test this hypothesis and optimise the time of SCTD-induced depression-related behaviour with associated biochemical and changes. Methods: Sprague Dawley (SD) rats were treated with a low TRP-containing diet for 0, 7 or 14 days. Peripheral and central neurochemical markers were measured. SCTD-induced depression-related behaviour was assessed by the forced swim test (FST). SCTD model sensitivity to antidepressants was tested by concomitant paroxetine treatment. Results: SCTD induced significant reductions in weight gain, measures of peripheral and central TRP and significant increases of corticosterone, aldosterone, and kynurenine (K), which has NMDA-ergic activity, whilst kynurenic acid (KA), an NMDA receptor antagonist decreased. 5-HT2 receptors measured by 3H-ketanserin binding were significantly up-regulated. SCTD significantly increased floating time and reduced swimming time in the FST which were reversed by paroxetine. Aldosterone plateaued after 7 days, whereas other changes maximised at 14 days SCTD. Conclusions: Findings indicate that 14 days SCTD was optimal in this proposed model which resembles human depression in terms of behavioural and neurochemical attributes. Aldosterone may be an early marker or causal link for depression development, whereas increased cortisol and hippocampus 5-HTreceptor density could be correlates of depressive behaviour. Consequential increases in NMDA signalling through increased K/KA ratios suggests the model may be useful for testing novel antidepressants which transcend the monoamine theory of depression.
