N2  - Objectives: To describe our experience with dexmedetomidine infusion in the care of children presenting with severe respiratory distress due to malignant airway obstruction. Methods: We reviewed the charts of four children, one girl and three boys admitted to our ICU between January and August of 2010 with lesions invading the tracheobronchial tree, including an 8 year old with mucoepidermoid carcinoma and three adolescents suffering from Ewing sarcoma, osteosarcoma, and Wilms tumor. Results: All patients were managed with supplemental humidified oxygen, racemic epinephrine, high-dose steroids, and antibiotics for suspicion of tracheobronchial or pulmonary infection. Helium-oxygen mixtures were used in two cases. Intravenous opioids were utilized for pain relief. Dexmedetomidine infusion, ranging from 0.1 to 0.7 micrograms /kg/hr was used to mitigate anxiety. Duration of infusion varied from 18 to 96 hours. No complications such as respiratory depression or hemodynamic abnormalities were noted. Two children eventually underwent stent placement along with outpatient chemoradiation. Dexmedetomidine was utilized on subsequent readmissions to ICU for disease progression with good symptom control in both instances. Two children had lesions not amenable to stent placement. One improved and was discharged home, and the other child was eventually placed on propofol infusion for palliative sedation for rapidly progressive respiratory failure. Conclusion: Dexmedetomidine appeared to be a valuable agent in the management of severe respiratory distress in our small group of patients with malignant airway obstruction, and merits further evaluation in this setting.
