N2  - Background: The elders are more sensitive to adverse effects of pharmacotherapy for reasons ranging from pharmacokinetic (Cytochrome P450 system of enzymes: 1A2, 2B6, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6, 2E1, 3A34) and pharmacodynamic properties of the agents to inherent biological differences in an aging body and brain. Pharmacokinetic factors and propensity to cause drug-drug interactions are particularly important in elderly patients who have often been prescribed multiple medications for various comorbid physical conditions. Objective: The objective of this study is evaluation of drug-drug interactions in treatment of Major Depressive Disorder with/ without psychiatric and somatic comorbidities. Method: Retrospective review study of the psychiatric charts of randomly taken 75 inpatients with Alzheimer's Disease (according to DSMIVTR) with/without psychiatric or somatic co-morbidities, admitted to Obregia Hospital over the last two years. First we collected data about patients age and gender, principal diagnosis, psychiatric and somatic comorbidities, psychiatric and somatic medications, smoking, coffee and alcohol use. Then, we reevaluated patient's treatment received in hospital and collected data about antibiotics and symptomatic drugs use. We analyzed statistically these data and assessed the drug interactions. Results: Among the 75 inpatients with Alzheimer's Disease we evaluated, the vast majority was between 60-80 years old; 69% of patients are female (elders tend to metabolize drug slower than younger, men faster than woman). Most of patients had psychiatric and somatic comorbidities. Treatment for cardiovascular (beta-blockers: 1A2, 2C19, 2D6, 3A34, their level is increased by duloxetine; calcium channel blockers: 1A2, 3A34; antiarrythmics: 2D6, 3A34), endocrine (HMGCoA reductase inhibitors: 3A34; oral hypoglycemic agents: 2C9; insulin: 1A2) and gastrointestinal (proton pump inhibitors: 1A2, 2C9, 2C19, 3A34; H2 blockers: 1A2, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6, 3A34, increase level of venlafaxine and long acting benzodiazepines causing sedation) comorbidities were the most frequent. Smoking (1A2, 2A6, decreases olanzapine, benzodiazepine, duloxetine and propranolol levels, increases memantine levels), increased synthesis of HSP-70 and a decrease of NGF. GK-2 decreased hyperexpression of HSP-70 in cerebral cortex and did not affect NGF level. Ischemic conditions caused a decrease in cell viability in cortex (about 11%) compared with the sham-operated group, but not in hippocampus. GK-2 completely restored the viability of the cerebral cortex cells. Conclusions: The dipeptide GK-2 fully restores the viability of the cerebral cortex cells, decreases hyperexpression of HSP-70 in the cerebral cortex and restores impaired exploratory behavior in rats subjected to incomplete global ischemia.
