N2  - Purple Urine Bag Syndrome (PUBS) is an uncommon condition in which the urine drainage bags and tubing of catheterized patients become purple. It is hypothesized that for some patients with PUBS, a product of dietary tryptophan metabolism called indoxyl sulfate is decomposed by bacteria in urine with phosphatase and sulfatase enzymes. The breakdown products of indoxyl sulfate are indirubin (red) and indigo (blue), and the purple color has been found to be a mixture of indirubin dissolved in plastic and indigo on its surface. PUBS has been found to be associated with constipation, high urine bacteria counts, alkaline urine, institutionalization, the use of polyvinyl chloride catheters, and being female. Although urinary tract infections (UTIs) associated with PUBS are usually easily and routinely treated, this sign of bacteriuria may cause anxiety not only in patients and their families, but also in healthcare providers who are unfamiliar with this interesting condition. We present the case report of a chronically bedridden 89 yearold male nursing home resident with advanced dementia, benign prostatic hypertrophy, chronic kidney disease, hypertension, gastroesophageal reflux disease, hypothyroidism, depression, osteoporosis, osteoarthritis, anemia, GI bleed, fecal and urinary incontinence. He was in his normal state of health when his urine became malodorous and his foley bag and tubing changed from appearing yellow to bluegreen then purple over a period of four days. A urinalysis showed yellow slightly cloudy urine with a pH of 7.5, moderate leukocyte esterase, negative nitrite, 105 WBC/HPF, 16 RBC/HPF, and many bacteria. The patient was started on empiric treatment with cephalexin (based on prior urine culture results). Approximately 36 hours after starting cephalexin, the patient developed a low-grade fever prompting a switch to ciprofloxacin. The patient had no further fevers, and when the purple foley bag and tubing were replaced two days after initiation of antibiotics, the purple color did not reappear. Urine cultures showed mixed flora consistent with contamination. In this nursing home resident with dementia and urinary catheterization, PUBS was a sign of clinically significant bacteriuria.
