N2  - Objective:to describe the incidence,etiology and severity of surgical site infections (SSI) following hip fracture surgery in the elderly as well as the characteristics and in-hospital outcome of the patients who develop them Methods:prospective longitudinal study including 1738 consecutive patients older than 64 years with a hip fracture.Descriptive variables analyzed were:social-demographic characteristics,functional status,surgical characteristics,medical complications,mortality and LOS.SSI was defined according to CDC criteria.We studied its incidence, etiology,severity,antibiotic used,rate of isolation and surgical debridement Results:mean age:83.5±7.2 years.82% female.41% subcapital fractures.103 patients(5.9%)developed a SSI. 21% were deep infections. 32% occurs in the first week.They were caused more frequently by Pseudomonas aeruginosa(21%),E. Coli(21%) and S. aureus( 16%).53% were polymicrobial.Preventive isolation of the patient was needed in 20%,most of the time because of Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococci aureus(39%) and extended-spectrum B-lactamases bacterias(22%).Echography was performed in 37%.The antibiotics most used were quinolones(47%) and amoxicillin- clavulanic(20%)and a 16% received an antibiotic combination. 7% needed surgical debridement.When comparing characteristics between patients who developed a SSI(SSIp)and patients who didn't:no differences were seen in terms of age,gender,comorbidity or type of fracture.They differed in:time to surgery (14% of SSIp operated after 7 days,vs 8%,p=0.07),ASA score III-IV(63% of SSIp,vs 53%, p=0.05),medical complications(83% of SSIp,vs 73%,p=0.02)such as delirium(42% vs 32%,p=0.05),pneumonia(11 vs 5%,p=0.01),heart failure(11 vs 5%,p=0.02),pressure sores(15 vs 6%,p=0.01),depression(9 vs 3%,p<0.01),transfusion requirements(49 vs 39%,p=0.03)and reoperation(7 vs 0.6%,p<0.01).No differences were seen in mortality but SSIp had a higher length of stay (19 vs 11 days,p<0.05) Conclusions:Surgical site infection after hip fracture surgery is a frequent complication in the elderly.Most of the times,it is superficial and caused by gram-negative bacteria.Although patients who developed it don't differ in basal characteristics,they are in risk of having a worse in-hospital evolution,with a more complicated clinical course and a higher length of stay.
