N2  - Mr. S was a vibrant and independent 95 year-old man who entered hospice with metastatic neck cancer. Upon his diagnosis, he made clear that his goal was a comfortable and dignified death at home, with no antibiotics, radiation, hospitalization or other life-prolonging therapies. Six months later he was experiencing increasing pain and functional debility, intractable drooling, and newly depressed mood. His estimated life expectancy was weeks. Two days after a fall left him crawling into the hall for help, he took an overdose of morphine to end his life.When the hospice providers arrived to discuss interventions with Mr. S and his daughter, he declined hospitalization. His daughter wished to respect his decision, feeling it was in keeping with his long-stated preferences despite his recent depressed mood. The case of Mr. S demonstrates many of the risk factors for suicide at the end of life. Rates of suicide and accidental death are higher after a cancer diagnosis, with the greatest risk in older white men like Mr. S. However, confirmed suicide at the end of life is rare: e.g., 0.1% of deaths in Oregon. Treating depression and distressing symptoms may reduce thoughts of suicide, but the most common reasons cited by patients are difficult to ameliorate, like loss of dignity and independence. Suicide is usually an irrational act which obviates decisional capacity, but at the very end of life it may be a well-reasoned choice to reduce suffering. Mr. S's values and preferences helped define the meaning of non-maleficence and beneficence as they apply to the decision to decline hospitalization. A traumatic hospitalization was antithetical to his goal of a dignified, comfortable end to his life at home. He might have never regained a level of function acceptable to him, or have returned home at all. In this case, non-maleficence might mean avoiding the burdens of hospitalization. With only weeks to live, beneficence might mean permitting a desired manner of death rather than postponing death. Mr. S's apparent decisional capacity, consistent prior preferences, and surrogate's affirmation all bolstered his decision to remain at home. The hospice team ultimately agreed with Mr. S and his daughter, and provided comfort and reassurance to both. Mr. S died comfortably two days later. This illustrative case describes the risk factors for suicide at the end of life, and how preferences can help define nonmaleficence and beneficence in navigating an ethical course.
