N2  - Purpose: Nearly one-fifth of Medicare patients are readmitted to the hospital within 30 days of discharge. A national program, Project BOOST (Better Outcomes for Older adults through Safer Transitions) aims to target high risk populations for intervention prior to discharge to prevent readmissions. Project BOOST uses 7 criteria to identify patients at high risk for readmission. Hypothesis: There are certain patient factors that correlate with 30-day readmission rate and a cumulative set of factors is associated with an increased observed rate. Design: IRB approved retrospective chart review of hospitalizations in patients aged 65 years and older. Methods: The electronic medical record was used to review patient charts from March 1, 2009 through February 28, 2010. A random sampling of 146 of 1080 patient charts was reviewed. Inclusion criteria was age 65 years and older. Exclusion criteria included patients who left AMA or died during the index admission. Patient factors of interest were: problem diagnoses (CHF, COPD, acute pneumonia, acute stroke, diabetes mellitus, depression), problem medications (anticoagulants, insulin, aspirin plus clopidogrel, digoxin, opiates, IV antibiotics), polypharmacy (defined as >10 or more medications), prior hospitalization within 6 months, and discharge destination. Results: Of the 146 patient charts reviewed, 126 met inclusion and exclusion criteria and were included in data analysis. The overall readmission readmission rate was 18.2%. Of the 23 patients with a 30- day readmission, 65% had problem diagnoses, 74% had problem medications, 83% had polypharmacy, 35% had a prior hospitalization, and 26% were discharged to a location other than home. The readmission rate did not significantly differ between those with and without each of the above specific factors. Conclusions: The patient-specific BOOST criteria evaluated in our study did not correlate with 30-day readmission in this limited set of older adults. Future studies will use a larger data set to test our hypothesis.
