N2  - Background: A wide variety of substances are capable of causing systemic poisoning in humans after topical exposure. Pediatric patients are uniquely susceptible to transdermal absorption of toxins because of an increased surface area to body weight ratio, and premature infants are at even higher risk because of the possibility of compromised skin barrier function. Our objective was to review reported pediatric toxicity from cutaneous exposure to various agents. Methods: A PubMed search was carried out by crossing each of the terms “percutaneous,” “cutaneous,” “transdermal,” and “topical” separately with each of the terms “pediatric,” “child,” “poisoning,” and “toxicity.” Results: Pediatric deaths have been reported as related to percutaneous absorption of compounds such as alcohols, tetracaine/adrenaline/cocaine (TAC), salicylic acid, and organophosphates. Commonly used topical agents associated with systemic pediatric poisoning include alcohols, anesthetics, antihistamines, and antimicrobials and insecticides. Alcohols, including ethanol and isopropanolol, have been associated with percutaneous poisoning in children. Twenty-eight cases of ethanol poisoning in children aged 3 months to 1 year resulted in CNS depression, five in respiratory depression and two cases resulted in death. Among poisonings from topical local anesthetics, hypertension, and seizures were reported after application of a mixture of TAC to burns or mucous membranes. Of 77 children, 75% were also found to have excessively elevated serum cocaine levels. There are reports of pediatric deaths after topical exposure to salicylic acid. At least 13 fatal pediatric cases after percutaneous salicylate intoxication have been reported. Ten were in children less than 3 years of age, and symptoms were those of salicylism. In neonates and infants, salicylate intoxication has occurred inadvertently through application of teething gels to the gums. Conclusions: Although systemic poisoning by percutaneous absorption in children is less frequent than poisoning by ingestion, fatal, and/or near fatal outcomes have occurred in association with a number of compounds. Moreover, children and infants, especially newborns, are at increased risk of systemic toxicity via inadvertent topical exposure to myriad agents not intended to cause harm. An increased public and professional awareness of these risks is necessary to minimize the likelihood of acute accidental poisoning via skin absorption in the pediatric age group.
