N2  - Objective: To evaluate the effects of adalimumab (ADA) on health-related quality of life (HRQOL) and identify factors associated with sleep impairment in patients with psoriasis with suboptimal response to other systemic therapies or phototherapy. Methods: M10-238 trial, a 16-week, open-label study of ADA for patients with psoriasis (N = 152) with suboptimal response to etanercept (ETN), methotrexate (MTX), or phototherapy, was used. HRQOL outcomes at week 16 included: (1) changes from baseline in Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI), visual analog scale (VAS) for psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis pain, Work Productivity and Activity Impairment Questionnaire, and Medical Outcomes Study (MOS) sleep scale; (2) proportion of patients with improvement achieving minimum clinically important difference (MCID), defined as 5 points for DLQI and half the baseline standard deviation for other HRQOL outcomes; and (3) Pearson correlation between changes from baseline in sleep problem index II (SPI-II), a composite index based on nine of the 12 items in MOS sleep questionnaire, and other HRQOL measures, as well as the Psoriasis Area and Severity Index (PASI). In addition, linear regression was used to identify factors associated with worse baseline SPI- II scores. Results: ADA treatment significantly improved DLQI (5.2 points), VAS (16.9 points), overall activity impairment (8.4 percentage points), and SPI-II (5.2 points); all P< .01. Percentages of patients with improvement achieving MCID were 52.3% (DLQI), 44.4% (VAS), 36.7% (overall work impairment), 34.2% (overall activity impairment), and 38.4% (SPI-II). Changes in SPI-II were positively correlated with changes in DLQI (r = 0.47), overall work impairment (r = 0.26), overall activity impairment (r = 0.49), and PASI (r = 0.40); all P<.05. Psoriatic arthritis, depression, and a 1-unit increase in DLQI scores were associated with a 6.0-, 7.7-, and 1.0-point higher baseline sleep impairment, respectively; all P<.05. Conclusion: ADA treatment significantly improved HRQOL outcomes in patients with psoriasis with suboptimal response to ETN, MTX, or phototherapy. Substantial proportions of patients achieved improvement meeting or exceeding MCID values for HRQOL outcomes. Changes in the SPI-II correlated with changes in DLQI and overall work/activity impairment measures. Psoriatic arthritis, depression, and greater DLQI scores at baseline predicted worse sleep quality.
