N2  - Case Report: CASE: A 60-year-old women with a past medical history of heart failure, coronary artery disease status post bypass graft, COPD, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, depression, peripheral vascular disease, and tobacco dependency was admitted for health care associated pneumonia and was discharged on 3 week course of levofloxacin and linezolid. She returned to the emergency department 3 weeks later with diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, and altered mental status which had been progressing for 14 days. She did not have any chest pain, cough, increased shortness of breath, fever, or chills. Her medications include pregabalin, furosemide, carvedilol, fluticasone/salme-terol, ramipril, isosorbide dinitrite, rosuvastatin, and omeprazole. Vital signs were significant for blood pressure of 90/4, GCS score of 11, and ABG revealed metabolic acidosis. On exam, she had a mildly distended, non tender abdomen. She had No rash or bruising noted on skin. Her labs showed a WBC of 3.3/L, with normal differential count, hemoglobin of 8.8 mmol/L, and platelet count of 9000/L. She had sodium of 123mmol/L, creatinine 2.5mg/dl, bicarbonate of 11mmol/L, anian gap 23, lactic acid 8.7 mmol/liter, and mild transminitis. CT scan of her abdomen was suspicious for mild colitis but her C.diff toxin was negative. Her cultures were negative and antibiotics were discontinued. In the context of negative infectious disease workup, pancytopenia, screen hematology felt that the linezolid induced the pan-cytopenia and lactic acidosis. Linezolid was stopped. She received platelets, blood transfusions, empirical antibiotics, and continuous renal replacement therapy for lactic acidosis. After two weeks, her platelets, WBC, and hemoglobin were back to baseline values and her lactic acid normalized. DISCUSSION: Linezolid is an antimicrobial agent with potency against Gram-positive bacteria, including methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus au-reus and vancomycin-resistant enterococci. Time and dose-dependent reversible myelosuppression has been attributed to linezolid. There are many case reports of thrombocytopenia occurring with linezolid use, but only few cases of pancytopenia and lactic acidosis are reported. Physicians should be aware of this rare but serious adverse effect of linezolid.
