N2  - Background and question: Recent clinical and population based studies including a meta-analysis (data 1967-2008) demonstrated a relevance of proinflammatory cytokines and markers of inflammation for depression. Interestingly combinatory treatments of fluoxetine and celecoxib (COX-2 inhibitor) in patients with depression were superior to fluoxetine alone. Therefore we investigated the effect of willow bark extract (STW33-I), a known COX-inhibitor and cytokine modulator, on the behaviour of rats in the Porsolt swimming test (FST), a standard model for depression. Methods: Male CD rats (n = 12/group) were treated with STW-33-I (30 mg/kg) (group A) and its different fractions (30 mg/kg) (Groups B-E) for 14 days. The fractions (Fr) differed in their composition (Group B) rich in polyphenols + flavanoids, (C) rich in flavonoids, (D) rich in salicin, (E) rich in proanthocyanidines. Imipramine (20 mg/kg) (group F) was used as reference drug, one group remained untreated [1,2]. FST was performed on day 15. RNA of blood samples from rats was isolated. Samples of groups with significant results in the FST were further analysed for their gene expression (Agilent Rat whole genome microarray) in comparison to controls (n = 4 per group). Results: Groups A, D and F showed a significant reduction in the cumulative duration of immobility (p < 0.05). However, gene expression differed in these groups with group A showing the strongest gene regulation in comparison to the controls. The strongest regulated genes (>7-fold, p < 0.01) belonged to the extracellularmatrix, the fatty acid metabolism and theCNS(Gria 2, Olr 156). Cytokines and related receptors (IL-3ra, IL-13ra, IL- 18bp, IL-20rb, IL22ra) were significantly modulated to a lower extent. Conclusion: Data support the view that modulators of cytokines and inflammation play a role in the pathogenesis of depression. The potential of willow bark extract in the treatment of depression should be evaluated.
