N2  - Glycine receptor chloride channels (GlyRs) mediate inhibitory neurotransmission in the spinal cord, brain stem and retina. These receptors have recently emerged as potential therapeutic targets for inflammatory pain, temporal lobe epilepsy, tinnitus and spasticity. We are interested in understanding how drugs bind to these receptors and in identifying novel GlyR-active compounds as leads for therapeutic development and pharmacological tools for basic research. This presentation will focus initially on our attempts to identify novel lead compounds as anti-inflammatory analgesics. It will then focus on understanding the mechanism by which the anthelminthic, ivermectin, binds to and activates GlyRs and structurally-related nematode glutamate-gated chloride channel receptors. We describe how a glycine residue in transmembrane domain 3 is essential for high ivermectin sensitivity both receptors. We also show that ivermectin sensitivity can be conferred on an ivermectin-resistant receptor by introducing a glycine at this position. Furthermore, we show that comparison of amino acid sequences of ivermectin-sensitive and ivermectin-resistant receptors reveals that the presence of a glycine reliably predicts ivermectin sensitivity. This information provides insights into ivermectin binding mechanisms and should facilitate the identification of novel anthelminthic targets and ivermectin-resistance mechanisms. Finally, we describe the development of a minimally mutated human homomeric GlyR activated by nanomolar ivermectin but insensitive to glycine. We speculate this construct may be useful as a tool for silencing electrical activity in defined neuronal populations in vivo, and thus may useful clinically for treating neurological disorders including Parkinson's disease, epilepsy and depression.
