N2  - Background: Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is a rare life-threatening disease. The diagnosis must be evoked in a stroke patient which presents microangiopathic haemolytic anemia (MAHA) with thrombopenia. Aetiology is usually auto-immune. In reaction to an antigenic factors, the patient synthesis ADAMTS 13 antibody. The results are inhibition of the Willebrand factor multimers, platelet aggregation, micro-vessels occlusion and acute haemolytic anaemia. The herein observation describe for the first time the association of stroke, thrombolysis and TTP. Observation: A thirty year old female suddenly presented left side hemiplegia and inhalation. She had past medical history of depression, sinusitis, appendicectomia and allergic to antibiotics. MRI showed hyper intense sylvian rolandic territory signal on DIFFUSION and proximal M1 occlusion in TOF and FLAIR. Her neurological NIHSS was 14. She was administered rtpa 1 h28 after the onset and continuous trans-cranial doppler monitoring. Neurological restoration started 1 h after the beginning of the procedure. Five days after she had thrombopenia and developed MAHA . She was treated with plasma exchange and recovered rapidly. Immunological findings were positive for ADAMTS 13 antibody and dramatic decrease of the ADAMTS 13 protease activity. Discussion: TTP can be a rare cause of small vessels stroke. It has also been described as responsible of large vessels stroke. In this observation we discuss the fact that endothelial lesion induced by MCA occlusion could be responsible in combination with bacteriemia and thrombolysis of platelet activation leading to TTP. Conclusion: Auto-immuneTTP should be recognized as a possible pathophysiologic mechanism for unexplained post-thrombolysis thrombocytopenia and plasma exchange therapy initiated once the diagnosis is established.
