N2  - Hepatic encephalopathy(HE) pathogenesis is related to gut microbial products which lead to deranged cerebral bioenergetics. Biofluid MR spectroscopy (MRS) can be used to determine changes in bioenergetics, gut microbial products, amino acid and lipid metabolites.Lactulose is used as a first-line HE Rx despite a poor evidence basis. Aim: To evaluate the clinical and metabolic consequences of lactulose withdrawal in HE. Methods: Cirrhotics on lactulose underwent cognitive testing (inhibitory control(ICT),block design(BD),digit symbol &number connection tests A/B),urine & serum tests for MRS &inflammatory markers while on lactulose. Lactulose was then withdrawn &pts followed for 30 days with visits at day 2,14 &30 during which all tests were repeated.HE relapse was defined clinically. Multivariate analysis of urine &serum metabolites involved principal components & partial least squares discriminant analysis (PLS-DA). Univariate analysis was applied to hypothesis-driven metabolites, multivariate-driven metabolites and inflammatory marker concentrations. Results: 7 cirrhotic men(age 53yrs,5 HCV,2 alcohol) on lactulose for 6±5 mths for precipitated HE(5 GI bleed,2 infections) were included.3 patients clinically relapsed 38±6 days post-withdrawal; all 3 had >15 ICT lures while on lactulose. None of those who scored <15 ICT lures on lactulose relapsed. ICT Lure increase OR, 2.5(CI:1.7-3.6) & BD,OR 0.5(CI:0.3-0.8) were the only cognitive tests that predicted relapse. Using ICT lures >15 as a cut-off for relapse, MRS distinguished with a sensitivity &specificity of 70.0%/63.6% using urine & 100%/100% using serum on PLSDA. Urine PLS-DA: In relapsers, urine TMAO was reduced indicating altered gut bacterial metabolism while malonic acid & citrate were higher demonstrating impaired energetics. Glycine & phenylalanine, associated with false neuro-transmitters, were also increased along with creatinine. Serum PLS-DA: Relapsers had higher choline & its metabolite, dimethylglycine levels. This is associated with choline extrusion due to astrocyte swelling. Lipids(LDL) were also higher. Univariate analysis confirmed that relapsers had lower TMAO(p=0.003) & higher choline (p=0.04),LDL(0.0265)& creatinine(p=0.001). Inflammation was depressed in relapsers compared to others evidenced by decreased endotoxin(p=0.03),IFN-γ(p=0.04),IL-4(0.0002)& TNF-α(p=0.001) suggesting a compensatory anti-inflammatory response syndrome. Conclusions: HE relapse post-lactulose withdrawal is associated with derangements in bioenergetics, amino-acid, lipid & gut microbial metabolism as well as depression of the inflammatory response.
