N2  - Experimental studies using in vitro and animal models have shed light on the defect in innate host defense that clearly exists in the CF lung. A number of these studies support the hypothesis that abnormal epithelial ion transport leads to dehydration of airway surface liquid (ASL). This, in turn, is expected to impair the critically important mucus clearance defense mechanism. Data generated from human studies of mucociliary clearance (MCC) are important for the validation of the “dehydration/mucus clearance” hypothesis. Further, careful examination of these data is expected to refine our understanding of how CF lung disease progresses, and how we might effectively target defective mucus clearance therapeutically. The first question needing to be addressed is whether MCC is indeed abnormal in the intact, human system. A number of investigators have utilized the γ-scintigraphy method, which follows the disappearance of inhaled radiolabeled particles from the lung over time, to address this question (1). The collected, large CF experience reported by Robinson et al., suggested that MCC was indeed slower in CF (2). Even subjects with fully normal lung function had significant slowing of MCC, and clearance appeared to worsen with disease progression. Our laboratory has also compared MCC measured in healthy and CF subjects (3,4). Interestingly, our consistent observation from many studies is that MCC parameters felt to reflect large airway clearance (e.g., whole lung clearance through 60 minutes) are generally normal in CF. In contrast, MCC parameters that likely reflect clearance from small airways (e.g. peripheral lung clearance; clearance rate measured over 24 hrs), are clearly abnormal in CF. Importantly, measured MCC defects in CF are likely significant underestimates of the real problem. This results from the fact that the radiolabeled particles used to characterize MCC preferentially deposit in the most preserved airways, and are excluded from the most diseased and obstructed airways. We are now developing analysis tools that allow characterization of altered particle deposition in CF, including the formation of particle deposition “hot spots” and “cold spots” in CF. More importantly, we are also advancing our ability to characterize the heterogeneity of clearance within the lung beyond the basic central/peripheral delineation that has typically been used. Indeed, it appears clear that MCC in the CF lung is quite heterogeneous. We interpret these data to reflect the idea that the CF lung is able to maintain normal mucus clearance under most circumstances, but is vulnerable to insults that compromise MCC. The patchy distribution of MCC defects also suggests that disease progression reflects the accumulation of newly affected lung regions. Acute pulmonary exacerbations are major drivers of disease progression in CF, yet our understanding of what actually causes an exacerbation is quite vague. We tested the hypothesis that exacerbations result from regional collapse of MCC, perhaps in response to viral infection or another trigger that alters ASL homeostasis. In this clinical study, adult CF subjects were studied twice while clinically well to firmly anchor baseline assessments and to define the repeatability of our outcome measures. Subjects were then followed clinically, including monthly phone calls, to detect the symptomatic onset of a disease exacerbation using the criteria validated by Rosenfeld et al. (5). This very sensitive exacerbation definition identified subjects (N=12) prior to a significant decline in their lung function (-1.8 ± 2.6%) and before any change in quantitative bacterial load was observed. Despite the mild severity of the clinical syndrome, an approximately 1/3rd reduction in MCC was observed at this time point. Interestingly, each of these subjects eventually became ill enough to warrant treatment with IV antibiotics (treatment decision made by treating physician). Follow-up studies following resolution of the exacerbation were then carried out to define the pattern of MCC recovery. Of note, many patients failed to achieve a durable period of clinical stability after initial antibiotic treatments and suffered one or more exacerbation recurrences. Patients received an average of 30.2 ± 9.1 IV antibiotic days for 2.1 ± 0.7 treatment episodes between their baseline and recovery studies. At recovery, however, the Rosenfeld pulmonary exacerbation score had returned to baseline, and FEV1 was slightly higher than originally recorded baseline values. Despite this clinical improvement, MCC rates measured 33.7 ± 10.7 days following completion of acute antibiotic interventions remained significantly below baseline, and were not different than those measured at the start of the pulmonary exacerbation. Given that these patients had experienced many prior exacerbations and had relatively normal measures of large airway clearance at baseline, the persistent depression of MCC one month following treatment likely reveals a slow rate of recovery, rather than establishment of a new, lower baseline. Analysis of sputum samples collected at each visit throughout the study demonstrated that while subjects had evidence of significant secretion dehydration at baseline (as indexed by % solids), a further worsening during exacerbation was not observed. Interestingly, theconcentration of gel forming mucins in secretions did significantly increase at the time of exacerbation onset, and had returned to baseline at recovery, suggesting that this could turn out to be a relevant biomarker for CF lung disease. Hypertonic saline (HS) has recently entered the CF treatment armamentarium, based upon data that it acutely stimulates MCC in a dose responsive fashion (6) and reduces exacerbation frequency (7). Of note, recent in vitro studies that realistically simulate HS delivery to a CF airway suggest that the duration of the resulting ASL volume increase is quite limited in duration. We have used MCC studies to further reveal the nature of HS effects in vivo, and specifically to understand if indeed the stimulation of MCC is short-lived. Our in vivo data demonstrate that the acute stimulation of MCC after the first dose of HS does not demonstrate tachyphylaxis, via comparison to the peak/initial MCC rate measured after >50 doses given over 2 weeks. Interestingly, only a fraction of subjects appear to be “HS responders,” however, and responsiveness (or lack thereof) appears to be reproducible in individual patients. In our prior study of HS, we also showed that a sustained effect of HS on MCC, after repetitive dosing, could be detected 8-12 hours after the most recent dose (4). Interestingly, acute responsiveness to HS was strongly correlated with the sustained response observed after repetitive dosing. Most recently we've begun to determine the pharmacodynamic effect of a single HS dose, via a collaborative study (Johns Hopkins University; Dr. Beth Laube). Once again, a mixture of responders and non-responders were observed in this study. Unexpectedly, theMCCresponse measured either 1 or 4 hours after HS dosing was similar in magnitude to that measured 15 minutes post-dosing. Understanding this variable, but seemingly prolonged response to HS in CF is an important goal as new therapies aimed at correcting ASL dehydration and restoringMCCare being developed. Despite improvements with HS therapy, it is certain that a great deal of established disease is not reversed, and new approaches are needed. First, this and other hydrator therapies need to be tested much earlier in the disease process, ideally before any fixed disease has developed. The ongoing ISIS-02 trial of HS in infants should be very instructive in this regard. Second, the development of therapeutics with complimentary effects on MCC could logically be used in conjunction with a hydrator to gain additional benefits. Inhaled surfactants, which may reduce the adhesive and cohesive forces that promote mucus stasis in established CF lung disease, are one such “add on” candidate. The development of biologically relevant and valid outcome measures that accurately predict clinical benefits in patients is critical to the process of testing therapeutic concepts developed in the laboratory.We believe that theMCC assay will continue to be a valuable tool that allows us to test, compare and select promising newtreatment approaches while furthering our understanding of CF lung disease.
