N2  - Poor adherence to medications has been linked to worse health outcomes for people with cystic fibrosis (CF), including an increased number of pulmonary exacerbations and baseline lung function (1), but data are scarce about the relationship between level of adherence to a CF treatment regimen and health outcomes for both children and adults. Adherence decreases as children transition to adolescence and may remain low through emerging adulthood (2). As more individuals with CF enter adulthood and as additional chronic therapies are added to the regimen, it is increasingly important to identify factors that impede and facilitate adherence unique to this segment of the CF population. While overall selfreported levels of adherence range from 83-92% for oral antibiotics to 65-80% for inhaled antibiotics and 72-90% for mucolytic agents, objective medication adherence estimates for adults using pharmacy refill history and electronic medication monitors, are low, ranging from 31-79% regardless of the type of drug (antibiotic, mucolytic, pancreatic enzyme) (1, 3-8). There are many factors associated with nonadherence; some are similar to those found in pediatric samples while others are unique to being an adult with CF. As in pediatrics, knowledge of CF and its regimen is poor among adults with CF. One study found that the median score for adults with CF tested on CF knowledge was 59% (9). Misunderstandings of the prescribed regimen can put a patient at risk for unintentional nonadherence, although it is unclear whether knowledge is associated with adherence, particularly among adults with CF (10, 11). Treatment burden is one of the most commonly identified treatment barriers in CF (12, 13). In addition to being prescribed more therapies than their pediatric counterparts, adults with CF regularly describe the perceived inconvenience of the regimen such as time burden (to set up, do treatment and clean up) and the cumbersome quality of the devices (e.g., nebulizer, the VEST), and how it impacts their adherence. Outcome expectancies about therapy play a significant role in adherence. When lacking confidence in the treatment's efficacy, many people will not adhere to it. For example, in one study, half the patients were rated as nonadherent to airway clearance, but 67% of them felt they “did enough” and rationalized that they were well enough or not as sick as others to warrant doing it more often (14). Similarly, from our own qualitative research (15), we found that if someone with CF did not perceive any short-term benefit from treatments, he or she reported it difficult to adhere. Conversely, if someone perceived a therapy as having a positive health effect (doing a treatment relieved symptoms or failing to do therapy resulted in increased symptoms), he or she reported better adherence to the chronic regimen. Other patient beliefs, such as self-efficacy and motivation also affect adherence. In a pediatric CF study, selfefficacy was found to be a strong predictor of self-management behaviors (16). While it has not been examined in adults with CF, self-efficacy has been found to be an important mediator of change for many illnesses including diabetes self management (17), CPAP adherence (18), and smoking cessation (19, 20). We have found that motivation to adhere to therapy and the perceived importance of doing the therapy daily are associated with objectively monitored medication adherence among adults with CF (unpublished data). More traditional psychosocial factors, such as depression and social support, also affect adherence. Depression estimates are higher among adults with CF than children and adolescents and can range from 29% to 46% (21, 22). While not yet studied in CF, depression is a significant predictor of nonadherence in many chronic illness groups (23). Older adolescents and emerging adults often have dramatic changes in their social network, shifting away from the central role of parents in their day-to-day lives towards more emphasis on peers and romantic relationships. Relationships and social support affect adherence at a person-level. In our qualitative research, we found that a person with CF who has a strong social network or wellestablished social support often reports less difficulty following his or her treatment regimen; however competing social opportunities were noted to be barriers to adherence. In other illness groups, social support, particularly direct assistance, is associated with better adherence (24). The existing research suggests that patients with CF can identify many barriers to adherence, but there are little data on how these perceived barriers affect actual adherence behaviors. Moreover, there is a paucity of theoretically grounded research examining predictors of adherence among adults with CF. It is clear that improving CF knowledge is necessary, but unlikely sufficient to increase regimen adherence. Data on the efficacy of adherence promoting interventions, particularly ones targeting identified treatment barriers, among older adolescents and adults with CF, are also lacking. As there is now a critical mass of adults with CF, research attention is needed to identify behavioral and psychosocial factors that influence their adherence and health outcomes, and interventions to overcome nonadherence are critical.
