N2  - Currently available antidepressant agents, such as tricyclic antidepressants, MAO Inhibitors or SSRIs act primarily through monoaminergic systems in the brain. Although the pathophysiology of depression is not completely understood, it is generally recognized that monoamine deficiency or disruption is not the only pathway involved. Agomelatine is the first melatonergic antidepressant acting as a potent MT1 and MT2 receptor agonist with 5- HT2c antagonist properties. Blockade of 5-HT2c heteroreceptors on dopaminergic and noradrenergic neurons enhances release of dopamine and norepinephrine in the frontal cortex. This effect is specific, since it was not produced in the nucleus accumbens and it is not modified by melatonin agonists. Both properties contribute to the antidepressant activity of Agomelatine. In addition, Agomelatine lacks monoamines reuptake inhibitor properties and lacks the capacity to block receptors as adrenergic, dopaminergic, muscarinic or histaminergic, responsible for the therapeutic and adverse effects of clasical antidepressants. Agomelatine has also demonstrated strong antidepressant and anxiolytic properties and has shown to resynchronize altered circadian rhythms in several animal models. On the other hand, Depression with continuous stress induces: a) high excitatory amino acid and cortisol levels, which would behave as neurotoxic factors; b) a deficit of BDNF , which disappears as a protective factor. Agomelatin reverts these effects and increase cell proliferation, neurogenesis and survival of newly formed neurons in the hippocampal formation. The efficacy of agomelatine in major depression has been demonstrated at doses of 25-50 mg against the full range of symptoms present in patients with moderate to severe major depression. The effect of treatment increases with the severity of the disease. Agomelatine also rapidly regulates the sleep-wake cycle without causing sedation and improves daytime condition. Agomelatine has an excellent safety profile, is weight neutral, does not affect sexual functioning and does not cause discontinuation syndrome. In conclusion, the multiple benefits of agomelatine that stem from the synergy between the targeted MT1 and MT2 and 5-HT2c receptors provide a novel approach to the treatment of depression. Its clinical efficacy as antidepressant agent that improves clinical conduct, such as sleep-wake, and its anxiolytic properties, has been demonstrated.
