N2  - Introduction: Increased intestinal permeability associated with increased upload of lipopolysaccharides (LPS) translocation from Gram negative bacteria may induce depressive symptoms [1]. Circulating LPS is known to activate proinflammatory cytokines transiently expressed by glial cells in the brain [2]. A probiotic treatment (L. farciminis) suppresses stress-induced increase of intestinal paracellular permeability through an inhibition of epithelial cell cytoskeleton contraction and the subsequent tight junction opening and attenuates the activation of the HPA axis in response to an acute stress. Thus, we aimed to evaluate whether (i) acute stress induces changes in LPS translocation triggering central neuroinflammation which may be reversed by L farciminis (ii) the prevention of leaky gut is involved in these effects. Material and Methods: In a first series of experiments, female Wistar rats received orally L. farciminis (1011 CFU dayQ1) during 2 weeks or NaCl 0.9 %. After a partial restraint stress or sham-stress session, LPS level in portal blood and hypothalamic cytokine mRNA expression level were assessed using LAL QCL-1000 kit and relative quantitative RT-PCR respectively. In a second series of experiments, rats received before stress or sham-stress ML-7, a myosin light chain kinase inhibitor (1 mg kg-1; i.p.) administered three times at 12 h interval or unabsorbable broad spectrum antibiotics (0.5% neomycin, 1% ampicillin in driking water during 12 days) or vehicle. Plasmatic ACTH and corticosteronemia were assessed 15 min and 2 h after the beginning of the stress session respectively using Lincoplex kit. In distinct groups portal blood LPS concentration and hypothalamic IL-1b, IL-6, TNFα and CRH mRNA levels were assessed. Results: Partial restraint stress significantly increased LPS concentration in portal blood (9.4 ± 1.2 vs 5.3 ± 0.3 EU mL-1 in sham-stress group) and hypothalamic IL-1b, IL-6 and TNFα mRNA levels by respectively 2, 4 and 3 fold compared to control group. Both effects were significantly decreased by L. farciminis treatment. In stressed rats, ML-7 and antibiotics treatment reduced (i) LPS concentration in the portal blood (ii) ACTH and corticosterone plasmatic levels and (iii) hypothalamic IL1b, IL-6 TNFα and CRH mRNA levels. Conclusions: This study shows that attenuation of the HPA axis in response to acute stress and associated central neuroinflammation by L. farciminis treatment is mediated through a prevention of gut leakiness, and decrease of circulating LPS levels.
