N2  - Background: Vagus nerve stimulation (VNS) is approved for the treatment of epilepsy and depression in Humans. Anti-inflammatory properties of VNS have been described through an activation of vagal efferent cholinergic fibers (1). The aim of this study was 1) to evaluate the anti-inflammatory properties of VNS in a model of colitis in rats, and 2) to look for a brain activation following VNS. Methods: Colonic inflammation was chemically induced by intracolonic instillation of trinitrobenzensulfonic acid (TNBS) in rats (n = 12) chronically implanted with electrodes on the left cervical VN. VNS was performed in freely moving animals 3 h per day for five consecutive days, starting 1 h before instillation, with stimulation parameters (1 mA, 5 Hz, pulse width of 500 ls; 10 s ON, 90 s OFF; continuous cycle) known to activate vagal efferents. After 5 days of VNS, efficacy of VNS on colitis was evaluated by body weight loss measurement, scoring of macroscopic and histological colonic lesions, myeloperoxidase (MPO) quantification, and cytokine/cytokine-related mRNA assessment. A multiparametric index of colitis was generated, using both physiological and molecular parameters. Three other groups (n = 12 rats per group) were either non-stimulated/saline injected (controls), stimulated/saline injected (VNS/saline) or non-stimulated /TNBS injected (noVNS/TNBS). In parallel, we studied the central effects of 5Hz VNS in anesthetized rats using BOLD fMRI on a 4.7 T Bruker small animal MR system. Functional scans were acquired using an EPI sequence. In addition, we used Dynamic Causal Modelling (DCM) to estimate directed neural connectivity in a small brain network including the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) known to receive vagal afferents. Results: Vagus nerve stimulation induced a significant improvement of colitis with a decrease in body weight loss and colonic MPO, associated to an improvement of the multiparametric index of colitis. The brain was mainly deactivated in subcortical structures (e.g. the NTS). Furthermore, significant cortical deactivations were also observed in several cortices (e.g. retrosplenial). Model comparison of DCMs still allowed the identification of the NTS as the input nucleus of the VNS pathway to the brain. Conclusion: Vagus nerve stimulation chronically performed in freely moving animals significantly improves TNBS colitis and induces central activations in addition to its peripheral action. These experimental data argue for the possible use of VNS therapy in patients with inflammatory bowel diseases.
