N2  - Background: Accumulating evidence suggest that pathophysiology of depression might be associated with neuroinflammation, which could be attenuated by pharmacological treatment for depression [1]. The mechanism of anti-oxidative and antiinflammatory reactions from antidepressant medications has been found to associated with an enhancement of heme oxygenase-1 (HO-1) expression, which is strongly inducible in response to diverse stress-related cellular stimuli and is important in neuroprotection and neuroplasticity. Omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) are anti-inflammatory and play an important role in neurobiological mechanisms of depression [2]. The function of n-3 PUFAs in brain is diverse, but the mechanisms of antidepressant effects from n-3 PUFAs have yet to be elucidated. The aim of this study was to identify the molecular mechanisms through which docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), the main omega-3 PUFA in the brain, modulates oxidative reactions and inflammatory cytokine production in microglial cells. Methods: The murine BV-2 cells were prepared in Dulbecco's modified Eagle medium (Gibco, Grand Island, NY) and were treated with DHA and/or cytokines according to the experiment aims. Protein samples were measured with the Western blot analysis. Total RNA was extracted from BV-2 cells using a TRIzol kit (MDBio Inc., Taipei, Taiwan). Quantitative real-time PCR was performed using SYBR-green detection of PCR products in real time using the 48-well StepOne Real Time System (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA). The cells were prepared and visualized with a fluorescence microscope with Immunocytofluorescent staining. Results: The results of this study showed that DHA reduced expressions of tumor necrosis factor-α, interleukin-6, nitric oxide synthase, and cyclo-oxygenase-2, induced by interferon-γ, and induced up-regulation of heme oxygenase-1 (HO-1) in BV-2 microglia. The inhibitory effect of DHA on NO production was abolished by HO-1 inhibitor zinc protoporphyrin IX. In addition, DHA caused AKT and ERK activation in a time-dependent manner, and the DHA-induced HO-1 up-regulation could be attenuated by PI-3 kinase/AKT and MEK/ERK inhibitors. DHA also increased IKKα/β phosphorylation, IúBa phosphorylation, and IκBα degradation, while both NF-κB and IκB protease inhibitors could inhibit DHA-induced HO-1 expressions. Conclusions: The main finding of this study is that DHA potently inhibits oxidative reactions and proinflammatory responses in microglia, probably via promoting up-regulation of HO-1 expression. To our knowledge, this is the first report to demonstrate the role of HO-1 in the anti-inflammatory effects by DHA and to identify the molecular mechanism through which DHA modulates oxidative reaction and proinflammatory cytokine production in microglial cells. Firstly, the inflammatory stimuli of IFN-g increase the up-regulation of oxidative and proinflammatory mediators, including iNOS, COX-2, IL-6 and TNF-γ, which can be attenuated by DHA. Secondly, our results support previous reports showing that HO-1 is a critical regulator of NO production in numerous cells. In connecting with inflammation hypothesis of depression and clinical studies supporting the antidepressant effects of omega-3 PUFAs, this study provides a novel implication of antidepressant mechanisms of DHA.
