N2  - Purpose: Mood depression is very common in peritoneal dialysis patients. Peritoneal dialysis patients usually show increased prevalence of inflammation such as peritonitis and oxidative stress[1]. Inflammatory response and oxidative stress have been thought to be associated with pathophysiological mechanism of mood disorders[2], particularly in peritoneal dialysis patients[3]. This study was aimed to investigate the clinical features of mood depression, its symptom characteristics, and the associations of depressive symptoms with inflammation and oxidative stress in peritoneal dialysis patients. Method: Sixty-six peritoneal dialysis patients were included. The depressive disorders were diagnosed according to DSM-IVTR, and the depressive symptoms were assessed with Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HRSD). Psychiatric evaluation was made by a psychiatrist through semi-structured interview. Patients with depressive disorders (Major depressive disorder and Dysthymic disorder) were treated with escitalopram for twelve weeks. Clinical and inflammatory indices such as dialysis duration, number of peritonitis, high sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs CRP), and serum ferritin were evaluated. Oxidative stress markers such as levels of thiobarbituric acid-reactive substances (TBARs) as a marker of lipid peroxidation, superoxide dismutase (SOD), catalase (CAT), and glutathione peroxidase (GSH-Px) activities as antioxidants were measured. Results: Nineteen (28.8%) subjects were diagnosed as clinical depression (Major depressive disorder was 18.2%, Dysthymic disorder was 10.6%). Compared to non-depressed patients, depressed patients showed significantly higher symptoms of depressed mood, feelings of guilt, suicidal ideation, sleep disturbances, psychomotor retardation, agitation, psychic and somatic anxiety, lower levels of work and activities, gastrointestinal and general somatic symptoms, and hypochondriasis. Among depressed patients, six subjects rejected to take medication, two stopped medication arbitrarily within two weeks, one patient dropped out, and ten patients took escitalopram. However, HRSD scores in depressed medicated patients were not changed after twelve week antidepressant treatment. Inflammatory indices and oxidative stress markers were not different between patients with and without depressive disorders. However, there were significant positive correlations between HRSD score and number of peritonitis (r = 0.305, p = 0.013) and hs CRP (r = 0.250, p = 0.043), significant negative correlations between HRSD score and hemoglobin (r = -0.265, p = 0.031), hematocrit (r = -0.247, p = 0.045), plasma HCO-3 (r = -0.283, p = 0.021) and serum albumin level (r = -0.388, p = 0.010). Conclusions: Peritoneal dialysis patients have various depressive symptoms regardless the clinical diagnosis of depressive disorders. In this study, antidepressant treatment was not effective; however, it might because the subject size was too small. Clinical depressive disorders including Major depressive disorder and Dysthymic disorder were not related to inflammation and oxidative stress in peritoneal dialysis patients; however, depressive symptom severity was correlated with inflammation. These results suggest that inflammation would be influencing factors of depressive symptoms in peritoneal dialysis patients. Inflammation might be more important factor of mood depression rather than clinical diagnosis and features of depressive disorders. In other words, active management and prevention of inflammation would be helpful for reducing depressive symptoms in peritoneal dialysis patients.
