N2  - Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is an effective treatment against depression [1]. Serotonergic, noradrenergic and dopaminergic activity are known to be raised after ECT, but less is known about aminoacids. Taurine: Taurine is a b-amino acid important for the development and regeneration of the central nervous system, modulating the calcium homeostasis, membrane stabilization and regulation of the immune system. Taurine also acts as an neurotransmitter. In healthy men, there is an association between taurine in plasma and cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) [2]. Taurine in plasma and lymphocytes is elevated in depressed patients and are normalized after oral antidepressant medication. Deviating levels of taurine in CSF have been found in patients with pathological gambling and schizophrenia. Pre-treatment dopamine activity influences the response to ECT. Patients with already high base-line dopamine activity do not benefit from the ECT induced dopamine excess. Taurine may, through its action on dopamine, play a role in depression and have a antidepressant effect after ECT. The properties of taurine as an antioxidant compound could also play a role for the inflammatory aspect of depression, known as the cytokine hypothesis of depression [3]. Many antidepressants and ECT have anti-inflammatory properties. Purpose of the study: The present study examines the effect of ECT on plasma taurine level in patients suffering from depression. Since ECT needs several administrations to induce an antidepressive effects the study examined the effect after three ECT treatments. Methods: Fifteen patients undergoing ECT were recruited. Severity of depression was measured with the Montgomery- Asberg Depression Rating Scale (MADRS) at the time of each blood sampling. Fasting blood samples were collected before the first and after three ECT treatments. Plasma was separated from the sample and amino acids were separated using high performance liquid chromatography. Amino acids were detected by spectrophotometry. Summary of results containing real data and appropriate statistical assessments: Response of depression was defined as MADRS <15. After three ECTs there were six responders and nine non-responders. Using t-test for dependent variables it was shown that there was a drop in plasma taurine levels after three administrations of ECT for responders (p = 0.04), but not for nonresponders.(Table presented) Conclusions: In conclusion, plasma taurine levels drop in patients responding to ECT but not in patients not responding. Even if there are accumulating studies pointing out changing concentrations of taurine in plasma and CSF in several psychiatric diseases the role of taurine is still uncertain, especially regarding levels in response to treatment. Taurine is transported from the blood to the brain through a taurine transporter. A hypothesis is that the decrease in plasma taurine during ECT may be due to redistribution of taurine from blood to the brain, thus increasing brain taurine levels, possibly affecting positive reinforcement circuits for dopamine.
