N2  - Despite the availability of effective antidepressants for over 50 years, a substantial minority of patients with major depression fail to respond satisfactorily to treatment. Classical available antidepressants known to activate central monoaminergic function are associated with a delayed therapeutic response and unacceptable side effects that compromise effective treatment. As the psychopathology of depression, and the mechanisms of action of antidepressants, is largely unknown, it is questionable whether the current targets for antidepressants are adequate. Cognitive decline often remains unaffected by current antidepressants and may be associated with a dysfunctional glutamatergic circuits in limbic regions. Ketamine and riluzole appear to owe their efficacy to a reduction in glutamatergic activity in these regions. Drugs such as lithium act on intra-neuronal signalling cascades and enhance the synthesis of neurotrophic factors, such as BDNF, and activate the anti-apoptotic Bcl-2 gene. Future antidepressant targets could therefore include such intracellular processes that enhance neuroprotection (1). Limited attention has been given to the adverse effects of hypercortisolaemia and chronic low grade inflammation to the symptoms of depression and the associated physical ill health (2). Moderate clinical responses to mifepristone and celecoxib suggest that non monoaminergic targets should be considered in future. Hopefully a more holistic concept of depression will enable novel antidepressant targets to be identified. The most recent novel target includes the control of circadian rhythms. Agomelatine, the first melatonergic antidepressant combines uniquely the properties of agonism at melatonergic receptors and antagonism at 5HT2C receptor (3) and had demonstrated its antidepressant efficacy in clinical trials.
