N2  - Background: There is increasing concern on late effects of treatment during childhood for diseases like ADHD and auto-immune disorders. Due to a delay between exposure and the possible effect, these ADRs are difficult to recognize and probably underreported. Objectives: To investigate whether the WHO-UMC spontaneous reporting database is a valuable datasource to identify late effects. Methods: We used all non vaccine related paediatric ADR reports from the WHO-UMC until 2006 where information on both the onset date and the stop date of the drug was available. Psychoanaleptic drugs were one of the drug classes of interest. Results: In total 69.514 ADR records were available for analysis, of which 70% occurred prior to the stop date, 26% within 3 months and 4% after ≥3 months. Only 2% of the ADRs occurred ≥12 months after discontinuation of the drug. Within the first 3 months, ADRs were mainly reported on palivizumab (12%) and isotretinoin (4%). Most frequent ADRs <3 months were pneumonia (2.4%), and coughing (1.0%) after palivizumab. ADRs with later onset (≥3 months) were mainly reported for isotretinoin (38%) and infliximab (3%). Most frequent ADRs were (ulcerative) colitis (2.9%), depression (1.8%) and suicide attempt (1.2%), all in isotretinoin. Changing the time window of late effects from ≥3 months to ≥6, 12 or 24 months did not alter the results. For psychoanaleptic drugs 2,980 ADRs were reported, of which 30% occurred within 3 months and 4% after ≥3 months. Up to 3 months most ADRs concerned paroxetine (22%). Most frequent ADRs were suicide attempt (1.5%) in atomoxetine and withdrawal syndrome (1.4%) after paroxetine. ADRs after ≥3 months were mainly amphetamine associated (18%). Most frequent ADRs were aggressive reaction (3%) and suicide attempt (3%) after atomoxetine. Conclusions: The WHO-UMC database contains few reports on late effects in children. As late effects are difficult to recognize, medical record databases with long follow-up might be a better source to detect them.
