N2  - Hyperkeratosis of the nipple and areola is rarely reported in the literature. Various conditions have been implicated for its development, and cutaneous T-cell lymphoma can masquerade s its presentation. A 54-year-old woman was referred from the breast clinic with a 3-year history of progressive bilateral thickening involving skin of the nipples and areolae. A mammogram was normal. On examination there was rugosity andthe areolae seemed enlarged. The rest of the skin revealed no similar changes. Her past medical history included rheumatoid arthritis, hypothyroidism and depression. She was concerned s it was worsening although it was asymptomatic. A punchbiopsy was initially reported as a basal cell papilloma but in the light of history and clinical photographs, the histology was reviewed and further levels were done. It confirmed extensive hyperkeratosis, some follicular plugging and papillomatosis, and acanthosis giving the appearance of filiform architecture which has been described in hyperkeratosis of the nipple and areola. As she had not responded to emollients and 2% salicylic acid, Diprosalic® ointment (3% salicylic acid and betamethasone dipropionate 0.05%; Schering-Plough Ltd, Welwyn Garden City, U.K.) twice daily application was advised and within 6 weeks there was remarkable improvement. The left breast looked normal and only minimal residual changes were noted in the right breast. She was advised to use Epaderm® (Medlock Medical Ltd, Oldham, U.K.) as an emollient and remained clear for 6 months. Hyperkeratosis of the nipple and areola has been described as an extension of epidermal naevus in either sex affecting one of the breasts, in association with ichthyosis in both sexes but affecting both breasts, and a naevoid type predominantly in women in the second or third decade of life, usually unilateral. This condition can be seen in pregnancy and patients with acanthosis nigricans, Darier disease, ichthyosis, chronic eczema, and rarely in men receiving oestrogens for prostate carcinoma or on spironolactone. Verrucous thickening and brownish discoloration of the nipples and areolae which occurs predominantly in the second or third decade in female subjects and usually involving both breasts has been described as a distinct entity. Although our patient developed this much later in life, it would best fit with this description in the absence of any associated skin conditions and/or medications. A combination of topical steroids and salicylic acid can be added to the list of treatments already suggested for this condition which includes topical retinoic acid, topical calcipotriol, cryotherapy and carbon dioxide laser.
