N2  - Background: Psoriasis is a chronic disease with high incidence and prevalence and it has a great importance for public health due to the great social, economic and medical importance of this disease. Erythrodermic psoriasis is an extremely inflammatory type of psoriasis. Objectives: We would like to present options and therapy limits in a case of severe psoriasis. Methods: We present the case of a male, 42-years old, rural area, alcohol drinker, great smoker. From medical history we keep in mind the debut of psoriasis at 13 years old and from 2004 the patient is diagnosed with hepatitis C. The patient is hospitalized in our clinic after 2 weeks of the debut of this erythrodermic episode. Between 2001 and 2002 the patient presented few episodes of erythrodermia, in Italy, where the patient worked in construction field. In Italy was initiated the therapy with methotrexat. Patient, also, presented few episodes of depression. Topic and systemic therapy was inconstant and incomplete. General examination showed a patient with influenced state of health, extremely agitated, irritated, no medical compliance. Local clinical examination showed erythematous edematous skin associating continuously scaling. This scaling was accompanied by severe pain, itching, swelling. On the scalp patient presented continuously erythematous scales like a shell. Deep ulcerations, painful were on the hands and flexural areas; three important ulcerations on the abdomen, self-made, cover with purulent fluids. Paraclinical findings showed high level of white blood cells (= 22.610/mm3), high level of liver enzymes (ALT = 80U/L, AST = 62U/L, GGT = 411U/L), low level of blood iron (= 18 microg/dL). Patient also presented active hepatitis C. Results: We started the systemic treatment with medium doses of corti-costeroids (= 0.5 mg/kg c) and associated medication, antibiotics, antihis-taminic drugs, antidepressant drugs and local treatment with corticosteroids, calcipotriol, emollients. UVA was initiated at patient request but not tolerated. Conclusion: Erythrodermic psoriasis is characterized by long term evolution and frequent rebounds. Usually, for this type of psoriasis, the treatment includes methotrexat, cyclosporine, aromatic retinoizi, biological therapies, mycophenolat mophetil. The only suitable therapy for our patient was systemic corticosteroids. Medical evolution was slowly favorable because the patient did not respect the periodic evaluation, and the hepatic condition aggravated the disease, also, the depressant episodes were not controlled by appropriate drug therapy. The exitus of patient happened few days ago.
