N2  - Background: We examined psychic and emotional conditions in patients with diagnosis of ''local psoriasis'' before and after therapy of topical glucocorticosteroids (GKS), containing calcipotriolum or salicylic acid. Objectives: Fifty-six patients were divided in two equivalent groups. Topical medicine of the first group was Daivobet (betamethasone + cal-cipotriolum). Patients of the second group got medicine that contained mometasoni fuorati and salicylic acid. Methods: Psychometric indicators before treatment were investigated by Dermatological Life Quality Index (DLQI) and Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS). DLQI points were high in all observations making up 11.3 ± 1.1 on averarage, testifying the significant decrease of patients quality of life. Depression scale of HADS showed 9.2 ± 0.4 average points that testified clinical symptoms of depression in 52% of patients. Results: Next investigation, carried out interatively two weeks later found significant difference in results between two groups of patients. First group treated with GKS and calcipotriolum (Daivobet), demonstrated 7.6 + 1.4 average points of DLQI, while the second group receiving GKS and salicylic acid (mometasoni fuorati and salicylic acid) demonstrated 9.8 + 1.6 points of wthis scale on average. Conclusion: We assosiated this with faster regress of rush and infiltration in patients applying calcipotriolum.
