N2  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: Recognize the clinical presentation of the Drug Rash with Eosinophila and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: CASE: A 19 year-old female with depression presented to her local emergency department with fever and rash four weeks after starting lamotrigine. Three weeks after initiation of the drug, she noted fevers followed by a pruritic rash on her chest and back. Lamotrigine was discontinued, but the fevers persisted and the rash continued to spread. Upon presentation, she was tachycardic with a temperature of 103 0F. Labs were notable for a white blood cell count of 13,000 cells/mm3 with 3% eosinophils, 20% bands, and many atypical lymphocytes. An abdominal CT scan showed retroperitoneal and inguinal lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and ascites. Blood cultures were drawn and Ceftriaxone was started. Four days later the patient was transferred to our institution. The patient arrived with an erythematous blanching maculopapular rash covering her face, neck, trunk, back, and all four extremities, but sparing her mucous membranes. She had significant facial and periorbital edema. She had a white blood cell count of 23,000 cells/ mm3 with 17% bands, 5% atypical lymphocytes and 10% eosinophils, transaminitis, and hyperbilirubinemia. Cerebrospinal fluid studies were normal. Heterophile, toxoplasma, human immunodeficiency virus, and autoimmune antibodies were negative, as were Epstein-Barr virus and cytomegalovirus viral loads. All blood cultures remained negative. Over the ensuing twelve hours, the patient became critically ill. A diagnosis of DRESS was made and intravenous methylprednisolone was started with clinical improvement. The methylprednisolone was subsequently tapered, and the patient was later discharged on a twelve week prednisone taper. DISCUSSION: DRESS syndrome is a rare drug reaction characterized by cutaneous eruptions, hematologic abnormalities, evidence of systemic inflammation, and visceral organ involvement. Proposed criteria define the syndrome as having a rash, eosinophilia or the presence of atypical lymphocytes, and evidence of systemic involvement defined as adenopathy >2 cm, hepatitis, interstitial nephritis, interstitial pneumonitis, or carditis-all following administration of a drug. It is most commonly seen in patients on aromatic anti-convulsants, but it is well described with lamotrigine as well as allopurinol, doxycycline, minocycline, and sulfa-based drugs. Younger adults are at increased risk of developing DRESS in response to lamotrigine, and the syndrome has been reported to occur anytime between 2 weeks and 6 months following initiation of the drug. As highlighted by this case, although DRESS is a drug reaction, the syndrome can worsen for weeks even after prompt discontinuation of the offending agent. It can progress to frank shock and is reported to have a 10-40% mortality. For severe cases, steroids and intravenous immunoglobulin have been used with success, usually with a slow and prolonged steroid taper to prevent disease relapse, although not all cases are steroid responsive. Early recognition and vigilant monitoring following withdrawal of the offending agent are critical to managing this often delayed and potentially fatal syndrome. As more patients are using anti-convulsants for a wide array of conditions, general internists will be in a position to recognize DRESS early and positively influence its clinical course.
