N2  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: Demonstrate the presence of the 3rd party provides an additional challenge to geriatric patient care LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: Reinforce elderly patients often present atypically with classic conditions CASE: A 71-y/o female with a chief complaint of one month of generalized malaise, presented to our clinic accompanied by her daughter. She denied fevers or chills, described low energy levels, fatigue, and decreased appetite. She admitted to occasional loose stools and intermittent abdominal pain. The pain was not associated with food intake or bowel movements and there were no particular exacerbating or alleviating factors. Her daughter stated that her mother's abdominal complaints had been present for a long time and were not her primary problem. Her daughter felt the patient was exaggerating her abdominal complaints and urged us to look elsewhere for a cause of her malaise. Physical exam revealed a pale woman in no acute distress. She was afebrile and normotensive. There was no lymphadenopathy; lungs and cardiac examination were unremarkable. Abdominal exam revealed a midline scar from her previous partial colectomy and a small hard area lateral to the scar there was no guarding or rebound tenderness on deep palpation. We drew a CBC, complete metabolic panel and TSH and collected a urine sample. We screened her for depression and urged her to consider talk therapy if her mood remained poor. We advised her to stay wellhydrated, and asked she follow-up the next week if she did not feel better. The following day her CBC revealed a leukocyte count of 28 with left shift. The patient was instructed to go to the ED. In the ED her exam remained unchanged, but abdominal CT scan revealed acute diverticulitis complicated by two abdominal abscesses. She was started on antibiotics and her abscesses were drained with pigtail catheters placed by interventional radiology. Her leukocytosis subsequently resolved and she was discharged home in good condition. DISCUSSION: We discuss this case because it is a near miss. Indeed, common diseases present uncommonly in the elderly, often with constitutional symptoms obscuring focal problems. Studies have shown that elderly patients are less likely than younger patients to present with acute chest pain when having heart attacks and are more likely to present atypically. Diverticulitis is well-established as a difficult diagnosis because of the low sensitivity and specificity of findings on physical exam and history alone. Evaluation of the geriatric patient may be further complicated by the presence of a third party. It's been estimated 15 - 20% of geriatric patients are accompanied by a caretaker. The presence of this individual can provide useful corroborative information. However, as in our case it can detract from a patient encounter and obscure a patient's chief complaint. In our situation we were influenced by the daughter to not pursue her abdominal symptoms more aggressively. The daughter downplayed the abdominal pain and encouraged us to focus elsewhere. Studies have shown assistance with the elderly is invaluable to their health. Often times they will not be able to maintain regular doctor's appointments without the assistance of others. It has been also noted as in our example that the presence of a 3rd party can obscure the process of history taking with caretakers often speaking over and interpreting the symptoms of the patient to the detriment of the patient. Here and as previously indicated in the literature an obtrusive caretaker may distract from obtaining an accurate history and physical thus politely asking them to leave the room during initial patient assessment may be beneficial.
