N2  - LEARNING OBJECTIVE 1: 1) Identify the clinical and laboratory findings consistent with toxic alcohol ingestion. LEARNING OBJECTIVE 2: 2) Differentiate between the 3 major types of toxic alcohol ingestion (ethylene glycol vs. methanol vs. isopropyl alcohol) using anion and osmolal gap calculations. CASE: A 62-year-old man with a history of alcohol abuse was discovered by his daughter unconscious in his apartment; three empty 100 cc bottles of rubbing alcohol (isopropyl alcohol) were at his side. In the ED, he was lethargic with a poor recall of events. Vitals signs were stable. Mild confusion was noted; otherwise the physical exam was normal. Since co-ingestion was possible, the patient was empirically treated with fomepizole and intravenous normal saline while laboratory tests were pending. The anion gap was mildly elevated at 17. The osmolal gap was 40, and the creatininewas 2.0mg/dL; an arterial blood gas demonstrated a mild metabolic alkalosis; and the urine toxicology screen was negative for ethanol but showed the presence of ketones. The EKG was within normal limits. The lack of a significant anion gap acidosis made co-ingestion of methanol and ethylene glycol unlikely, so fomepizolewas discontinued. The patient was followed for signs of alcohol withdrawal. His mental status improved and creatinine as well as the anion and osmolal gaps returned to normal without any further interventions. Mental health services were consulted to address the patient's alcohol abuse. DISCUSSION: Isopropyl alcohol is found in disinfectants, antifreeze, solvents, and comprises 70% of rubbing alcohol. Like ethanol, its ingestion results in CNS depression. Similar to the other toxic alcohols, ethylene glycol and methanol, it is toxic at low amounts with only 250 mL considered a lethal dose. The increased toxicity of these alcohols results from their metabolism by alcohol dehydrogenase to toxic metabolites. Isopropyl alcohol is converted to acetone, which can cause marked ketosis, but not a severemetabolic acidosis. Incontrast, ethylene glycol and methanol are both metabolized to carboxylic acids, which significantly increase the anion gap. Ethylene glycol is metabolized to glycolic and oxalic acid, of which the latter precipitates as calcium oxalate, leading to acute kidney injury and organ damage.Methanol ismetabolized to formaldehyde and formic acid, producing retinal damage. Early diagnosis and identification of causative agents can prevent irreversible organ damage. Arterial blood gas analysis should be performed and anion and osmolal gaps calculated. Mental status assessments and testing for common co-ingestions (ethanol, acetaminophen, salicylate) are essential. A defining characteristic of toxic alcohol ingestion is the presence of an osmolal gap resulting fromthe addition of uncharged and osmotically active alcoholmolecules to the plasma. The anion gap allows differentiation of the causative alcohols: isopropyl alcohol produces ketones but no acids, while ethylene glycol and methanol produce aldehydes and acids. Thus, only an osmolal gap is present in isopropyl alcohol poisoning, while both a significant anion gap acidosis and osmolal gap appear with ethylene glycol and methanol poisoning. Differentiation is important for treatment. Isopropyl alcohol ingestion is treated supportively, while ethylene glycol and methanol ingestions necessitate the use of fomepizole (a competitive inhibitor of alcohol dehydrogenase) to prevent formation of toxic metabolites, gastric decontamination, and management of metabolic acidosis. In summary, toxic alcohol intoxication should be considered and the causative agent identified in any patient presenting with an altered mental status and osmolal gap, as ingestion-specific treatments can prevent irreversible organ damage.
