N2  - Malaria infection is accompanied by increased production of Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) that are produced both by the parasite and its host as well. This production of ROS leads to the induction of an oxidative stress on the host cells. In the other hand, pro-oxidants that are found among xenobiotics such as artemisinin and its derivatives and foods components potentially cause vascular damages to the host cell. These biochemical injuries caused by oxidative stress represent a key factor in the physiopathology of malaria. The measurement of the activity of antioxidant enzymes as well as the status of antioxidant vitamins may give the possibility of a specific prevention of malaria based on nutrition. The present investigation was therefore undertaken to determine the extend of lipid peroxidation and to investigate the alteration in antioxidant enzymes Superoxide dismutase (SOD) and Catalase as well as in some major antioxidant vitamins E and C in Plasmodium falciparum malaria infected pregnant women. Plasma Thiobarbituric acid reactive substances (TBARS) were quantified as malondialdehyde level to assess the degree of lipid peroxidation. Activities of antioxidants enzymes SOD and catalase were measured and the Total antioxidant capacity (TAC) was estimated to determine the status of the antioxidant vitamins E and C. Plasma MDA level was significantly increased in malaria patients compared to controls. Activities of antioxidant enzymes SOD and catalase as well as the antioxidant vitamins E and C were decreased significantly in malaria patients when compared to healthy controls. The general depression in antioxidant levels in malaria patients suggest that nutritional improvements of antioxidant capacities may be a therapeutic strategy to prevent the occurrence of oxidative stress and thus the severity of malaria and therefore, may help save some of the 2 million lives lost to malaria in sub-Saharan Africa every year.
