N2  - Aim: Analize the etiology factors and treatment of dipnoea in a patients treated in Hospice-Sue Ryder -care in Macedonia in end of life. To show that dyspnoea in patients with terminal disiese is caused from many etiology factors, local and systematic, and is a bad prognosis symptom at the and of life. To show that is it the most difficult symptom for medical staff to improving the quality of life for the patients. Methodology: 150 patients were included. The following variables were studied: age, sex, kind of terminal disease, location of cancer, etiology factors of dipnoea and the best possible treatment to improve the quality of life of this patients. In our measuring scale, dispnoea is being measured with other common cancer symptoms, specialy emotional status. Results: The most patients were with cancer-88% (lung cancer-59%, lumphangitis carcinomatosis-12%, pleural effusion-9%, SVC obstruction-5%, radiation-induces pulmonary fibrosis-3%), 12% of the patients were patients with COPD and heart failure in terminal stage. Sumpomatic treatment included: oxygen if patient is hipoxic SaO2 < 90%,bronchodilatators, corticosteroids, antibiotics, diuretics, massage, aromatherapy, position,morphine oral or in nebulized form and in any cases lorazepam 1mg. sublingual. Conclusion: Patients with dispnoea died more frecuently in hospital or in hospice.The survival analisis identified patients without dipnoea had more life expentancy. Hypoxemic patients and thos who had low levels of haemoglobin showed higher probability to suffer dyspnoea. Ther is a cases in which opioids can relieve the dipnoea before respiratory depression occurs. Only in patients with severe ventilatory failure such as advanced COPD this therapeutic possibility is very narrow. It is very difficult for many professionals in our country to accept the use of opioids for dispnoea in terminal stage and it is barrier to good assessment of dyspnoea.
