N2  - Aim: The Asia-Pacific study in Patients to be treated with Etanercept or an Alternative Listed DMARD (APPEAL) compared the efficacy and safety of etanercept (ETN) + methotrexate (MTX) vs. usual disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARD) + MTX in subjects with moderate-to-severe rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Methods: In this randomized, open-label, parallel-design, multicenter study, subjects (n = 300) with moderate-to-severe RA (DAS28 at least 3.2; mean disease duration 6.7 years; no bony erosion or positive rheumatoid factor required) and inadequate response to MTX (7.5-25 mg/wk) received ETN (25 mg twice weekly) + MTX (n = 197) or DMARD + MTX (n = 103). For DMARDs, investigators could choose sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine, or leflunomide; MTX was the same as their screening dose (mean 12.9 mg/wk). Various clinical and patient-reported outcomes were analyzed 16 weeks after treatment. Results: The American College of Rheumatology response (ACR-N) AUC (primary outcome) was significantly greater for the ETN + MTX group vs. the DMARD + MTX group at week 16 (mean difference - 145.3; P < 0.001). The Health Assessment Questionnaire improvement from baseline to week 16 was significantly greater (P < 0.05) with ETN + MTX (baseline: 1.37, week 16: 0.69) compared with DMARD + MTX (baseline: 1.41, week 16: 0.87). Other health outcomes improved more from baseline to week 16 in subjects receiving ETN + MTX vs. DMARD + MTX including the Short Form-36 Physical (P < 0.01) and Mental (P < 0.05); Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale for depression (P < 0.05) and anxiety (P < 0.05); and Work Productivity and Activity Impairment Questionnaire: General Health (WPAI : GH) activity impairment (P < 0.001) and WPAI : GH overall work impairment (P < 0.01). No new safety signals with ETN + MTX were reported. Conclusion: In this Asia-Pacific population of subjects with moderate-tosevere RA, ETN + MTX showed superior efficacy and improvements in patient-reported outcomes vs. usual DMARD + MTX regimens, with similar safety profiles. Thus, ETN may help address the unmet need for effective RA therapy in this region.
