N2  - RATIONALE: Exacerbations are the primary drivers of health care resources and costs in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). The purpose of this study was to estimate costs of COPD-related inpatient admissions (IP) and emergency department visits (ED). METHODS: Retrospective cross-sectional study using inpatient administrative data for 2008. Four cohorts were defined: a) ED only; b) hospital outpatient; c) standard IP admission; and d) severe IP (with/without intensive care unit (ICU)). Patients aged >40 years at time of admission were included with primary discharge diagnosis of COPD (ICD codes 491.xx, 492.xx, 496.xx). Outcomes of costs, LOS (episode level) and readmission rates (subject level) were analyzed for all available years of data to capture trends across the years. RESULTS: of COPD-related admissions (n=85,789) in 2008, 49.8% were standard IP, 28.7% for ED visits, 16.7% for hospital outpatient, and 4.8% for severe IP. Patients mean age 67 years (59% >65 years), and 56% female. Main comorbidities were cardiovascular (45%) and depression (17%). Average bed size was 361 and 30% of hospitals were hospitals with teaching status. Overall, average admission cost was $4,856 with length of stay (LOS) of 5 days for hospital admissions. The average costs per admission were: ED visit $647, outpatient $366, and standard IP admission $7,242. Mean costs of severe IP ($20,757; LOS=9 days) broken down into: ICU with intubation (n=838) $44,909; ICU without intubation (n=3,015) $13,203; and intubation but no ICU (n=289) $29,530. Average COPD and all-cause IP readmission rates (entire sample) within 60 days of the index admission were 5% and 13% respectively. Respiratory medications accounted for 35% of total pharmacy costs. Overall, medical costs (89%) accounted for majority of total costs for COPD-related admissions in 2008 compared to pharmacy costs (11%). While hospitalized, patients received: 44% short acting anticholinergics, 15% long acting anticholinergics, 17% combination inhaled steroid and long acting beta agonist, 65% oral steroids, and 55% antibiotics CONCLUSIONS: COPD exacerbations (ED and IP) result in substantial COPD-related health care costs. Majority of total costs for treating a COPD exacerbation are medical costs as opposed to medication costs. (Table presented).
