N2  - RATIONALE: Cystic Fibrosis (CF) is the most common lethal inherited disease in Caucasians, with a current median survival of 37 years. As the CF population has aged, a number of chronic health problems have emerged, including diabetes mellitus and osteoporosis. Previous studies have suggested that pain is also common in CF. However, little is known about the factors associated with pain in CF, and its impact on clinical outcomes. We hypothesized that pain in CF is common, is associated with psychological distress, and adversely affects clinical outcomes. METHODS: Adults with CF were emailed and asked to complete an online survey. Of the137 patients contacted, 83 responded to the survey (61% response rate). The questionnaire included the Brief Pain Inventory, Pain Catastrophizing Scale, Cystic Fibrosis Questionnaire-Revised, Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale, and the Medical Outcomes Survey Sleep Questionnaire. Clinical outcomes, including acute pulmonary exacerbations, FEV1, and deaths were assessed through chart review for the 12 months following survey completion. Bivariate analyses were performed using Wilcoxon log rank tests, Kruskal Wallis tests, and Spearman correlations. Logistic regression models and Cox proportional hazard models were used to analyze clinical outcomes including exacerbations and death. RESULTS: The median age was 29.3 years (range 19-71) and 56.6% were female. The median FEV1 was 63.6% predicted (27.6 - 134.0%). The majority of patients reported pain within the last 30 days (82%) and many reported current pain (44.6%) and moderate-severe pain.(29%). This pain interfered with general activities (41.9%), mood (56.8%), and work (47.3%). The most common pain site was the head (63.5%) followed by sinuses (54.0%), back (50.0%), chest (45.9%), and abdomen (33.8%). FEV1 and gender were not significantly associated with the presence of pain, while there was a trend for patients with pain to be older (mean age 34.6 vs. 29.7 years, p=0.06). Depression and anxiety scores were significantly higher in patients with pain in the last 7 days (Depression=6.2 vs. 4.9 p=0.03; Anxiety 8.3 vs. 6.9, p=0.04). The risk for pulmonary exacerbations treated with intravenous antibiotics was increased in patients with higher levels of pain, after adjusting for FEV1 (OR=1.36, p=0.03). Five patients died during follow and the risk of death was higher in patients with higher average pain scores (HR=1.61, p=0.01). CONCLUSIONS: Pain is common in patients with CF, interferes with common activities and increases the risk of CF exacerbations and death.
