N2  - INTRODUCTION: COPD exacerbations are associated with an increased risk of hospitalization. Our objective was to study the distribution and descriptors of COPD patients hospitalized for their exacerbation (=severe exacerbations) using one year follow-up data in the ECLIPSE cohort. METHOD: ECLIPSE is a 3-year observational study of COPD natural disease history (Vestbo et al ERJ 2008). Exacerbations were defined as a condition that required treatment with antibiotics (ATB) and/or oral corticosteroids (OC) (moderate exacerbation) and/or specific hospitalizations (severe exacerbation). This analysis utilises data collected during the first year of follow-up (Y1). Baseline subject characteristics were used to describe specific groups. RESULTS: 2,154 COPD patients were included in the analysis, of whom 14.5% experienced at least one severe exacerbation during Y1 (N=313). Most of these patients (8%) had only one hospitalization. Hospitalisation for exacerbation was associated with an increase in GOLD stage. Table 1 provides a comparison of selected descriptors in patients (1) without any exacerbations, (2) with at least one moderate exacerbation only and (3) with at least one hospitalised exacerbation. Patients with at least one severe exacerbation had lower BMI, worse lung function, walked less and had more emphysema. They experienced lower quality of life and reported more symptoms (respiratory symptoms, fatigue, depression). Their BODE score was significantly higher, but the age and current smoking prevalence were similar. Exacerbations in the 12 preceding months were substantially increased (p<0.001) in those with severe exacerbation (73%) as compared to patients with moderate exacerbation only (59%) or no exacerbation (28%). CONCLUSION: Patients with severe COPD exacerbations are more likely to have advanced disease, impaired quality of life and suffer with depression and fatigue. (Table presented).
