N2  - Background: The EASiQoL is a measure of quality of life reflecting outcomes identified as important by ankylosing spondylitis (AS) patients. It is reliable and valid in a UK population. To be useful in routine practice or trials, a measure should also be able to reflect any changes in individual's health (responsiveness). This study compares the responsiveness of the EASiQoL with other measures commonly used in AS patients; evaluating how these measures assess improvement in patients with normal variation in disease activity and in patients following DMARD/biologic initiation. Methods: 1000 AS patients from 10 UK secondary care rheumatology centres were sent a postal questionnaire including the EASiQoL at 0 and 6 months. The EASiQoL has 20 items and four domains: disease activity, physical function, social participation and emotional wellbeing. The responsiveness of the EASiQoL index and each domain were compared with: AS Quality of Life measure (ASQoL), Bath AS Disease Activity Index (BASDAI), Bath AS Functional Index (BASFI), Short Form 36 (SF-36) physical function, social function and mental health subscales and Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS). Self-reported disease transition was assessed at 6 months with patients reporting no change, deterioration or improvement in ASrelated health. Study 2: a separate cohort of 44 AS patients participating in a randomized trial of etanercept 50mg weekly (ETN) versus sulphasalazine 2 g daily (SSZ) completed the EASiQoL and ASQoL at baseline and after 38 weeks of treatment. Responsiveness was assessed using effect size. Effect sizes over 0.5 are regarded as moderate and over 0.8 as high. Results: Study 1: 612 (64%) patients completed the baseline postal questionnaire (71.6% male; mean age 50.8 (S.D. 12.2) yrs; mean disease duration 17.3 (S.D. 11.7) yrs). The Table shows the effect sizes in patients experiencing an improvement in health at 6 months. Study 2: 23/29 patients on ETN and 12/19 patients on SSZ completed all questionnaires. Responsiveness for the EASiQol ranged from 1.4 (emotional well-being) to 2.8 (disease activity) for the ETN group and from 0.6 to 0.9 for the SSZ group. The EASiQoL total score was more responsive than the AsQoL for the ETN group (2.3 vs. 1.7) and of equal responsiveness for the SSZ group (both 0.8). Conclusions: The EASiQoL index score and the individual domains have comparable or superior responsiveness to treatment in a clinical trial of ETN and SSZ and in patients reporting improvement in their AS after 6 months, than other more established measures currently used in AS. The EASiQoL is recommended for use in evaluative research in AS, including clinical trials. (Table Presented).
