N2  - Cancer patients who receive doxorubicin, a common chemotherapy drug, exhibit decreased maximal inspiratory pressures. This decrement suggests diaphragm weakness, which may cause dyspnea and exercise intolerance. Previous data from our laboratory shows that doxorubicin-induced limb muscle weakness involves TNF/TNFR1 signaling, leading us to hypothesize that doxorubicin causes diaphragm muscle dysfunction through TNF/TNFR1 signaling. We injected C57BL/6 male mice intravenously with doxorubicin (20 mg/kg), a dose equivalent to chemotherapy given to lung cancer patients. Diaphragm bundles were excised three days post injection. Compared to controls, doxorubicin lowered maximal force in the diaphragm by 23 ±; 4 % (mean ±; SE; n= 11, p<0.05). TNFR1 receptor-deficient mice injected with doxorubicin (20 mg/kg) were protected against the depression in specific force (TNFR1; dox: 21 ±; 2 N/cm2, control: 22 ±; 1; n= 8/group). We tested whether a clinically available anti-TNF drug, etanercept, could prevent diaphragm weakness. Mice were injected with etanercept (5 mg/kg), a soluble TNF receptor, in combination with doxorubicin (20 mg/kg). Etanercept prevented the depression in force caused by doxorubicin (etanercept + dox: 22 ±; 2 N/cm2, dox: 16 ±; 1; n= 5/group). Our data shows that doxorubicin causes diaphragm muscle weakness, an effect mediated through TNF/TNFR1 signaling.
