N2  - Background:The French neurologist Jean M. Charcot characterized acute cholangitis, infection of the biliary tract, as a triad of fever, abdominal pain, and jaundice. Up to 70% of adults with acute cholangitis manifest the complete triad.We report a case of an older adult with an atypical presentation of acute cholangitis. Case: An 88 year-old woman presented to an urgent care facility with one month of right hip pain. Past medical history included chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, hypertension, prior left hip fracture, and depression. Her medications were mirtazapine, amitriptyline, albuterol, and ipratropium. She reported that the hip pain started radiating to her abdomen over the past two days. Vital signs were: temp 97.8°F, pulse 88 and blood pressure 125/77 mmHg. She remained alert and oriented. On exam, she had scleral icterus and mild right upper quadrant tenderness. Laboratory results revealed WBC of 34.2 K/cumm, total bilirubin of 4.2 mg/dL, AST 88 U/L,ALT 107 U/L, lipase 34 U/L, lactic acid 0.6 meq/L and bilirubinuria. Abdominal ultrasound demonstrated choledocholitihiasis, common bile duct diameter of 1.9cm, pericholecystic fluid, and ultrasonographic Murphy's sign. Antibiotics and supportive care were initiated. Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography provided definitive management. Discussion: Charcot's triad is only seen in 12% of patients with acute cholangitis who are over 75 years of age. Jaundice is the most common presenting sign of colangitis in this age group, seen in 51% of patients with gallstone etiology and 91% of those with malignancy as the cause of biliary obstruction. This case exemplifies the atypical presentation of cholangitis in older adults. Given the significant morbidity and mortality associated with acute cholangitis, jaundice in the elderly should prompt an emergent and thorough evaluation.
