N2  - Introduction: Psychiatric and medical pathology often coincide, especially in elderly patients. It is important to differentiate the underlying etiology for acute changes in mental status, personality and personal care. Catatonia should be considered an independent diagnosis( separate from depression)in patients presenting with classic catatonic findings especially when other problems are effectively resolved. Case Report: 76-year-old female with past medical history of depression was admitted to adult psychiatry for complaints of self-neglect. She had not been eating, refusing medication and not attending to hygiene.On exam, she was unkempt, confused, mute and rigid. She was diagnosed with acute mental status changes and geriatric medicine was consulted for care. After evaluation, she was found to be dehydrated, hyponatremic (sodium 133; anion gap 21) and had a urinary tract infection. She was treated with fluid resuscitation and antibiotics with resolution of medical illnesses however, she remained with drawn, anorexic, aphasic and rigid. Psychiatry asked clearance for electroconvulsive therapy to treat depression. Geriatric medicine suggested diagnosis of catatonia and administered single intramuscular dose of alprazolam. Within 30 minutes of administration, she awoke, became talkative and asked for food. She continued to improve on a regimen of twice-daily alprazolam orally and was discharged home a few days later.Discussion: Catatonia is a disturbance of motor behavior that may result from psychological or neurological etiologies1.This is a separate diagnosis from depression with catatonic features and is characterized by the rigid, immobile positions that patients often display for prolonged periods. Most cases of catatonia occur in patients who have a co-existing mood disorder (25-50%) or other psychiatric disorder such as schizophrenia (15%)1. It is important to differentiate catatonia from depression with catatonic features or other psychiatric or medical pathology as the treatment choice, especially in the geriatric patient, is different. Treatment for catatonia involves administration of benzodiazepines such as alprazolam.
