N2  - Introduction: Gout is defined metabolically as elevated levels of uric acid in the extracellular space. Gout often presents itself in the joints as recurrent attacks of acute inflammatory arthritis and monosodium urates crystal deposition in a tophus form, and in the kidney as nephrolithiasis and chronic renal failure. The association of amyloidosis as a secondary complication to gout is uncommon but still documented. We report rare case of amyloidosis secondary to gout caused nephrotic syndrome, adrenal and parathyroid insufficiency. Case Presentation: A 61-year-old male with a 20-year history of severe tophus gout and was hospitalised because of weight loss, weakness, diarrhoea and depression. Two year before his was he developed renal failure and nephrotic syndrome. Recently hypoparathyroidism was diagnosed because of hypocalcaemia and low PTH level. He was treated by colchicin and allopurinol. On admission patient had mild hyponatremia and hyperkalemia, serum creatinine 2.8 mg/dl and serum albumin 1.8 g/dl. On physical examination orthostatic hypotension was found. Adrenal function tests showed low basal cortisol level that failed to rise after cosynotropin stimulation. ACTH level was high and Addison's disease was diagnosed. Abdominal CT scan showed anatomically normal adrenal glands and slightly enlarged kidney. The constellation of chronic inflammatory disease followed by renal, adrenal and parathyroid failure suggests amyloidosis. The patient underwent rectal biopsy that confirmed diagnosis of secondary amyloidosis. Discussion: Association of gout and amyloidosis is uncommon. The most common organ affected by amyloidosis is the kidney. While amyloidosis can affect other sites including endocrine glands, it's rare to cause symptomatic symptomatic dysfunction. Two cases have been reported where Addison's disease was due to amyloidosis. However, no association has been made between gout and amyloidosis of adrenal glands. Conclusion: The case report shows systemic amyloidosis affecting kidney, adrenal and parathyroid gland secondary to gout.
