N2  - Therapy for multiple sclerosis (MS) has changed dramatically over the past decade, yielding significant progress in the treatment of relapsing/remitting and secondary progressive MS. Disease-modifying treatments are now widely available, and their beneficial effects on relapse rates, MRI outcomes and,in some cases, relapse-related disability have been shown in several clinical studies. Currently approved immunomodulatory therapies for relapsing/remitting MS include glatiramer acetate (GA) and recom-binant interferons (IFN-β), (IFN-β-la Avonex, IFN-β - la Rebif, IFN-β-lb Betaseron). All these immunomodulatory treatments reduce disease activity and the accumulation of disability in RRMS. Common side effects associated with these protein-based therapeutics are injection-site reactions, flu-like symptoms and headache. Other side effects include leukopenia, elevated liver enzyme, depression and myalgia. Natalizumab (Tysabri) and mitoxantrone (Novantrone) are also approved for treatment of MS as second-line therapy. Natalizumab is used in RRMS patients who are unresponsive to immunomo-dulatory treatment or in severe RR forms of disease. Mitox-antrone, an antineoplastic agent, is also approved for treatment of relapsing MS, but is generally reserved for secondary progressive and severe RR forms of disease. However, as immunomodulatory therapies are only partially effective in halting the MS disease process and Natalizumab and mitoxantrone are associated with important adverse events (i.e. PML, leukemia), new oral therapeutic approaches are warranted. New promising results have been obtained in clinical trials using emerging oral drugs for MS. We observed several advances in current and novel oral treatment approaches for MS. Nevertheless, most of die data were obtained from phase I/II clinical trials, we need further confirmation of their safety and efficacy profile from longer phase III clinical trials. Several pivotal reports have provided promising results for new oral therapies evaluating the safety and efficacy of new agents including fingolimod, fumaric acid, cladribine, teriflunomide and laquinomid. It is unknown whether these oral drugs could be used as first-line treatment for MS; this will depend mostly on their safety profile. Alternatively, these drugs could be used as add-on treatment for failed first-line therapy, or as an effective induction agent. The potential combination of oral drugs and IFN-β or GA needs to be tested.. The safety of drug combinations will be crucial to decision-making process. The future therapeutic options forMSwill dependon improve-mentsinunderstanding the precise factors involved indisease onset and progression, and subsequently the development of oral therapeutics that translate sustained benefit from the preclinical context into clinical background.
