N2  - Psoriasis is a chronic, unremitting disease affecting 2% to 3% of the world's population with approximately 35% of patients having moderate to severe disease. These patients are often prescribed biologic agents including adalimumab, alefacpt, efalizumab, etanercept, and infliximab. Over time, many patients do not sustain a response to one drug and subsequently switch to another biologic medication. This study involved a retrospective chart review of active psoriasis patients seen at Mount Sinai's Department of Dermatology between 2005 until the current time. We characterized 229 nonswitchers and 185 switchers. The average age of the entire population and disease duration was 51 and 18 years, respectively. There were 140 males and 89 females in the nonswitch cohort and 100 males and 86 females in the switch cohort. There were no significant demographic differences in the two populations. Among nonswitchers, 171 patients were on etanercept, 15 on alefacept, 12 on adalimumab, 14 on efalizumab, and 17 on infliximab. The average duration of therapy was 2 years. Sixty-one percent of patients found treatment efficacious while 10% experienced an adverse effect and 25% found monotherapy ineffective but had not yet switched to another biologic medication. More than half of nonswitchers were also on concomitant medications. Among switchers, 120 patients were started on etanercept, 20 on alefacept, 13 on adalimumab, 20 on efalizumab, and 12 on infliximab. Thirty-two percent of patients were on concomitant medications. The average number of switches per patient was 1.8. Uniformly, patients switched biologic medications because of inefficacy rather than adverse events or other reasons. Controlling for age and gender, the risk of switching was lower with adalimumab [-3.62, 95% CI, 0.14-0.56] and infliximab [-4.62, 95% CI, 0.13-0.43]. Compared to nonswitchers, switchers were less likely to have subtypes of psoriatic arthritis and erythrodermic psoriasis. Switchers were also more likely to have comorbidities of hypercholesterolemia, asthma, eczema, and arthritis (P<.05), while nonswitchers were more likely to have depression (P<.05). This study reports some differences in the population of psoriasis patients who switch from one biologic treatment to another. Where treatment decisions are complex and contingent on many factors, additional studies will be needed to elucidate which patients may benefit from switching to a different class of biologic agent.
