N2  - Necrotizing fasciitis is a life-threatening infection of the fascia and subcutaneous tissues. We report on a 30-year-old-man with history of intramuscular injection resulted in gluteal abscess that progressed to necrotizing fasciitis. On admission, the patient developed circulatory collapse, severe left ventricular dysfunction, and ST-segment elevation in the inferior leads. Wound and blood cultures indicated staphylococcal infection. The clinical, laboratory, echocardiographic, and electrocardiographic features are discussed. A 30-year-old man presented to the emergency department with severe left lower limb pain for 3 days. He received intramuscular injection in a private clinic 5 days before this presentation. On examination, he was conscious and alert but in distress. Vital signs revealed the following: temperature of 35.3°C, pulse rate of 115 beats/min, blood pressure of 80/50 mm Hg, respiratory rate of 24 breaths/min, and oxygen saturation of 89% on room air. Apart from peripheral cyanosis, abdominal, cardiac, and respiratory examinations were unremarkable. Lower limb examination showed tender, tense, and reddish collection in the left gluteal area and the back of the thigh down to the left leg. Results of the laboratory investigations are as follows: red blood cells, 15 × 106 μL; white blood cells, 5.7, 10.5, and 4.20 × 103/μL; platelet, 177 × 103; myoglobin, 1746, 2278, and 416 ng/mL; troponin T, less than 0.01, 0.17, and 0.45 ng/mL; creatine kinase, 1267, 2190, and 140 μg/L; creatine kinase MB, 43.3, 59.8, 77.7, and 4.0; serum glucose, 14 mmol/L; urea nitrogen, 12.5 mmol/L; creatinine, 197 μmol/L; potassium, 4.0 mmol/L; and sodium, 130 mmol/L. Gram stain test for specimen from the lesion showed scanty polymorph nuclear cells, scanty epithelial cells, and profuse gram-positive cocci (Staphylococcus aureus). Blood culture revealed S aureus. Electrocardiogram (ECG) showed sinus tachycardia and ST-segment depression in leads II, III, aVF, I, and aVL. The second ECG (30 minutes later) showed ST-segment elevation in limb leads II, III, and aVF (Fig. 1). Immediate echocardiogram revealed global hypokinesia with poor ejection fraction (EF) of the left ventricle (LV; 20%), trivial posterior pericardial effusion, and normal valves. Patients underwent immediate fluid resuscitation, intravenous antibiotics, and incision and drainage. Upon drainage, dirty colored fluid was drained with obvious loss of fascial resistance and marked ongoing necrotizing process and underwent extensive, multiple debridement. On the third day, patients become hemodynamically stable with blood pressure of 140/80 mm Hg, and second echocardiogram revealed diffuse hypokinesia with LV EF of 35% and normal pericardium. One month later, the third echocardiogram revealed normal LV with EF of 51%. The patient was doing well and referred to plastic surgery. Staphylococcal necrotizing fasciitis (NF) is a very rare disease that may be complicated with serious systemic manifestations [1-3]. The differentiation of the NF from other conditions causing shock is important as the time lag in diagnosis, and treatment is an important factor to predict the outcome in NF. Here we reported a rare case of staphylococcal NF resulted from iatrogenic gluteal abscess in a young person that led to serious cardiovascular complications. Necrotizing fasciitis was complicated with circulatory collapse and severe myocardial dysfunction with ST elevation in the inferolateral leads; we assumed differential diagnosis to reach the correct diagnosis such as acute inferior myocardial infarction (MI), severe coronary spasm, sepsis-induced myocardial dysfunction, perimyocarditis or immunomediated myocarditis, and stress cardiomyopathy (Table 1). Perimyocarditis/myopericarditis reflects simultaneous inflammation of the pericardium and heart muscle [4]. Acute myocarditis may produce an initial clinical presentation difficult to distinguish from acute MI, especially when it is associated with pericardial involvement [5]. Also, perimyocarditis can present with acute or insidious heart failure with dilated cardiomyopathy or as sudden unexpected death [4]. The proposed pathogenesis of postinfectious myocarditis is either a direct invasion of the bacteria or its toxin into the myocardium or an immunologically mediated inflammation. Simultaneous occurrence of NF and carditis favors the first hypothesis, where a time span of weeks favors the latter [4,6]. However, absence of chest pain and the localized pattern of ST elevation that completely resolved without evolutionary ST-T changes in addition to the fast resolution of the trivial pericardial effusion may not support this diagnosis. We assumed that the electrocardiographic changes and severe global myocardial dysfunction were mainly related to stress cardiomyopathy because those changes were dramatically resolved soon after improvement of the clinical and hemodynamic status using nonspecific treatment, that is, fluid resuscitation and relief of pain. Stress cardiomyopathy is a unique reversible cardiomyopathy that is frequently precipitated by a stressful event and has a clinical presentation that is indistinguishable from MI [7]. Myocardial dysfunction may occur in critically ill patients with or without sepsis or secondary to severe pain. The most common ECG change is ST-segment elevation, and approximately one third of patients with stress cardiomyopathy present with ST elevation [7]. The ST elevation is usually transient and typically present in the precordial leads, but it may be seen in the inferior and lateral leads. The ECG features at presentation do not correlate with the magnitude of LV dysfunction [7-9]. Most, if not all, patients have elevated cardiac markers that peak within 24 hours [7]. The LV systolic dysfunction and the regional wall motion abnormalities are transient and resolve completely within a matter of days to a few weeks [6]. Necrotizing fasciitis is a serious disease that needs early recognition and prompt management. Cardiovascular complications of NF should be expected and well evaluated. We proposed that stress cardiomyopathy could be a novel complication of NF that needs further studies. © 2010 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
