N2  - We previously identified cPLA2α as the sole subtype of phospholipase A2 involved in long-term depression (LTD) in Purkinje cells of the cerebellum. In this study, we explored downstream signal transduction via cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) that converts arachidonic acid (AA) to prostaglandins (PGs). We found that, when cPLA2α was deleted by gene knockout or COX-2 was inhibited pharmacologically, LTD was no longer induced, yet it was effectively rescued by replenishing PGD2 or E2 by either intracellular infusion or extracellular perfusion. PGE2, however, was unable to rescue the LTD blocked by a PKC inhibitor. These results are difficult to reconcile with the current hypothesis that a PLA2-AA-PKC-ERK1/2-PLA2 loop provides the induction mechanism of LTD. We conclude that coincidental activation of the cPLA2α-AA-COX-2-PGD2/E2 cascade with a pathway involving PKC is critically required for LTD induction.
