N2  - Introduction: Studies have shown that the nature of the immune response to trauma and sepsis may depend on the etiology of injury and complexity of the inciting pathogens. Hypothesis: We hypothesized that there would be differences in the response of circulating lymphocytes to three inciting events, each varying in severity. Methods: Pigs were anaesthetized and surgically instrumented for extensive hemodynamic monitoring. Following surgery, pigs were randomized into three groups: Ischemia Reperfusion(IR, n=3) -the superior mesenteric artery was clamped for 30 minutes and released; Fecal Clot (FC, n=3) -feces were collected from the cecum, mixed with blood to form a clot, and implanted into the abdomen; and “2-hit”(n=3) the animals received both FC and IR. All pigs were monitored for 48-hours (T0, i.e. zero hours post-injury -T48) or until early death and received fluid management, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and mechanical ventilation to simulate ICU care. Results: Circulating lymphocytes fell initially after the inciting event (T0-T6) in all groups, but increased in the FC group at each following time point (T6-T48) and recovered nearly to baseline levels by the end of the study. In the IR and “2-hit groups” circulating lymphocytes remained depressed throughout the study. Statistically, circulating lymphocyte levels in the FC were significantly different than both the IR (p=0.0181) and the “2-hit” group (p=0.0112) in the effect of treatment∗time in a repeated measures ANOVA. All animals had positive blood cultures for gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria. Conclusions: This study suggests a predictable trend in circulating lymphocytes, clearly delineating the effect of peritonitis compared to IR. The initial fall in circulating lymphocytes is likely due to the recruitment of lymphocytes to sites of inflammation. An explanation for the recovery of lymphocytes in the FC group is that there may have been a combination of recruitment from lymph tissue and cell proliferation in these tissues in response to an established locus of infection. Despite the similarity with FC, there was no upward trend in the “2-hit” suggesting a more profound dysfunction. We speculate that the additive effect of IR is responsible for the depression of circulating lymphocytes in the “2-hit”. It is possible that this combined injury leads to a decrease of blood lymphocytes by confounding lymphocyte migration, stunting cell proliferation and recruitment.
