N2  - Purpose: CNS inflammation is acknowledged as an important factor in seizure pathophysiology, however, little is known about the role of peripheral inflammation. We recently demonstrated that peripheral inflammation is capable of increasing susceptibility to seizures in-vivo and epileptiform events in vitro and this is highly dependent upon microglial activation and TNF(alpha) release in the brain. Here, we examined the mechanisms underlying the increased CNS excitability following peripheral inflammation. Method: We used arat model of peripheral inflammation (colitis induced by intracolonic administration of 2,4,6-trinitrobenzene sulfonic acid). Hippocampal slices were obtained and field excitatory postsynaptic potentials (fEPSP) arising from Shaffer collateral stimulation were recorded from CA1 area. To evaluate the role of microgliain hippocampal excitability we inhibited microglial activation by chronic minocycline injection (45 mg/kg/12h; ip). Real time rt-PCR using validated primers evaluated glutamate receptor subunit expression in microdissected CA1 tissue. Results: In slices from inflamed animals, fEPSP slopes were significantly enhanced with no change in paired-pulse ratios. The increase in fEPSP slope was inhibited by minocycline. In addition, the ability to induce both long-term potentiation and long-term depression in CA1 was significantly reduced. Glutamate receptor subunit expression analysis revealed a significant decrease in GluR2 and an increase in NR2B in CA1 from animals with peripheral inflammation. Conclusion: Our data suggest a postsynaptic change of synaptic transmission in hippocampus following peripheral inflammation, as well as decreased synaptic plasticity. These changes are mediated by microglial activation and may underlie both increased susceptibility to seizures and epileptogenesis.
