N2  - Objective: The changes in blood cytokines which indicated the imbalance between pro- and anti-inflammatory cytokines have been reported to occur in psychiatric disorders such as major depression, schizophrenia and bipolar mania. It was reported that pro-inflammatory cytokines reduce the synthesis of 5-HT by stimulating the activity of indoleamine 2,3 dioxygenase (IDO), an enzyme which converts tryptophan to kynurenine. Kynurenine is further metabolized to kynurenic acid (KYNA), 3-hydroxykynurenine (3OHK) and quinolinic acid (QUINA) by kynurenine aminotransferase, kynurenine 3-monooxygenase (KMO) and kynureninase (KYNASE). The 3OHK is neurotoxic apoptotic while QUINA is the excitotoxic N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) receptor agonist. Conversely KYNA is an antagonist of all three ionotropic excitatory amino acid receptors and α7-nicotinic acetylcholine receptor (α7-nAChR). The imbalance between those protective and toxic metabolites are proposed as one of the pathophysiological mechanisms in schizophrenia. Method: The kynurenine pathway metabolites were measured in serum, plasma and CSF of schizophrenic patients. Results: Tryptophan breakdown has been found to be increased but KYNA, the neuroprotective metabolite is decreased in the serum/ plasma of schizophrenic patients compared to healthy controls. Moreover, in the patients with schizophrenia, the neurotoxic 3OHK is significantly increased. Such changes in these metabolites are reflected also in the cerebrospinal fluid. Those changes were found to be corrected after neuroleptics. Conclusion: These findings lead to the hypothesis an imbalance neuroprotection and neurodegeneration in terms of kynurenine metabolites and their immunological, neurochemical and biochemical interactions in the brain might further induce the apoptosis of the neuroprotective astrocytes and neurodegenerative changes such as loss of brain volume.
