N2  - Background: Psychiatric co-morbidity is common in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). It is unknown whether it precedes or follows colitis. Here we investigated whether chronic gut inflammation alters behavior and examined underlying mechanisms. Methods: Female AKR mice were infected with Trichuris muris for 4 weeks or treated with 3 cycles of dextran sodium sulfate (1 wk 3% DSS+ 1 wk washout). BALB/C mice were infected with Helicobacter pylori (Hp) for 4 months. Additional T. muris-infected mice received anti-inflammatory treatment therapy with etanercept (TNF-a inhibitor) or budesonide. Anxiety/depression-like behavior was assessed using the light preference and step-down tests. Gut inflammation was assessed using MPO and histology, and ELISA for circulating cytokines. BDNF mRNA levels were measured by in situ hybridization in brain sections. Changes in serum protein levels were assessed using proteomic analysis. Results: Chronic parasitic, chemically induced-colitis and bacterial gastritis, induced mild gut inflammation and anxiety/depression-like behavior. Abnormal behavior in T. muris infected mice was associated with decreased hippocampal BDNF mRNA. Pro-inflammatory serum cytokines were mildly increased. Proteomic analysis showed altered levels of several proteins related to neural function, including afamin,?an?a-tocopherol transport protein. Abnormal behavior normalized after anti-inflammatory treatment. Conclusion: Our results indicate that chronic gut inflammation alters behavior and brain neurochemisry, likely through multiple mechanisms. Our results suggest that psychiatric co-morbidities in IBD develop as a consequence of gut inflammation and are amenable to anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory therapy. Supported by CIHR grant.
