N2  - Acute tryptophan depletion (ATD) is an experimental model to reduce central serotonin levels. It has been shown to induce gastrointestinal symptoms in IBS patients, mood changes in depression, and nausea in migraine patients. We wondered whether ATD can induce nausea in healthy subjects during motion. Methods: Thirty-eight healthy female subjects age 21 to 28 with variable susceptibility to motion sickness were recruited and randomly assigned to two groups (ATD and placebo) in a randomized, double blinded parallel-group design. Following a standardized and balanced amino acid diet on day 1 (including 2.3 g L-tryptophan), they received either a protein drink without tryptophan (ATD condition) or the balanced protein drink with tryptophan (placebo condition) on day 2. Four h after its consumption, they were exposed to a standard rotation procedure (5 x 1 min, with 1 to 2 min breaks in between,). They could terminate each rotation upon request, but were encouraged to continue after each interruption. Symptom ratings (SR) were taken on a 7 item VAS scale prior to rotation, at each break, and 15 and 30 min thereafter. Saliva samples were collected to assess the cor- tisol levels in the morning upon arrival in the laboratory, and immediately prior to and immediately after rotation, and 15 and 30 min later. Data were compared between conditions (ATD, control) by ANO- VA with psychometric (SR) and biological variables (cortisol) as dependent variables and menstrual cycle phase as covariate. Results: Four subjects prematurely terminated the rotation procedure and were excluded from analysis. For the remaining 34, SR was significantly higher during ATD than during control conditions (P = 0.024). Corti- sol levels rose significantly with rotation (P<.001) but were unaffected by ATD. The susceptibility scores and the maximum SR during rotation were significantly associated (r=0.51, P = 0.003), as were SR and cortisol increase with rotation (r=0.424, P = 0.014). Higher baseline cortisol levels were significantly associated with lower SR during rotation (r=-0.40; P = 0.023). This protective effects of high cortisol were pronounced during the menstrual and follicular phase of the cycle (r=-0.735, P = 0.01, n = 11) and not present during the luteal phase (n = 13). Contraceptives did not affect these associations. Conclusion: ATD does increase symptoms of motion sickness in healthy female subjects when exposed to nauseogenic body rotation. However, nausea developed under this condition is as well affected by the acute cortisol levels, with high levels protecting against motion sickness; this protection varies substantially across the menstrual cycle.
