N2  - INTRODUCTION: A pharmacovigilance system for reporting of adverse reactions to veterinary medicinal products was established in Sweden in 1992. According to the Swedish legislation (LVFS 2001:12 and 2006:4) 'Veterinarians are obliged to report to the Swedish Medical Products Agency (MPA) all serious adverse reactions in animals, all adverse reactions in humans exposed to veterinary drugs and all adverse reactions which seem to increase in frequency in animals. The obligation to report adverse reactions also applies to drugs registered for humans, which are used in veterinary practice'. Thus, in Sweden most reports of adverse drug reactions are sent by the veterinarians to the Swedish MPA. Only few reports are sent to drug companies. During recent years about 250-350 reports of adverse reactions to veterinary medicinal products have been received at the Swedish MPA per year (Table 1). Table 2 shows the number of adverse reactions reported in different species during 2003-2008 grouped according to the ATCvet system. It appears that most reports concern dog, followed by cat and horse. Only few reports concern adverse reactions in other animal species and adverse reactions in humans exposed to veterinary drugs. (Table Presented) Below an overview is given of the adverse reactions to veterinary medicinal products which have been reported via the Swedish pharmacovigilance system. The intention is not to give a full record of all reports which have been sent to the Swedish MPA. Instead, focus has been put on adverse reactions which occur frequently, recur from year to year or may be worthy of attention for other reasons. In most instances the underlying mechanism(s) are discussed in connection to the description of the adverse reactions. HORSE: An important adverse reaction in horse is 'penicillin-shock', which occurs following intramuscular injections of procaine benzylpenicillin or, less frequently, after intravenous injections of sodium or potassium benzylpenicillin. The reactions arise during or immediately after the injections. The dominating clinical signs are locomotor and behavioural changes. In most instances the horses recover within few minutes, but in some cases they die. An acute allergic anaphylaxis probably underlies the adverse reactions in the horses given sodium or potassium benzylpenicillin and in few horses given procaine benzylpenicillin. However, in most horses reacting to procaine benzylpenicillin the acute effects are probably due to procaine toxicity [1]. Horses are very sensitive to CNS-effects of procaine and experimental studies have shown that systemic administration of procaine in horses gives similar neurological signs as those observed in horses reacting to procaine benzylpenicillin [2]. Negative effects of different vaccines (group QI in Table 2) are frequently reported in horse and include injection site reactions, fever, urticaria, depression and general weakness. There are several reports that intravenous injections of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAID), such as flunixin and ketoprofen (group QM in Table 2), can induce immediate collapse with ataxia and seizures. Most horses recover, but there are also several reports of death [3]. The mechanism underlying these reactions is unknown. There are also reports that NSAID can cause erosions and ulcerations of the mucosa of the gastrointestinal tract, preferentially in the glandular part of the stomach. There are several reports of acute jointreactions in horses given intra-articular injections of sodium hyaluronate. There are several reports that hyperventilation can be observed in horses given α-2-receptor agonists, such as detomidine and romifidine (group QN in Table 2). The hyperventilation has in some instances been severe, with up to 150 breathings per minute [3]. The period required for normalization can be 1-1.5 h. To counteract the reactions atipamezole has usually been given, often without any immediate effect. Lack of expected efficacy has been reported for a product containing a combination of a barbiturate (quinalbarbitone) and a cardiotoxic compound (cinchocaine), which is used on license in Sweden for euthanasia in horse. Veterinarians given licence for this product are informed that there are cases of lack of efficacy and they are recommended to be prepared to have an alternative method for euthanasia. The reason for the lack of efficacy in some horses is not known. Euthanasia in horses is a difficult task and available methods have both advantages and disadvantages. For pharmacological euthanasia in horses there is still no optimal drug available. CATTLE: Penicillin-shock occurs in cattle, although less frequently than in horses. The underlying mechanisms can be assumed to be the same as in the horse. Thus: procaine toxicity or acute allergic anaphylaxis. There are rare but recurring reports of anaphylaxis and death in calves given a live vaccine against ringworm (Trichophyton verrucosum). A large number of calves is vaccinated each year against ringworm in Sweden within the so called 'Hide and Skin Trade and Improving Quality Program' (in Swedish 'Felfri Hudprogrammet'). This implies that the incidence of these reactions is low. There have been few reports that administration of anthelminthic intraruminal boluses can lead to oesophageal obstruction in calves. It is indicated in the Summary of Product Characteristics (SPC) and in the Patient Information Leaflet (PIL) of such products that this effect can be seen in young calves which have not yet developed the ruminating function. SWINE: In swine there are reports of local site reactions after intramuscular injections of mineral-oil containing vaccines. Mineral oil is used as an adjuvant to stimulate development of immunity and it is indicated in the SPC:s and the PIL:s of such products that local site reactions can occur. In the reported cases these reactions were more severe than expected [4]. This appears to be a problem especially in young sows, in which the reactions due to loss of appetite and lowered general condition also can lead to delayed oestrus. It should be noted that injection of mineral oil in man can lead to serious tissue injuries. A warning for this and a recommendation concerning which actions should be taken in cases of accidental self injection are given in SPC:s and PIL:s. There are several reports of anaphylaxis, seizures, vomiting and deaths in young pigs injected intramuscularly with iron. This is a well-known adverse reaction following parenteral iron therapy in pigs. The underlying mechanism is unclear. The most notable adverse reaction event in swine in Sweden occurred 1999-2000 and concerned animals treated with valnemulin. The adverse reactions were reported almost only in Sweden and Denmark (there was a single report from Finland and a single report from Ireland) [5]. The reactions were seen in 1-4 months old pigs given valnemulin in the feed as therapy for swine dysentery. The pigs reacted with fever, inappetence, apathy, erythema and ataxia. In Sweden more than 2000 pigs were affected and there were several deaths. The reason for the selective toxicity in pigs in Sweden and Denmark is not yet known, but is suspected to be related to some hereditary characteristic in swine of the Swedish and Danish landraces. It is known that antibiotics belonging to the pleuromutilin group, such as tiamulin and valnemulin, via an inhibition of CYP 3A, may potentiate the toxicity of ionophore antibiotics, such as narasin and monensin [6]. The latter are used as coccidiostatics in chicken and are degraded by CYP 3A. Contamination of valnemulin-containing pig-feed with ionophore antibiotics can result in symptoms similar to those described above. However, there were no indications that such a contamination had occurred. DOG: In dogs most reports concern adverse reactions to vaccines and to antiparasitic agents (QI and QP in Table 2). In Sweden dogs are vaccinated mainly against canine distemper virus infection, canine hepatitis, parvovirus infection, parainfluenza virus infection, rabies and leptospirosis. The incidence of the reported adverse reactions is in the range 0.5 - 5 reports per 10 000 doses of vaccine. Most reactions are probably due to allergies. The dogs can react with a spectrum of symptoms, which may include oedema of the nose and around the eyes and ears, itching, fever, urticaria, vomiting, diarrhoea, ataxia and anaphylactic shock. Our results indicate that one of the vaccines induces an adverse reaction-pattern which differs from that seen for the other vaccines [7].
