N2  - INTRODUCTION: Infliximab (IFx) has demonstrated it's efficacy to induce and maintain remission in Crohn's Disease (CD)(ACCENT I/II). Durability data beyond 54 weeks is needed with IFx use to help refine therapy and obtain optimal long-term response. METHODS: We created a database of 106 CD pts treated with IFx between Oct 1999 and Oct 2008 (9yrs). Data collected included demographics, adverse events, time to loss of response, concomitant immunosuppressives, smoking status, disease anatomy/type. Remission was defined by standard clinical parameters. Loss of response to IFx was defined as the need for a reduction in dose interval or increased dosage required to recapture a response. Kaplan-Meier Survival analysis was used to compare time to loss of response of IFx. Subgroups were compared using the Log Rank test. Analysis was performed using XLStat with significance taken at p= <0.05. RESULTS: A total of 106 pts, (60F:46M, median age 39yrs (17-79)) were included. Disease anatomy, 40 (37.7%) pts ileum, 26 (24.5%) colonic, 33 (31.3%) ileocolonic and 7 (6.6%) UGI. 51 (48.1%) pts had perianal involvement. Pts received induction IFx at 0, 2 and 6 weeks. 20 (18.9%) pts stopped IFx at induction due to SE's, lack of response or surgery. 14 pts (13.2%) were treated on an episodic basis, while the remainder received scheduled IFx. The mean duration of IFx was 19.7 mths (2 - 93). 51 pts received 8 wkly doses whereas 21 pts were maintained on 12 wkly doses. There was no difference in loss of response between 8 or 12 week maintenance (p=NS). 16% of initial responders to IFx were in remission at 8 yrs follow-up. 18% of perianal pt's were in remission at 5 yrs vs 44% of pts without perianal disease (p=0.056). Non-smokers had a response of 58.7% at 5 yrs vs 31.6% of smokers. Ex-smokers performed poorly with no responders beyond 5 years. This was significant (p=0.007). There were no significant differences in durability between disease anatomy and age groups. 10 (9.4%) pts had an allergic reaction to IFx. 4 (3.7%) pts developed infections (pneumonia,EBV,cellulitis and TB) that required IFx cessation. 2 pts developed dyspnoea which led to IFx withdrawal. Treatment was stopped due to depression in 1 pt. 2 pts died of causes unrelated to IFx Rx. CONCLUSION: This data confirms published medium term durability data and suggests only 16% of pts remain in remission at 8 years following IFx. Pts with Perianal disease and smokers have worse long-term outcomes to IFx maintenance treatment. Episodic Rx does not appear to be inferior to scheduled therapy. As the majority of pts were on concomitant immunomodulators, the effects of this on durability could not be assessed.
