N2  - Background: Role of anxiety and depression in CD patients in remission has been poorly investigated. We hypothesized that despite clinical remission, anxiety and depression symptoms are frequent in CD patients. Aim of the study was to evaluate the frequency of anxiety and depression symptoms in CD patients in remission and the potential factors influencing the development of these symptoms. Methods: CD patients older than 18 years, and in remission for at least 6 months before study entry were included. CD remission was defined as a Crohn's Disease Activity Index (CDAI) <150 points together with a C-reactive protein <5 mg/L. A questionnaire including age, gender, academic education, marital status and smoking habits was fulfilled. Patients were classified according to the time (years) from diagnosis of the disease, location and behaviour of the disease according to the Montreal Classification, presence of perianal disease, presence of extraintestinal manifestations, previous bowel resection, and development of steroid-dependency or steroid-resistance. Therapy maintaining remission was also recorded. Hospital Anxiety and Depression scale (HAD) was calculated in all patients. Results are shown as OR and 95%CI and analyzed by logistic regression. Results: 92 consecutive patients were included (48 male, mean age 37 years, range from 18 to 71 years). One patient failed to fulfil correctly the questionnaire. Anxiety and depression symptoms were present in 36 (39%) and 22 (24%) patients, respectively. Infliximab therapy was the only factor associated with anxiety (OR=3.11; 95%CI: 1.03-9.43; p<0.05). On the contrary, presence of depressive symptoms is less frequent in patients under infliximab therapy (OR=0.16; 95%CI: 0.02-0.97; p<0.05). Previous bowel resection (OR= 13.01; 95%CI: 1.89-89.51; p<0.01) and ileal disease (OR=8.26; 95%CI: 1.24-55.04; p<0.05) are factors associated to depressive symptoms. Conclusions: Despite clinical remission, an important number of CD patients presents with anxiety or depressive symptoms. Infliximab therapy in CD patients is associated to more anxiety, but fewer depressive symptoms. CD patients in remission would probably benefit from psychological support.
