N2  - Background: The vagus nerve (VN), which innervates the colon, is composed of 20% and 80% efferent and afferent fibers respectively. The anti-inflammatory properties of the VN were first attributed to its afferent fibers, through an activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary adrenal axis. More recently, it has been demonstrated that efferent fibers also have an antiinflammatory effect through a peripheral release of acetylcholine which binds to an alpha-7-nicotinic receptor to inhibit the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g. TNFalpha) by macrophages (1). VN stimulation (VNS) has been approved for the treatment of epilepsy and depression in humans. VNS has also been successful for the treatment of experimental sepsis and pancreatitis. Aim: to use VNS to prevent or improve an experimental colitis in rats. Methods: colonic inflammation was chemically induced by intracolonic instillation of trinitrobenzensulfonic acid (TNBS; 30mg/kg in 50% ethanol). Control animals received intracolonic instillation of saline. VNS was performed by stimulation of the left cervical VN (3 hours per day for 5 days; 1mA, 500μsec, 10sec ON, 90sec OFF, 5Hz). Non-stimulated rats were implanted in the same conditions. Twelve animals were used per group. At the end of VNS (day 5), body weight was measured (and compared to the initial weight) and digestive tissues were removed for biological analysis (myeloperoxydase activity: MPOA) and TNFalpha mRNA assays by PCR. Results: VNS induced a decrease in body weight loss at day 5 in TNBS animals and a decrease of MPOA levels in the distal colon compared to non stimulated animals. A decrease of TNFalpha mRNA in colonic tissue was also observed after VNS in both TNBS and non-TNBS groups, but only significant in control animals. Conclusion: Expected anti-inflammatory effect of VNS was established by the MPOA decrease while a slight decrease of TNFalpha mRNA was observed in VNS-TNBS group in our conditions. These results suggest that other anti-inflammatory pathways could be involved in the improvement of colonic inflammation by VNS. These experimental data argue for the possible use of VNS in treatment of inflammatory bowel diseases in humans. 1.Tracey KJ. Physiology and immunology of the cholinergic antiinflammatory pathway. J Clin Invest. 2007;117:289-96.
