N2  - Background: Accumulating evidence suggests that microglial activation associated with inflammation might be involved in the pathophysiology of neuropsychiatric diseases including schizophrenia, drug abuse, depression, and autism. The present study was undertaken to examine the possibility of minocycline (a blocker of microglial activation) as a therapeutic drug for schizophrenia and methamphetamine psychosis. Methods: The effects of minocycline on behavioral changes (e.g., prepulse inhibition (PPI) deficits and cognitive deficits) in mice after the administration of the NMDA receptor antagonists were examined. Furthermore, the effects of minocycline on the behavioral changes and neurotoxicity in mice and conscious monkeys after the administration of methamphetamine were also examined. Results: Minocycline significantly attenuated PPI deficits and cognitive deficits after the administration of the NMDA receptor antagonists. Furthermore, minocycline significantly attenuated behavioral changes, neurotoxicity, and microglial activation in mice after the repeated administration of methamphetamine. Moreover, a PET study using conscious monkeys revealed that the reduction of dopamine transporter in the monkey striatum after repeated administration of methamphetamine could be attenuated by posttreatment and subsequent administration of minocycline. Conclusions: These findings suggest that minocycline would be a potential therapeutic drug for schizophrenia and methamphetamine psychosis. Drugs (e.g., minocycline), which can block microglia activation, could be novel therapeutic drugs for neuropsychiatric diseases associated with microglia activation.
