N2  - An inflammatory mechanism has been postulated for both major depression and schizophrenia. In major depression, glutamatergic hyperfunction seems to be closely related to the lack of serotonergic and noradrenergic neurotransmission and to the core symptoms of major depression. In schizophrenia dopaminergic hyperfunction in the limbic system and dopaminergic hypofunction in the frontal cortex may be the main neurotransmitter disturbances. Recent research shows that glutamatergic hypofunction might be the cause for this dopaminergic dysfunction, i.e. glutamatergic dysfunction might be a common pathway in the neurobiology of schizophrenia and depression. Since the function of the glutamatergic system is closely related to the immune system and to the tryptophankynurenine metabolism [1], which both seem to play a key role in the pathophysiology of schizophrenia and major depression [2,3], an immune mediated glutamatergic dysfunction is a key feature in both disorders. Differences in the activation of the enzyme indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase (IDO) and in the tryptophan-kynurenine metabolism resulting in an increased production of kynurenic acid in schizophrenia [2] and probably of increased quinolinic acid in depression [1,3] might play a key role in these diseases. IDO is activated by pro-inflammatory cytokines such as Interferon-7 or Tumor-Necrosis-Factor-a and is inhibited by anti-inflammatory cytokines. Inhibition or activation of IDO, however, lead to different products in the kynurenine metabolism, which are associated with an imbalance in the glutamatergic neurotransmission, contributing to an overweight of N-methyl-D-aspartate (NMDA) agonism in depression and of NMDA antagonism in schizophrenia.
