N2  - Introduction: Adverse Drug Reactions (ADRs) are today an important cause of hospital admission (between 0.5 and 10% in different studies) [1] and of morbimortality in hospitalized patients. Near 20% of hospitalized patients will suffer an ADRs during the hospital stay. Neuropsychiatric ADRs are not the most frequent but are generally serious and can be responsible of several hospital admissions. The aim of this study was to determine the incidence of neuropsychiatric ADRs and the neuropsychiatric drugs-induced ADRs in hospitalized patients in a tertiary care hospital. Material and Method. We performed this study within the pharmacovigilance department of a tertiary care hospital in Buenos Aires, Argentina (Argerich Hospital). We look twice weekly for Adverse Events in all the departments of this hospital except the emergency room. Every time a possible neuropsychiatric adverse event or a neuropsychiatric drug-induced ADRs appeared, the Naranjo Score was applied to calculate the drug causality. We included for this work all the adverse events that applied as probable or highly probable according to this score. All the ADRs that were included for this study were reported to the National Regulatory Agency of Drug and Food (ANMAT - Agencia Nacional de Medicamentos, Alimentos y Tecnología Médica). This study was performed between July 2008 and January 2009. Results: In this period we detected 704 ADRs. Of this, 43 (6.1% of total ADRs) were neuropsychiatrics (26 in males and 17 in females) and 16 of them were serious (three ADRs related admissions and 13 prolonged the hospitalary stay). There were three cases of drug related hospital admission (two sensory depression and one case of confussional syndrome). The neuropsychiatrics ADRs were: sensory depression 13 cases, generalized seizures 4 cases, parkinsonism 4 cases, confussional syndrome and paresthesia 3 cases, neuromuscular blockade, psicosis and vertigo 2 cases and others ADRs appeared in only one case (confussion, ataxia, withdrawal syndrome, etc). The drug most frequently involved were halopidol and morphine 4 cases, clonazepam 3 cases, colistin, ranitidine, phenytoin and metoclopramide 2 cases and multiple other drugs were involved in only one event (imipenem, meropenem, salbutamol, phenobarbital, pregabalin, gentamycin, clindamycin, diazepam, dexametasone, risperidone, vincristine, risperidone, simvastatin, omeprazole, valproic acid, oxaliplatin, docetaxel, etc). Of the 704 ADRs. 76 (10.8%) were caused by neuropsychiatric drugs. The group of drugs most frequently involved were: antiepileptic drugs 34 events, antipsychotics 14 events, antidepressant 11 events, opioids 8 events, hipnotics 8 events and antialzheimer drugs 1 event. Considering individual drugs, the most frequently involved were phenytoin 15 events, carbamazepine 12, halopidol 7, phenobarbital 5, midazolam and morphine 4, clonazepam, risperidone and sertraline 3, amitriptyline, cytalopram and fluoxetine 2. The most frequently ADRs were hyponatremia 16 cases, sensory depression 12, hepatotoxicity 9 cases, skin rash 6 cases, asimptomatic QTc prolongation and interaction 5 cases. There were three cases of drug induced diabetes asossiated with risperidona (2 events) and halopidol. Conclussion. The incidence of neuropsychiatric ADRs was lesser in this study than reported in other studies from regional [2] and international [3] bibliography (aproximately 15% to 20% of the total ADRs). Antiepileptic drugs were the neuropsychiatries drugs most frequently involved in Adverse Drug Reactions but this is variable in other international studies. The incidence of hyponatremia (generally as a cause of inappropriate ADH secretion) was higher than in other studies. Antipsychotics-induced diabetes was not a common ADRs (0.4% of total ADRs).
