N2  - Background and Aims: The link between cannabis use and psychosis has been subject to a great deal of media and scientific attention, whilst the association between cannabis use and mood disorders has been less well researched. It has been suggested that cannabis use is associated with increased levels of depression [1] and animal models have shown that reduced activity in the endocannabinoid system is linked with depression [3]. Whilst these changes are thought to be caused by repeated exposure to the main constituent of smoked cannabis, Δ9-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC), it has also been shown that the second most prevalent cannabinoid, cannabidiol (CBD) has anxiolytic effects [3]. This study aimed to assess the link between cannabis use, mood and mood disorders link using THC acid levels measured from urine samples and CBD and THC levels in naturalistically smoked cannabis. Method: 106 cannabis users and 33 non-cannabis using controls were tested on 2 separate occasions. Cannabis users were assessed once while intoxicated with their own naturalistically smoked cannabis and again when drug free; controls were assessed twice whilst drug free. All groups were indexed on state anxiety, euphoria and anhedonia, and on trait depression, anxiety, and dependence. In addition, vulnerability factors such as childhood trauma were investigated. Results: Level of THC acid in urine was found to be a significant predictor of depression in vulnerable individuals. Cannabis dependence also strongly predicted depression. Neither gender nor family diagnosis of mental health problems were significant predictors of depression. State anxiety, anhedonia and euphoria were all higher in cannabis users when intoxicated. These symptoms decreased when drug free, though anhedonia remained significantly higher in daily cannabis users on the drug free day compared to recreational users and controls. Cannabis samples were predominantly of a 'skunk' variety that currently dominates the illicit UK cannabis market and is characterised by high levels of THC combined with little or no CBD. Acutely neither THC, CBD nor the ratio of the two cannabinoids in smoked cannabis were not associated with differences in euphoria, anxiety or anhedonia. Conclusions: Greater THC levels in urine of cannabis users and dependence on the drug were found to be associated with increased incidence of depression. This elevation of depressive symptoms may be related to lifestyle factors associated with cannabis dependence, or may possibly be related to disruptions of the endocannabinoid system due to prolonged, heavy use of the drug. The euphoric and anxiogenic effects of naturalistically smoked cannabis in this study may be due to the type of cannabis that is predominantly smoked in the UK, with high levels of THC accompanied by little or none CBD. Future research should assess whether cannabis with a higher CBD:THC ratio can acutely attenuate cannabis induced increases in anxiety and anhedonia, and chronically protect against cannabis induced depression.
