N2  - Objective: To describe a first case of encephalopathy following intramuscular botulinum injection. Description: An 11-year-old girl, 30 kg, with cerebral palsy presented with an acute encephalopathy following her routine botulinum injections (1000 Units Dysport) under midazolam sedation (7.5 mg). She presented with acute amnesia and aphasia, which worsened over a 4 week period before improving slowly over many months. She was investigated including two MRI head scans, EEG, infection screen including CSF studies, metabolic studies, thyroid function tests, anti thyroid antibodies, vasculitis screen and lead levels (all normal). Urine toxicology was positive for benzodiazepines on day 2 and had cleared by day 7. She was treated with antibiotics and acyclovir. Flumazenil, an antidote to midazolam did not improve her symptoms. The following 2 weeks she developed head lag and this improved over the next 8 weeks; however, she remained amnesic and aphasic. To complicate matters, her symptoms were amplified as she developed a depression due to difficult circumstances. Discussion: Initially we thought the deterioration was related to the midazolam and queried if she was a slow metabolizer. However she did not improve with flumazenil and mizolam was undetectable on day 7. We explored the possibility of botulinum. There are no known case reports of intramuscularly botulinum causing an encephalopathy. Botulinum treats disorders characterized by hyperfunction of cholinergic terminals, such as dystonia and spasticity. Intramuscular botulinum is thought to have local neuromuscular effects alone, remaining at the synaptic termina; near to the injection site. However, a recent paper demonstrates that botulinum applied in the periphery can affect central neurons via retrograde axonal transport and transcytosis to distant synapses [1]. Conclusion: We suggest this girl's encephalopathy may be secondary to botulinum gaining access to the brain after peripheral administration.
