N2  - Background: The chance for bone marrow depression is increased using immunosuppressant after kidney transplantation. However, severe bone marrow depression is rare. Objective: To analyze the drug-induced IV degree of bone marrow depression after renal transplantation and summarize the treatment and outcome. Design, time and setting: The cases analysis was performed at Transplantation Urological Surgery of Ruikang Hospital of Guangxi Traditional Chinese Medical University from January 2005 to January 2007. Participants: Four patients with IV degree of bone marrow depression after allogeneic kidney transplantation, 2 males aged 50 and 56 years and 2 females aged 56 and 29 year, admitted to Transplantation Urological Surgery of Ruikang Hospital of Guangxi Traditional Chinese Medical University. They were infected in different degrees. Methods: The immunosuppressant and the medications that could cause bone marrow depression were decreased or withdrawn. Instead, the patients were treated with cyclosporine + mycophenolate mofetil + prednisone along with granulocyte colony-stimulating factor 7-9 days. After transplantation, the 3rd generation of cephalosporins antibiotics was used for one week. Main outcome measures: Blood-routine changes were tested daily. Results: Decreased granulocyte treatment could be recovered in 7 days. Platelet was recovered in about 2 weeks after the treatment. Hemoglobin was not affected by the treatment. Infection was controlled at 10-14 days. Conclusion: Using symptomatic and supportive treatments, drug-induced bone marrow depression after renal transplantation can be cured.
