N2  - Depression of COX-2 derived vascular prostacyclin (PGI2) formation is the most plausible explanation for the cardiovascular hazard from NSAIDs, most evident in those selective for inhibition of COX-2. However, these drugs exhibit distinct and diverse effects in vitro, raising the possibility that “off target”effects might contribute to their cardiovascular risk profile at concentrations attained after clinical dosing in vivo. Cultured human aortic smooth muscle cells (hASMC) were stimulated with interleukin (IL)-1β (10 ng/ml) in the presence of rofecoxib (500 nM), celecoxib (500 nM) and vehicle and RNA expression profiles obtained at 5 time points (before treatment (-2hr), 0, 2, 8 and 24hr after IL-1/β stimulation). IL-1/β induced time- dependent formation of PGE2 and PGI2, but not of PGF2α and thromboxane (Tx) A2. Both rofecoxib and celecoxib suppressed induced PGE2 and PGI2 formation significantly at both 8 and 24 hr. Gene expression was evaluated on 52 Affy HG-U133A 2.0 arrays and analyzed using LIMMA. Following stimulation with IL-1/β, 10687 of 22216 probe sets were significantly changed in their expression (false discovery rate adjusted p <0.1). However, only 8 genes were differentially regulated between rofecoxib and celecoxib treatments at either of these time points. Celecoxib upregulated IL27RA vs rofecoxib and dowregulated TOP3A, CDK6, XPO4, DHRS3, PTGIS, LXN and GALNT12. Using Ingenuity Pathway Analysis software, a total of 50 regulatory networks were identified as IL-1β responsive. Eight of these were differentially regulated between rofecoxib and celecoxib (P < 0.05) including interferon signaling, G1/S cell cycle control and glycosphingolipid biosynthesis. Thus, our comprehensive and unbiased analysis provides evidence for a small number of potential “off-target”effects independent of COX-2 inhibition at concentrations of celecoxib and rofecoxib with proven selectivity for COX-2. (AHA 0430148N - T.G.; PO1 HL62250-G.A.F.).
