N2  - Chronic C hepatitis is a global health problem. The treatment of it is still unresolved. The pegylated interferon means substantive breakthrough in the treatment. The longer effect, the lasting, steady therapeutic blood level are the pharmacokinetic advances of them. There is no significant different in the spectrum of the side effects of pegylated interferon and standard interferon. The most frequent side effects leading to dose reduction or cessation of the treatment are the depression and the hematological disorders. Neutropenia is induced frequently by pegylated interferon, than the standard form according to the literary data. Combined antiviral treatment (pegylated interferon alpha-2a and ribavirin) of a 54 years old woman, suffered from posttransfusion chronic C hepatitis was started. The dose of the pegylated interferon alpha-2a and ribavirin was reduced at the 8. week due to leucopenia and mild anemia. Fever, cough, sore throat and weakness occurred. Agranulocytosis was detected which was accounted side effect of pegylated interferon treatment. Antibiotic, antimycotic therapy and filgastrim was given. Leukocyte number increased, fever stopped after 10 days therapy. Patient returned 17 days later. She has been high fever, weakness, sore throat for 4 days. Ciprofloxacin was given by GP before her registration because of the suspicion of urine infection, then she took sulfamethoxazol + trimethoprim without medical advise. Agranulocytosis was detected again, Staphylococcus sepsis developed. No sign of hematological disease was shown by the examination of bone marrow. Agranulocytosis was considered aftermath of sulfamethoxazol + trimethoprim. Antibiotics, antimycotic and antiviral treatment, and filgastrim were given, sepsis healed, leukocyte number became normal. 274 patients suffered from chronic C hepatitis were treated by standard interferon, and 43 were treated by pegylated interferon. The rapid, significant decrease of the leukocyte number of the patients treated by pegylated interferon was observed in the first 4 weeks of the treatment then it remained stable. Cessation of the treatment or dose-reduction was no necessary due to neutropenia among patients treated by standard interferon, while dose reduction was reasonable in further two cases besides this, treated by pegylated interferon. Authors stress the importance of the exact follow-up of the patients according to the protocol, which renders the early recognition of the side effects, the prevention of the development of the complications, and the early, adequate treatment of them possible. The importance of them, that the pegylated interferon - despite of its sharp side effect spectrum and more distinct suppressive effect on bone marrow - is the most efficacious drug for the treatment of chronic C hepatitis.
