N2  - Although the major focus of treatment of depression in this country has been psychopharmaceutical, there is growing interest in exploring the role of certain nutrients. Omega-3 fats, particularly DHA, seem to play important roles in both the physiologic structure as well as optimal function of neuronal membrane and synthetic pathways of certain neurotransmitters. Patients with lower omega-3 levels are more likely to have depression. Both omega-3 fats, in the form of fish and fish oil supplements, and folate have been shown to have synergistic benefits for the treatment of depression when used in conjunction with standard pharmaceutical antidepressants; fluoxetine is especially studied in this regard. For folic acid, this benefit may be particular true for women, for unknown reasons. Low levels of the B vitamins folate and thiamine have each been associated with depression. Less than 20% of adults and children eat five daily servings of fruits and vegetables, the richest dietary sources of folate. The American diet is also lacking in fiber-rich foods such as whole grains and legumes,32 which are also good sources for thiamine. These dietary patterns raise concern for a large proportion of the population who may be deficient in key nutrients for healthy mood. © 2005 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
