N2  - The Nuss procedure has been widely accepted and performed for the correction of pectus excavatum. However, many surgeons still have concerns about the displacement of pectus bars and postoperative complications. We have modified the Nuss procedure and performed this operation in 103 cases. Our methods are described. Regarding the fixation of the pectus bars, steel wire is used. The wire is introduced behind the 6th or 7th rib using a Duchenne needle and the ribs and the edges of pectus bars are tied with the wire without using a stabilizer. The pectus bars are placed under the serratus muscle to avoid skin irritability due to protrusion of the bars. This method is also effective for its cosmetic results for thin patients in particular. In older children and adults with a wide depression in the chest, two bars are used to achieve better chest elevation and stable fixation. To avoid postoperative infections, intraoperative contamination should be eliminated and appropriate antibiotics are given intravenously for 5 days and orally for the following 5 days. Hemothorax and pneumothorax were rare in uncomplicated cases without using a chest tube. However, chest drainage was performed in patients with two bars and in patients who needed lung dissection due to adhesions. Postoperative pain relief is very important to reduce the oxygen requirement and to promote an early recovery from the operative stress. To accomplish this, a mixture of fentanyl (0.5 mcg/kg/h) and bupivacaine (0.15 mg/kg/h) is infused through an epidural catheter for 5 days.
