N2  - This article reviews the importance of laboratory testing in the diagnosis of depressive disorders and for management during treatment. There is no useful test to establish the presence of a Major Depressive Episode, but medical conditions requiring laboratory testing should be excluded before arriving at a diagnosis. Treatment may lead to physiologic dysfunction warranting monitoring various laboratory functions. The following tests should be considered in establishing diagnosis and to establish baseline levels for comparison in subsequent monitoring: ■ Electrolytes ■ TSH (and free T4 if TSH is abnormal) ■ CBC with platelet count ■ Creatinine and BUN ■ LFTs (AST, alkaline phosphatase) ■ Calcium and phosphate ■ Pregnancy testing (hCG) for selected female patients. Lithium, carbamazepine, and valproic acid levels should be monitored when these medications are used to treat patients with depression.
