N2  - Since the 1940s the causes of death in the United States have changed. Before the advent of antibiotics and advanced technology, people died in their homes during a course of days to weeks. Of the 2.5 million people who die annually in the United States, more than three fourths die in institutions (61% in hospitals and 17% in nursing facilities). The prevalence of chronic illnesses has altered the usual process of dying, which has become so prolonged that it is recognized now as a distinct stage of life, along with childhood, adolescence, and adulthood. Society struggles with the task of providing cost-effective, high-quality compassionate care for the increasing numbers of patients for whom death occurs after months of gradual debilitation from a chronic and incurable illness. When other physicians state, "There is nothing more we can do for you", palliative care is the direction of choice. Patients with advanced disease require special attention and care from the primary care physician as well as a palliative model of care or a palliative care team consisting of experienced medical and clinical personnel. Controlling a patient's symptoms - whether pain, nausea, agitation, depression, or anxiety - is not sufficient to provide comfort while dying. Patients in palliative care programs need to be viewed as whole people as well as members of families, with a broad range of physical, psychosocial, and spiritual needs that may benefit from the outside assistance of an interdisciplinary health care team.
