N2  - There is accumulating evidence suggesting the role of protein phosphorylation in intracellular signal transduction contributing to many neuronal events. Studies have been conducted on the effect of protein phosphorylation in long term potentiation and depression, neuronal kindling as well as learning and memory. The present study was conducted to examine the effects of acute treatment of calcineurin phosphatase inhibiting immunosuppressant drugs, cyclosporine (Cys) and Tacrolimus (Tac) on the Vasopressin V2 receptor stimulated physiological actions like the locomotor activity, and anti-nociception. The locomotor activity of albino mice weighing between 20-35 g were evaluated in an open field locomotor box made of wood and comprising of 16 squares. In the control group of mice (n = 8), it was observed that pretreatment with vehicle alone appreciably altered the V2 receptor agonist, Desmopressin (DDAVP) (5 μg/kg ip) induced reduction in locomotor activity (P < 0.05). In two other groups of animals (n = 8), the mice were pretreated with Cys (2.4 mg/kg ip) or Tac (50 μg/kg ip) and the effect of DDAVP was found to be further potentiated resulting in a decrease in locomotor activity counts from 44 ± 4.09 to 26 ± 4.69 and 21.5 ± 2.48 to 4.1 ± 1.9 respectively (P < 0.01). On the other hand, the effect of the two calcineurin phosphatase inhibitors Tac and Cys, was similar on the anti-nociceptive effect elicited after DDAVP injection in mice. Both the immunosuppressant drugs significantly potentiated the effect of DDAVP on Acetic acid induced writhings in mice i.e. resulting in a decrease in writhings fro 21.9 ± 0.54 to 3.84 ± 1.35 and 4. 5 ± 0.53 respectively (P < 0.01). The results of the present study clearly suggest that these immunophilin binding drugs modulate the effect of the V2 receptor agonist DDAVP by inhibiting the enzyme calcineurin phosphatase. This study points to a new signalling mechanism of the Vasopressin V2 receptor in central nervous system (CNS).
