N2  - In 102 patients who had undergone splenectomy for various hematological disorders front 1982 to 1995 at Sher-i-Kashmir Institute of Medical Sciences, Srinagar, an assessment for morbidity and mortality was made. It was found that the overall morbidity encountered was 18.62%, highest for lymphoma patients i.e. 37.5% while as overall mortality was 6.86%; highest for idiopathic hypersplenism patients (20%). This study included all age groups and an effort was made to come to a conclusion about subsequent infection following splenectomy in a broader perspective. The results indicate that complication rate is very less in carefully selected patients, patients <50 years of age but greater than 17 years, patients with shorter duration of illness, patients with mild to moderate depression in preoperative hematological parameters, patients without any concomitant disease and patients without any severe immunological depression. Besides, good pre- operative build up by blood and blood products supportive measures during surgery, excellent post-operative management by supportive measures and antibiotics also form the pre-requisite for splenectomy to decrease the morbidity and mortality. The study indicates that therapeutic splenectomy still continues to be the mainstay of management for various hematological disorders in order to offer the patients a qualitative life inspite of the risks of infection involved.
