N2  - Objective: The conservative treatment and favorable prognosis of ten patients who presented with acute loss of vision is described. They showed only slight signs of occlusion of the central retinal artery on ophthalmoscopy; i.e. incomplete central retinal artery occlusion (ICRAO). Design: Case series. Methods: Ten patients with ICRAO were treated conservatively with massage of the globe, immediate depression of the blood pressure where hypertension was present, isovolemic hemodilution in the presence of an increased hematocrit reading and the daily administration of 100 mg acetylsalicylic acid. Results: ICRAO has a good visual prognosis, and these patients showed a marked recovery of vision following conservative treatment. Their initial clinical signs included a slight and localized degree of perimacular edema, visible on ophthalmoscopy and showing also a faint cherry-red macular spot. Delay in fluorescein filling of the retinal arteries was found, but in most of the patients the loss of vision was moderate, showing a decrease to between 20/500 and 20/30. Conclusions: Spontaneous recovery of vision is not to be expected in patients with ICRAO. Immediate conservative treatment is necessary, and this must be started at once if a possible increase in the occlusion - with its correspondingly poorer prognosis - is to be avoided. It is important to be aware of the ocular signs of ICRAO, in order to avoid beginning an unnecessarily drastic treatment such as fibrinolysis.
