N2  - Experiments were carried out on male BALB/c (recipients) and C57B1 (donors) mice. The transplantation of ear skin was carried out. Recipient mice were infected 23 days before transplantation with Trichinella spiralis or Trichinella pseudospiralis larvae. It was found that allogenic skin allografts survived longer in infected mice as compared to controls. The suppression of allograft rejection was also observed when normal mice were inoculated 1-6 days after transplantation with extract from Trichinella sp. The effect was abolished when the infected mice were injected with inhibitors of prostaglandin synthesis: indomethacin or acetylsalicylic acid. This suggests that prostaglandins play an important role in the mechanism of depression of the host immune response.
