N2  - There is a preponderance of recent evidence that malignant disease presenting cancer cachexia is heralded by depressive illness of an apparently endogenous kind, not related to gross brain damage. The subjects of this research were patients suffering from gastrointestinal cancers and progressive diseases. The patients were serologically and/or histologically confirmed to have metastasizing and/or recurrent cancers. Serum neopterin, tryptophan, serotonin and cortisol were synchronously measured in patients and healthy controls. Patients with cancers had significantly raised neopterin, a marker of endogenous gamma-interferon (IFN-γ), and cortisol values, whereas those without them had decreased tryptophan and serotonin levels. Much work has been done to elucidate the possible role of serotonin(5-hydroxytryptamine : 5-HT) in depressive illness. IFN-γ induces a high level of indoleamine dioxygenase (IDO), tryptophan degrading enzyme, and cortisol levels induce high tryptophan oxygenase activity, which in turn increases the metabolism along the tryptophan-nicotinic acid pathway. These results suggested that immune system overactivation and intense adrenal activity occurred in patients with advanced cancers, resulting in disorders of the tryptophan metabolism followed by depression in cancer cachexia.
