N2  - Osmoticity and sodium chloride equivalents of solutions of ketorolac tromethamine and isoniazid at various concentrations were determined by freezing point depression method and the osmolality values were confirmed by using OSMOMAT 030 osmometer. The iso-osmotic solutions of these drugs were tested for isotonicity using red blood cells (RBC) hemolysis method developed by Husa et al. The diffusion of ketorolac tromethamine and 'isoniazid' across the RBC membrane has been shown as the cause of hemolysis of human RBC even in the iso-osmotic solutions of these drugs. The addition of amino acids such as glycine, alanine, lysine and aspartic acid decreased the percent hemolysis and this has been attributed to the adsorption of amino acids on the cell membrane.
