N2  - Irreversible and unspecific inhibitors of MAO were the first modern antidepressants, but after an initial success they fell into discredit due to adverse side effects. In the past two decades interest in MAO inhibitors has been renewed because of progress in basic research, a milestone being the finding that there are two subtypes of MAO, MAO-A and MAO-B. These are distinct proteins with high amino acid homology, coded by separate genes both located on the short arm of the human chromosome X. The enzyme subforms show different substrate specificities in vitro and different distributionss within the central nervous system and in peripheral organs. In the central nervous system of man MAO-A seems to be mainly involved in the metabolism of 5 HT and noradrenaline, whereas 2-phenylethylamine and probably dopamine are predominantly deaminated by MAO-B. In the intestinal tract tyramine is mainly metabolized by MAO-A. These characteristics indicate distinct physiological functions of the two MAO-subforms. Several irreversible and reversible non-hydrazine inhibitors with relative selectivities for one of the MAO-subforms have been developed. They belong to vaarious chemical classes with different modes of enzyme inhibition. These range from covalent mechanism based interaction (e.g. by propargyl- and allylaminederivatives) to pseudo-substrate inhibition (e.g. by 2-aminoethyl-carboxamides) and non-covalent interaction (e.g. by brofaromine, toloxatone and possibly moclobemide). The most important pharmacological effects of the new types of MAO inhibitors are those observed in neuropsychiatric disorders. The inhibitors of MAO-A show a favorabled action in various forms of mental depression. The drugs seem to have about the same activity as other types of antidepressants, including tricyclic and related compounds as well as classical MAO inhibitors. The onset of action of the MAO-A inhibitors is claimed to be relatively fast. Other possible indications of these drugs include disorderssd with cognitive impairment, e.g. dementia of the Alzheimer type. In subjects with Parkinson's dsiease the MAO-B inhibitor L-deprenyl exerts a L-dopa-sparing effect, prolongs L-dopa action and seems to have a favorable influence regarding on-off disabilities. The action is in general transitory (months to several years). In addition L-deprenyl has been shown to delay the necessity for L-dopad treatment in patients with early parkinsonism. Whether the drug influences the progression of the disease is still a matter of debate. L-deprenyl also appears to have some antidepressant effect (especially in higher doses) and to exert a beneficial influence in other disorders, e.g. dementia of the Alzheimer type. The role of intermediary metabolites, i.e. amphetamine and metamphetamine, in its action has not yet been fully clarified. The side effects of the new generation of MAO-inhibitorss are relatively mild. They compare favorably with those of the classical MAO-inhibitors and tricyclic and related compounds. As shown in preclinical and clinical studies, the new types of MAO-inhibitors enhance tyramined sensitivity less markedly than the classical ones and stringent dietary restrictions for the prevention of hypertensive crises can in general be avoided in patientssd under treatment with low doses of MAO-A and MAO-B inhibitors. Also, these drugs have virtually no anticholinergic effects and, with some exceptions, can generally be safely combined with a variety of other drugs. Dangerous toxicity to liver or other organs has not been observed up to now. The modes of action of MAO-A and MAO-B inhibitors in human disorders have not yet been fully clarified. Mechanisms discussed are based on selective inhibition of MAo-subforms and consequent changes in the metabolism of monoamine transmitters, leading to a cascade of established or putative events as summarized in Table 9. Also, MAO inhibitorsf possibly exert some pharmacological and clinical actions by interference with trace amines, inhibition of amine uptake, protection against environmental toxins and prevention of the formation of intermediary free radicals.
