N2  - Antibiotics and psychotropic drugs are the most frequent substances which are known to induce side effects. Additional important agents like Ca antagonists, analgesics, thrombocyte aggregation inhibitors and drugs in Parkinson's disease can produce adverse reactions related to the nervous system. Flunarizine is known to induce extrapyramidal symptoms and depression. Mixed type analgesics can cause chronic headache and psychological and physical dependence. Acetylsalicylic acid as an effervescent formulation has the best bioavailability and the lowest rate of adverse reactions thus 500 mg as an effervescent agent should be the first choice of application. MAO-B inhibitor can decrease the progredience of Parkinson's disease and dopamine agonists can significantly reduce the side effects of a long lasting dopamine therapy.
