N2  - Many fire related deaths are caused by the inhalation of smoke. The mechanisms are: (1) thermal injury to the airways and lungs, (2) inhalation of foreign matter and (3) carbon monoxide poisoning. Investigations should include chest radiology, arterial blood gases and CO-oximetry. In specialized centers 133Xenon lung scans aid in assessing the degree of pulmonary injury and fiberoptic bronchoscopy is used to evaluate the degree of thermal damage to the airways. Initial therapy is with humidified oxygen; mechanical ventilation may be indicated for respiratory failure or central nervous system depression. Percussion and postural drainage help to clear particulate material from the lungs and bronchodilators may relieve bronchospasm. Steroids should not be routinely prescribed as they may increase the rate of infection. Antibiotics should be prescribed only for culture proven infections, unless there is strong clinical evidence of pneumonia. Children who have suffered from smoke inhalation do well with supportive care although many will have minor abnormalities of pulmonary infection on long-term follow-up.
