N2  - Dietary omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, especially eicosapentaenoic acid, might be involved in the low incidence of coronary artery disease in individuals consuming diets containing large amounts of these fatty acids, like those of Greenland Eskimos. Eicosapentaenoic acid is known to interfere with blood lipid levels, blood viscosity, red cell deformability and platelet function. To evaluate the effect of dietary supplementation of 3 gram omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids per day on exercise capacity and objective signs of exercise-induced myocardial ischemia we studied five patients with stable angina pectoris and with angiographically proven coronary artery disease. After six months of dietary supplementation, plasma total cholesterol (mmol/L) fell from 7.6 ± 1.6 to 6.5 ± 1.7 (p<0.03) and plasma HDL-cholesterol rose from 0.92 ± 0.12 to 1.02 ± 0.10 (p<0.05). However, exercise duration and maximal ST segment depression remained unchanged during the study period. In this limited series of patients with coronary artery disease, dietary enrichment of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids does not influence the objective manifestations of exercise-induced myocardial ischemia. However, the changes in blood lipid levels are promising and necessitate sequential angiographic studies on the long-term effect of these dietary manipulations.
