N2  - Multiple, nonspecific symptoms of a seemingly unrelated nature, which are often misdiagnosed as psychoneurotic in origin, are now known to be caused by Candida albicans. This illness is widely prevalent but frequently unrecognized. Physical examination and laboratory tests seldom point to a definitive diagnosis. Incorrect diagnoses as psychoneurosis, hypochondriasis, or endogenous depression are common. Fatigue, depression, and adverse reactions to numerous foods (so-called food allergy), chemical fumes (chemical sensitivity), and airborne allergens (typical hay fever) are often present. Gastrointestinal, genitourinary, and musculoskeletal symptoms are common. Etiology is partly iatrogenic and includes the prescribing of antibiotics (often inappropriately), corticosteroid medications and oral contraceptives - in concert with a diet which is high in sugar and which contains suboptimal amounts of essential micronutrients. Effective treatment consists of oral nystatin and avoidance of reactive foods, combined with measures to support the immune system and reduce fungal colonization in body cavities. Clinical recognition of this syndrome and appropriate therapy can often relieve enervating symptoms which previously puzzled and frustrated both physicians and patients. An enormous amount of unnecessary suffering can thus be relieved.
