N2  - Survival of stem cells determines the subsequent performance of the marrow. The ability of surviving stem cells to self-renew and to differentiate depends upon the survival of the haemopoietic microenvironmental stroma. The numbers of circulating blood cells gives no indication of the severity of damage to, and the recovery potential of, haemopoietic tissues following cytotoxic damage. Complete suppression of blood cell production occurs following relatively low acute doses of radiation and peripheral blood counts can sometimes be maintained in the face of severe damage by chemicals and chronic radiation exposure. Similarly, even studying the cellularity of the marrow gives little or no information on the fundamental damage to the marrow. The reappearance of cell production can, however, be seen somewhat earlier by examining marrow rather than peripheral blood. Even though sufficient stem cells survive cytotoxic therapy for ultimate recovery to be adequate for normal production of functioning cells, nevertheless the recovery may occur too late to prevent death from infection and/or haemorrhage due to prolonged lack of granulocytes and platelets. It may be necessary, therefore, to give supportive therapy (transfusions of whole blood, buffy coat cells or platelets, and antibiotics) to keep the patient alive until endogenous cell production is significantly established. Barrier nursing to reduce the external hazards is also desirable. The damage inflicted upon the stem cells by cytotoxic drugs is not the same for every drug. Similar initial measurable damage to stem cells can be followed by quite different events e.g. length of cellular depression, rate of recovery, extent of recovery etc. At present the sequelae of drug administration can only be assessed by studying each drug individually and none can be predicted. Since the CFU-S clonal assay can be performed only in the mouse some other criteria are required for assessing marrow damage in humans. These can be based upon the measurement of other clonogenic assays of some of the earliest progeny of the stem cell e.g. GM-CFC, CFC-Mix etc. Whilst measuring these is less reliable than measuring stem cells themselves, they are the best available criteria and act as useful practical indicators.
