N2  - A number of drugs in common use have been associated with an unusually high incidence of bone marrow depression. Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents are cited particularly often and, of this group, the most toxic are phenylbutazone and its active metabolite oxyphenbutazone. Chloramphenicol is unique among antibiotics in causing a high incidence of aplastic anaemia. Similarly, mianserin is associated with agranulocytosis more commonly than any other antidepressant. The published data on these drugs are reviewed here.
