N2  - This study showed that none of the aminoglycoside antibiotics lacked neuromuscular blocking activity. The neuromuscular blockade produced by all members of this antibiotic group can only be reversed by calcium chloride. It was also shown that the H2-receptor blocking agent cimetidine (3 mmol.l-1) produced a complete neuromuscular blockade in the isolated phrenic nerve-hemidiaphragm preparation of the rat. On the other hand ranitidine (0.5-1.5 mmol.l-1), another H2-receptor blocking agent, did not exhibit any neuromuscular blocking activity. This agent (0.7 mmol.l-1) seems to have an anticholinesterase activity at the neuromuscular junctions. The neuromuscular blockade produced by cimetidine has the same characteristics as that of aminoglycoside antibiotics and differs from that of non-depolarizing muscle relaxant agents in that it is not reversed by anticholinesterase drugs such as neostigmine. This blockade, as in the case of aminoglycoside antibiotics, is only reversed by calcium chloride (1 mmol.l-1). Moreover, aminoglycoside antibiotics and cimetidine are potentially capable of interacting and produced a complete neuromuscular blockade at concentrations significantly reduced (to one tenth or to one twentieth of the individual concentrations). The mechanism by which aminoglycoside antibiotics and cimetidine produce neuromuscular blockade must be the same. All the findings lead to the assumption that both aminoglycoside antibiotics and cimetidine are involved in the process of acetylcholine release by nerve impulses, antagonizing calcium ions. The interaction of aminoglycoside antibiotics and cimetidine is of clinical significance because both classes of drugs are widely used during the perioperative period and may lead to respiratory depression or prolonged apnoea in patients.
