N2  - The inhibitory profiles on respiratory rate, visceral pain (chemically induced abdominal constrictions) and prostaglandin (PG) biosynthesis of the peripherally acting analgesics aspirin, paracetamol and mefenamic acid were compared with those of the opiates dextropropoxyphene and dihydrocodeine. There was a good correlation between analgesia and PG inhibition for the peripherally acting agents. Both mefenamic acid and aspirin also possessed low properties towards depression of respiratory rate and it was concluded that they had most beneficial profiles compared with the other agents.
