N2  - Neonatal septicemia is clinically characterized by respiratory failure, profound arterial hypoxemia, pulmonary hypertension, leukopenia, and shock. In experimental animal studies that closely parallel these clinical findings, the development of hypoxemia during bacterial infusion results from: a bacterial toxin-induced depression of cardiac output with a subsequent decrease in mixed venous PO2; and an increase in V̇(A)/Q heterogeneity in response to pulmonary edema associated with altered endothelial permeability. The precise mediators of these physiologic alterations are unknown, but it is clear that arachidonic acid metabolites, such as prostacyclin, thromboxane A2, and the leukotrienes play an important role in producing pulmonary hypertension and pulmonary edema. The release of other granulocyte by-products, such as activated complement fragments, toxic oxygen radicals, and lysosomal enzymes, may also be important in mediating the sepsis-induced lung injury associated with granulocyte sequestration in the lungs. Although pharmacologic therapy directed at inhibiting these pathways holds promise for the future, current treatment of neonatal sepsis is limited to antibiotics and supportive care.
