N2  - The high number of hazardous chemicals at home is well established but the increasing availability of potentially lethal drugs in the home due to market availability and physician's prescribing habits has not been well studied. We randomly selected 206 campers, ages 5-15, attending a YMCA camp for 'normal' children. Forty-three out of 206 (20.5%) were taking at least one medication as follows: anticonvulsant (1); bronchodilators (5); decongestant (9); antibiotics (2); antituberculosis antibiotic (1); vitamins (5); nonnarcotic analgesics (3); barbiturate analgesic (1); hematinic (1); thyroid (1); ibuprofen (1); psychotropic (8). After careful clinical evaluation and follow-up during camp duration it was determined that only the children on anticonvulsant (1); antituberculosis (1) and one of the asthmatics really needed their medication. The children (8) on psychotropic drugs for hyperactivity, enuresis or depression did not need their medication and they were stopped without any untoward effect during the duration of the camp. Thus, the increasing availability of potentially lethal drugs should alert the physician to be cautious in his/her prescription habits.
