N2  - Prostaglandin E (PGE) has been reported to inhibit markedly glucagon-stimulated lipolysis in the adipose tissue and isolated fat cells of chickens. Experiments were conducted to determine if acetylsalicylic acid (aspirin, an inhibitor of PGE synthesis) would affect abdominal fat deposition in broiler chickens. Levels of 0, 200, or 2000 mg of aspirin per kg of diet were fed to broilers from 17 to 31 days of age in experiment 1; 14 to 28 days in experiment 2; and either 52 to 66 or 60 to 66 days in experiment 3. At the end of the experiments, livers and abdominal fat pads were removed and weighed. Blood samples were obtained in experiments 1 and 2 for determination of glucose and free fatty acids (FFA). Growth rate and liver weight per unit of body weight were not depressed significantly by aspirin in the younger birds, but the 2000 mg level significantly depressed growth rate in the older birds. Abdominal fat as a percent of body weight was not reduced significantly by aspirin in experiments 1 or 2. A reduction was observed with the highest level in experiment 3, but this was associated with a marked growth depression. Neither blood glucose nor FFA were significantly affected by dietary treatments. The results indicate that acetylsalicylic acid is not an effective agent to reduce fat deposition in broiler chickens.
