N2  - It is known that aminoglycoside antibiotics can produce the neuromuscular blockade which is mainly based on their antireleasing action of acetylcholine (Ach) at neuromuscular junctions. The neuromuscular blocking actions of kanamycin sulfate (KM) were firstly reported in vivo by Timmerman et al. and its presynaptic blocking action was suggested by Singh et al. Furthermore, pre- and post-junctional blocking actions of KM were clearly showed in vitro, and it was confirmed that KM can act preferentially at the presynaptic site and that the antireleasing action of KM can be completely antagonized by 3,4-diaminopyridine in vitro. 3,4-Diaminopyridine can reverse the neuromuscular depression by amikacin, polymyxin B, lincomycin and clindamycin in vitro and 4-aminopyridine (4-AP) can reverse lincomycin-pancuronium-induced neuromuscular blockade in man. KM has not only the neuromuscular blocking action but also the cardiovascular depressive action, as have the other aminoglycoside antibiotics. The aim of this study was to examine whether, as in vitro experiments of neuromuscular system, 4-AP could antagonize the neuromuscular and cardiovascular depressive actions of KM in anesthetized rats.
