N2  - Glucocorticoids administered in pharmacological doses to mice at midgestation results in cleft palate (CP). Inbred strains exhibit different susceptibilities to glucocorticoid-induced CP with A/J being highly susceptible and C57 BL/6J (C57) more resistant. Elevated levels of palatal epithelial cAMP during midgestation have been implicated as playing a role in palatal medial edge epithelial (MEE) differentiation. The objectives of this study were to measure cAMP levels as well as examine the localization and distribution of cAMP in the developing A/J and C57 secondary palate after maternal administration of a teratogenic dose of cortisone acetate. Levels of palatal intracellular cAMP were analyzed on each of Days 13-15 by radioimmunoassay. A transient rise (three- to four-fold) of palatal cAMP levels were seen with maximal values occurring during the early phase of MEE cell death (early Day 14). Maternal cortisone treatment resulted in a dramatic depression of both A/J and C57 palatal cAMP on Day 14 of gestation. Arachidonic acid, previously shown to protect against cortisone-induced CP, administered simultaneously with cortisone, failed to reverse the cortisone-induced depression of palatal cAMP. Developing palatal shelves in organ culture undergo histological and biochemical developmental changes identical to those seen in vivo. Moreover, changes in cAMP levels in the culture media corresponded to those found in devloping palates in vivo. A transient peak of cAMP was seen after 18 hr in vitro. This time corresponds to the period of peak levels of cAMP in vivo (early Day 14). Addition of 1 x 10-6 M cortisone acetate or dexamethasone to the culture medium depressed levels of tissue cAMP in both stains at all time points studied and resulted in an elimination of the transient peak of cAMP in media from organ cultured palates. Localization and distribution of cAMP were visualized by immunohistochemistry in both A/J and C57 fetal palates on early Day 14 of gestation. Staining for cAMP was most intense in palatal epithelial cytoplasm. Mesenchymal staining for cAMP was faint and restricted to mesenchymal plasmalemmas. Maternal cortisone treatment resulted in a dramatic reduction of palatal epithelial staining intensity in both strains of mice. These data demonstrate that maternal administration of teratogenic doses of cortisone results in a depression of fetal palatal epithelial cAMP levels and may function to alter MEE differentiation. Moreover differential susceptibility to cortisone-induced CP in AJ and C57 does not appear to involve an effect on palatal cAMP levels as cAMP levels were equally inhibited in both strains.
