N2  - The resistance arteries supplying individual exchange villi of the full-term human fetal placenta were examined for their reactivity to various prostaglandins (PG) as well as for their ability to synthesize biologically active PG. PGA1, PGF(2α), PGE2 and PGE1 produced dose-dependent contractions between 10-7 and 10-5M. The order of potency observed was PGA1 ≃ PGF(2α)>PGE2>PGE1. TXB2 was without activity in this preparation. Prostacyclin (PGI2) produced a dose-dependent relaxation of pre-contracted strips between 10-8M and 10-5M. Arachidonic acid (AA) produced stable dose-dependent contractions (10-5 M to 10-3M) which were totally abolished by pretreatment with 10-7M meclofenamate (MF). At no concentration of AA was any evidence of vascular relaxation observed. Larger concentrations of MF (>10-6M) resulted in a non specific depression of the placental vascular smooth muscle. Meclofenamate (10-7M) pretreatment of strips subjected to dose-response studies using PGF (2α), PGE2, bradykinin (BK) and angiotensin II(AII) revealed a significant reduction in tension developed to both BK and AII. This finding suggests that the vasoactive peptides BK and AII stimulate the synthesis of vasoconstricting PG in the fetal placental resistance arteries which relax in response to PGI2 and contract in response to the other PG tested.
