N2  - Infection is the major cause of death in the burn patient. The burned skin is an ideal culture media for bacteria. This added to the severe immune depression seen after burn injury produces a patient very prone to infection. Although endogenous bacteria readily colonize the wound, life threatening infections are frequently caused by exogenous hospital born organisms resistent to standard antibiotics. These organisms are not only dangerous to the individual patient but can also infect other neighboring patients by cross-contamination. It is difficult to determine how significant infection by airborn transmission of these organisms, is as compared to contact spread by personnel. Laminar airflow techniques have been shown to significantly decrease infection rate in other infection prone patients which indicates airborn transmission to be an important source of infection. As a result of a laminar airflow system, particles and bacteria in the air are rapidly removed producing a sterile air environment. The University of Wisconsin Burn Center constructed a four bed horizontal laminar airflow isolation unit in 1974 to be used exclusively for the care of the burn patients in order to decrease infection rate. Recently the burn center moved to a new university hospital where two isolation units with a total of seven beds, is being used. The rate of laminar air flow in the system can be adjusted between 60 and 90 feet per minute depending on activity. The air filters then remove 99.85 per cent of particles more than 0.3μ. The patient area in each unit measures 20x20 feet with no physical barrier between beds. This allows room for ambulation, therapy and burn care. The nurse's station, kitchen and storage are in an area outside of the laminar airflow system. The authors reviewed their experience over a three year period (1975-1978) to determine the effectiveness of the system in decreasing infection and also to identifying any problems encountered with the system.
