N2  - 1. Symbiosis is a common principle of life, a co-operation with mutual support. 2. The micro-organisms living on the skin and in the mucous membranes of the human are named his 'flora'. In the ideal case of a healthy person there exists a real symbiosis between the human organism and his physiological flora, which has (according to Haenel) the following components in the human intestine: a) Main flora: obligatory anaerobic germs, mainly bifido bacteria. b) Accompanying flora: anaerobic germs, coli group, enterococci and lactobacilli. c) Residual flora: different groups of germs: proteus, yeast, clostridium staphylococcus, aerobic spore forming organisms. 3. The normal relationship between organism and bacterial flora can be disturbed by endogenous or exogenous factors. Under certain circumstances such a disorder to symbiosis can be found bacteriologically. Then one speaks of a dysbiosis (according to Haenel) which represents significant deviations from the eubiotic standard and causes different proportion varieties of the individual germ groups in the feces. Usually the bidifus group is reduced by coliforms, enterococci and lactobacilli, increasing proportions of the residual flora. 4. The diagnosis of a symbiosis disorder can already be made by an anamnesis: bad habits or bad nutrition, environmental burdening and drug misuse: antibiotics, antiseptics and laxative misuse are more and more causing functional failure of the defense and metabolic performance. The depression of the immunological system is the most important result. 5. The therapy of symbiosis disorders aims at restoring and preserving the normal flora by application of physiological bacterial cultures in a probiotically prepared milieu. 6. Symbiosis guidance is a basic therapy which can be performed with quite different but of course probiotic, biologically orientated forms of therapy. In case of absolutely necessary antibiotic and chemotherapeutic measures in connection with acute medicine the symbiosis restoration counteracts a negative effect on the immunological system.
