N2  - The effects of exogenous hyaluronic acid on cell cultures of chick embryo limb chondrocytes are reported in this paper. The evidence shows that exogenous hyaluronic acid (HA) can both depress the incorporation of 35SO(4) into glycosaminoglycans and cause a displacement of newly synthesized proteoglycan from the cell layer to the culture medium. The results demonstrate that these two effects are mediated by distinct mechanisms. The displacement effect has a rapid onset (by 2 hr) while the effect of exogenous HA on 35SO(4) incorporation has a long latency (12 hr). The displacement effect is produced by a lower concentration (5 μg/ml) of hyaluronate oligomers than the effect on 35SO(4) incorporation (50 μg/ml). In addition, displacement is produced only by hyaluronate oligomers that are decasaccharides or larger. The depression of 35SO(4) incorporation is produced by tetrasaccharides as well as high molecular weight HA. In fact tetrasaccharides can depress 35SO(4) incorporation without causing this displacement effect.
