N2  - In the postoperative care of neurosurgery patients a thorough understanding must be gained of the special features of neurosurgery, especially carniotomy, and pathophysiology of the brain, especially the special characteristics of brain circulation and the pathology of brain edema. It must be noted that the physiology of normal brain circulation especially autoregulation cannot be directly applied to the brain after craniotomy and that hypoxia, post hypoxic state, and luxury perfusion exist as the possible factors by which autoregulation is lost. As for the mechanism of blood-brain barrier, the existence of tight junction in the capillary endothelium of the brain and the function of astroglia have been rather extensively elucidated not only anatomically but also physiologically. In view of the function of the blood-brain barrier, it is necessary to administer fluids and antibiotics. Description is made of recent studies on teh protective action of barbiturate against hypoxia of the brain to control the unbalance at onset of brain edema when there is a depression of energy production despite the increased consumption of oxygen by the brain. In analyzing the mechanism of the prophylactic effect of barbiturate against brain edema and the brain protective action of barbiturates inhibition of metabolism, decrease in capillary pressure of the brain, elevation of osmotic pressure of the brain, and correction of osmotic pressure were observed.
