N2  - The effects of 3-day pretreatment of cats with the synthetic glucocorticoid, dexamethasone, was examined in vitro using isolated cat liver preparations perfused with a blood-free medium at a constant flow rate. In both the vehicle and steroid-treated cats, hypoxia did not have an effect on perfusion pressure or shunt flow. However, cellular integrity in the vehicle-treated livers was severely compromised, as reflected by increases in perfusate lactic acid dehydrogenase, amino nitrogen and cathepsin D activities, and increases in tissue lysosomal fragility, and enlargement and vacuolization of hepatocyte lysosomes. However, 3 days of dexamethasone treatment altered the in vitro response of the liver to hypoxia by effectively preventing the hypoxia-induced hepatic changes. Furthermore, steroid pretreatment did not have an inhibitory effect on in vitro phagocytic activity of the liver, but did prevent the hypoxic-induced depression of the hepatic reticuloendothelial system. These results indicate that glucocorticoids exert a lysosomal stabilization and suggest that glucocorticoids can induce prolonged in vitro stabilization of cellular membranes in response to the challenge of hypoxic stress.
