N2  - This article is a concise summary of major contributions related to the depressed immune defenses that follow burn trauma, mechanical injury and operation. The important practical effect of this reduction is increased susceptibility to infection. The methods by which the depression can be assessed are described; they include measurements of phagocytic function, humoral and cell-mediated immunity. Immune function can be decreased by other factors such as age, nutrition, previous disease, radiation therapy, steroids, chemotherapy, antibiotics and anesthesia. The effects of burns, trauma or operation on humoral immunity have been studied in less detail than the effects on cell-mediated immunity or on phagocytic activity. The author, after his brief but comprehensive summary of clinical and experimental observations, emphasizes the difficulty of accurate comparison of clinical patients and the consequent need for further experimental studies. (Welch - Boston, Mass.)
