N2  - According to experience in occupational medicine, prolonged exposure to small quantities of lead can lead to nervous symptoms: headache, tiredness, vague gastrointestinal disorders, depression and, as a result of these, misuse of medication. The author's own findings have shown that such disorders occur much more frequently in those living near a main road than in those living in areas free from motor traffic. When such patients were treated with calcium EDTA (to chelate the lead) the symptoms disappeared. At the same time the levels of delta aminolevulinic acid became normal and increased quantities of lead were excreted in the urine. Over a period of observation of 12 yr, 11% of those living near roads died of cancer, while only 1.2% of those living away from the influence of road traffic died from this cause. Almost without exception, these figures concern patients who had previously received no treatment with calcium EDTA. This, together with many reports in the literature, leads to the assumption that the lead in auto fuel influences the occurrence of malignant disease.
