N2  - (+)-Octanoylcarnitine chloride, which has been proved to be antiketogenic experimentally, was investigated from a viewpoint of side effects employing several pharmacological experimental methods in vitro and in vivo. The results are summarized as follows. (+)-Octanoylcarnitine chloride had no effect on the central nervous system. It had no effect on the respiratory movement. It showed a slight ganglion blocking action like hexamethonium and had a vasodilating effect with increase of peripheral blood flow and depression of blood pressure at the large doses. It had a tendency to decrease the heart rate and had no effect on the ECG. It had a very slight atropine-like action and caused a depression of gastrointestinal movements. No influence on bile flow or gastric acid secretion was noted. It slightly inhibited the contractile movement of skeletal muscle at the large doses. It had less effect on the urination. It reduced the PSP excretion at a large daily dose for consecutive 14 days. However, the effect was reversible and of no influence on the urine concentrating capacity.
