N2  - The present paper deals with findings involving the chronic administration of aspirin for 21 days to healthy volunteers in a daily dose of 60 mg/kg body weight, and a study aimed at investigating a possible pharmacokinetic interaction between aspirin and diclophenac sodium. Steady state salicylic acid plasma levels were reached by day 7 in all the trial subjects receiving aspirin only. These levels had decreased by 50% on day 21 in 5 out of 6 subjects. An analysis of the renal elimination of salicylic acid and its main metabolites following loading doses of aspirin revealed that the subjects in whom a decrease in steady state salicylic acid plasma levels occurred had a more rapid renal clearance rate of total salicylate after chronic ingestion of aspirin when compared with the pretreatment values. The subject who did not exhibit a decrease of steady state salicylic acid plasma levels showed no change in his renal clearance of total salicylate. Pretreatment with diclophenac sodium (1 to 2 weeks) also appeared to enhance the renal elimination of total salicylate. On the other hand, concomitant oral administration of aspirin and diclophenac sodium resulted in significant acute depression of the 'area under curve' of diclophenac serum profiles. The clinical significance of these findings are speculative at present but they seem to indicate that combination therapy with the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents is fraught with pharmacokinetic interaction possibilities which may have a deleterious effect upon the pharmacodynamics of one or all of the agents being employed concomitantly. Furthermore, the finding that chronic ingestion of aspirin gives rise to auto-induction of elimination tallies with observations of unexpectedly low steady state salicylic acid plasma levels in patients receiving doses of aspirin in the order of 60 mg/kg daily.
