N2  - Metabolites of warfarin are excreted in the bile of rats; 1 hr after the i.v. injection of 14C-warfarin (1 mg/kg) in male Sprague-Dawley rats, radioactivity was excreted in the bile at a concentration of about 19 nmoles/ml and at a rate of 0.53 nmoles/min. When BSP was injected 60 min after the 14C-warfarin, there was a decrease in the excretion of radioactivity. The greatest depression occurred within 15-20 min when the concentration of radioactivity in the bile and the rate of excretion was reduced to about one-third the preinjection level. As the BSP was eliminated from the plasma the excretion of radioactivity returned to normal. The injection of Rifamycin 60 min after warfarin also reduced the rate of excretion of warfarin to about one-third the pre-injection rate but it did not return to normal within an hr at which time the experiment was terminated. Antibiotics, as well as drugs from other classes, were tested for potential interactions affecting the excretion of warfarin.
