N2  - Considering immuno-depression due to drug toxicity we have to mind the whole range of nitrogen mustards (e.g. cyclophosphamide), anti-metabolites (e.g. methotrexate) and antibiotics (e.g. actinomycin D). All these interfere with DNA formation and inhibit cellular proliferation in the bone marrow, giving rise to the well-known clinical symptoms. The toxic agents discussed in this paper belong to a different category of compounds widely and chronically used clinically,. viz. those that possess membrane tropism because of their liposolubility in the double-lipoid layer of the cellular membrane which have a low acute toxicity. As described by Seeman (1973), this category includes the groups most frequently used in treatment: general anesthetics (volatile), anti-arrhythmic agents (procainamide), anti-hypertensive agents (hydralazine), anti-rheumatic agents (salicylic acid and phenylbutazone), antihistamines (pyribenzamine), hypnotic agents (barbiturates, slow-acting pentobarbital), anticonvulsive agents (phenylhydantoin) and psycho-tropic drugs (chlorpromazine, benzodiazepine, imipramine and the cannabinoids). These substances may be used daily for years without causing clinical signs of immuno-incompetence, although some of them may cause autoimmune manifestations. Drugs implicated in autoimmune disease are: hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid, sulphonamides, diphenykhydantoin, chlorpromazine, trimethadione, α-methyldopa and nitrofurantoin.
