N2  - Treatment of acute respiratory insufficiency due to poisoning by hypnotic drugs must primarily be directed towards reversing the alveolar hypoventilation arising from the depression of the respiratory centres. This can only be achieved by artificial respiration. Administration of analeptic drugs is obsolete. As there is no primary bronchopulmonary damage, a respiratory rate of 15 p. minute and a minute volume of 8-10 litres should generally be adequate; but the impairment of the cough and other respiratory reflexes caused by the central nervous depression may lead to retention of secretion and atelectasis. Toxic damage to the respiratory epithelium may also prevent elimination of secretion. Treatment of these complications is by intensive physiotherapy, bronchial toilet, repeated inflation of the lungs and aerosol administration. Aspiration pneumonia, a frequent complication caused by the absence of respiratory reflexes, is treated by early bronchial lavage and administration of steroids and antibiotics. Poisoning with non barbiturates or with a combination of hypnotic agents may present special problems, e.g. bromcarbamide, glutethimide and methyprylone may cause sudden apnoea without a premonitory stage of drowsiness, confusion and loss of consciousness; methaqualone, the tricyclic anti depressive drugs and pyrazolone derivatives are liable to produce convulsions which may necessitate curarization and artificial respiration. For preventing pulmonary oedema which is a frequent occurrence in poisoning with bromcarbamide and glutethimide haemodialysis or haemoperfusion are preferable to forced diuresis. In cases of severe bromcarbamide poisoning the fluid balance and clotting mechanism require close attention. Once the 'bromcarbamide lung' has become established therapeutic attempts are unlikely to succeed. Some recently introduced detoxication techniques for treating respiratory insufficiency caused by hypnotic poisoning are reviewed, especially haemoperfusion. They effect rapid elimination of the poisonous agent, thereby reducing the risk of pulmonary damage accompanying prolonged artificial respiration.
