N2  - Of all head injuries, 42% are estimated to be due to traffic accidents. In a general review of the pathology, clinical course and management, the author points out that injury to the brain is most commonly the result of a collision between brain and skull. In more severe injury the brain may be lacerated, especially against sharp internal bone edges or the free tentorial edge. Immediate transfer to a hospital is obviously necessary in cases of impression fracture or intradural hemorrhage; however, there may be doubts with slighter injuries. The doctor's task is in most instances that of an observer. After the patient has regained consciousness, he should make a neurological examination to establish the base-line situation, paying special attention to presence or absence of weakness, sensory loss, alteration in the reflexes, defects in visual fields, disturbances of speech, and abnormalities in the pupils and ocular movements. After initial diagnosis and treatment the doctor must look for other injuries, such as fractures of the cervical spine and internal injuries. Low blood pressure with a rapid pulse point toward the latter; slowing of the pulse and rising blood pressure, toward increasing intracranial pressure. Supportive measures may include antibiotics in unconscious patients threatened with pneumonia. Meperidine and chlorpromazine can be used in early stages to treat pain and restlessness symptomatically. Morphine is better avoided due to respiratory depression and interference with neurological assessment. Parenteral diazepam can serve well to cut short epileptic manifestations. Osmotic agents or cortisone should be used to combat brain swelling only if intracranial hemorrhage has been excluded. The patient should be kept in bed until symptoms such as giddiness and headache are subsiding, after which time, if his condition is otherwise satisfactory, he may be gradually mobilized. Sometimes recovery is slowed by complications. The most urgent is epidural hemorrhage; acute subdural and intracerebral hemorrhage may present less rapidly causing deterioration in the neurological status within the first few days. They may be confirmed by computer tomography or angiography. Still later persistence of neurological symptoms or headache may be due to a chronic subdural hematoma, which may be diagnosed by head scanning.
